import{n as e,r as t,t as n}from"./helpers-ClX0bpsp.js";var r={building:`blueprint-hall`,overview:[n(`Some design questions come up before any service is chosen: how the application is divided (one deployable unit or many small services), where its state lives (in the servers or outside them), and how its parts are layered. This building covers microservice design principles, stateless versus stateful workloads, and multi-tier architectures.`,`msa-whitepaper`,[`Adopting microservices offers benefits, such as improved scalability, resilience, flexibility, and faster development cycles.`]),n(`AWS itself says the choice is not automatic: monolithic architecture or alternative approaches may be more appropriate in some cases, and the decision should be made case by case.`,`msa-whitepaper`,[`Monolithic architecture or alternative approaches may be more appropriate in some cases.`,`Deciding between microservices or monoliths should be made on a case-by-case basis, considering factors like scale, complexity, and specific use cases.`])],bullets:[{id:`2.1-K4`,concepts:[n(`Microservices rely on small, loosely coupled services that communicate through well-defined APIs and are managed by autonomous teams. They combine ideas from agile development, service-oriented architectures, API-first design and CI/CD.`,`msa-whitepaper`,[`This method relies on small, loosely coupled services that communicate through well-defined APIs, which are managed by autonomous teams.`,`Microservices architectures combine successful and proven concepts from various fields, such as: Agile software development Service-oriented architectures API-first design Continuous Integration/Continuous Delivery (CI/CD)`]),n(`A microservices architecture separates functionality into cohesive verticals according to specific domains, rather than technological layers. A layered monolith, by contrast, splits an application into a presentation layer, an application layer and a data layer.`,`msa-simple`,[`Typical monolithic applications consist of different layers: a presentation layer, an application layer, and a data layer.`,`Microservices architectures, on the other hand, separate functionalities into cohesive verticals according to specific domains, rather than technological layers.`]),n(`Each microservice maintains its own data instead of sharing one database. That promotes independence and decentralization, but brings the trade-off between consistency and performance: it is often more practical to accept slight delays in data updates (eventual consistency) than to insist on instant updates.`,`msa-distributed-data`,[`In contrast, each component of a microservices-based application maintains its own data, promoting independence and decentralization.`,`It's often more practical to accept slight delays in data updates (eventual consistency) than to insist on instant updates (immediate consistency).`]),n(`A {{term:stateless}} workload does not store session information, which means any available compute resource can serve any request. A stateful workload keeps data between requests in the server's own disk or memory, so requests depend on which server they reach. Stateless is the design AWS recommends where possible.`,`waf-stateless`,[`Systems should either not require state, or should offload state such that between different client requests, there is no dependence on locally stored data on disk and in memory.`,`In addition to server replacement, another benefit of stateless applications is that they can scale horizontally because any of the available compute resources (such as EC2 instances and AWS Lambda functions) can service any request.`]),n(`To make an application stateless, offload the {{term:session}} data. A stateless architecture decouples user data and offloads the session data. Microservices guidance names in-memory caches (Memcached or Redis, offered as Amazon ElastiCache) as popular stores for session data.`,[`waf-stateless`,`msa-data-store`],[`waf-stateless|A stateless architecture decouples user data and offloads the session data.`,`msa-data-store|Popular stores for session data are in-memory caches such as Memcached or Redis.`]),n(`Once a workload is stateless you can use serverless compute such as AWS Lambda or AWS Fargate, and it is also easier to replace servers.`,`waf-stateless`,[`Once designed to be stateless, you can then use serverless compute services, such as AWS Lambda or AWS Fargate.`])],services:[`Amazon ElastiCache`,`AWS Lambda`,`AWS Fargate`,`Amazon DynamoDB`],design:[n(`For individual microservices, AWS notes that each is designed to do one thing well and so typically has a simplified data model that might be well suited to NoSQL persistence (for example Amazon DynamoDB), though NoSQL has different access patterns, such as no table joins.`,`msa-data-store`,[`Because individual microservices are designed to do one thing well, they typically have a simplified data model that might be well suited to NoSQL persistence.`,`For example, it's not possible to join tables.`]),n(`Stateless components that store state in an external data store lose no state when an instance is replaced, which suits capacity that can be interrupted such as EC2 Spot Instances, which AWS describes as a cost-effective choice if your applications can be interrupted. (Spot is explained in the Treasury notes.)`,[`waf-stateless`,`ec2-spot`],[`waf-stateless|A stateless architecture decouples user data and offloads the session data.`,`ec2-spot|Spot Instances are a cost-effective choice if you can be flexible about when your applications run and if your applications can be interrupted.`])]},{id:`2.1-K10`,concepts:[n(`Typical monolithic applications consist of a presentation layer, an application layer and a data layer, a {{term:three-tier|layered (multi-tier)}} shape. Static web content can be served from Amazon S3 through Amazon CloudFront, and a single-page application can call backend APIs.`,[`msa-simple`,`msa-ui`],[`msa-simple|Typical monolithic applications consist of different layers: a presentation layer, an application layer, and a data layer.`,`msa-ui|Static web content can be served using Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) and Amazon CloudFront.`,`msa-ui|Modern web applications often use JavaScript frameworks to develop single-page applications that communicate with backend APIs.`]),n(`The data tier persists data. In-memory caches such as ElastiCache sit between the application servers and the database, reducing the read load on the database, which may let it support more writes; caches can also improve latency.`,`msa-data-store`,[`Putting a cache between application servers and a database is a common mechanism for reducing the read load on the database, which, in turn, may allow resources to be used to support more writes.`,`Caches can also improve latency.`]),n(`Relational databases (Amazon RDS and Amazon Aurora) remain popular for structured data and business objects, but are not designed for endless scale. NoSQL databases favour scalability, performance and availability over the consistency of relational databases.`,`msa-data-store`,[`Relational databases are still very popular to store structured data and business objects.`,`Relational databases, however, are not designed for endless scale, which can make it difficult and time intensive to apply techniques to support a high number of queries.`,`NoSQL databases have been designed to favor scalability, performance, and availability over the consistency of relational databases.`])],services:[`Amazon CloudFront`,`Amazon S3`,`Amazon ElastiCache`,`Amazon RDS`,`Amazon DynamoDB`],design:[n(`Each layer can then scale independently: put a load balancer in front of a fleet at the application layer (see Scaling Floodgate) and a cache between it and the database. How the layers are placed in subnets is covered in Surveyor's Grid and Gatehouse.`,[`msa-data-store`,`elb-what-is`,`waf-stateless`],[`msa-data-store|Putting a cache between application servers and a database is a common mechanism for reducing the read load on the database`,`elb-what-is|Elastic Load Balancing automatically distributes your incoming traffic across multiple targets, such as EC2 instances, containers, and IP addresses, in one or more Availability Zones.`,`waf-stateless|This provides the flexibility to scale each component independently to meet varying workload demands and optimize resource utilization.`])]},{id:`2.1-S1`,concepts:[n(`Event-driven, microservice and multi-tier are patterns to combine, not exclusive choices. The Microservices on AWS whitepaper describes three microservices patterns: API driven, event driven and data streaming.`,`msa-whitepaper`,[`This whitepaper explores three popular microservices patterns: API driven, event driven, and data streaming.`],{allow:[`three`]}),n(`Serverless is characterised by no infrastructure to provision or manage, automatic scaling by unit of consumption, a "pay for value" billing model, built-in availability and fault tolerance, and event-driven architecture.`,`msa-whitepaper`,[`Serverless is characterized by the following principles: No infrastructure to provision or manage Automatically scaling by unit of consumption "Pay for value" billing model Built-in availability and fault tolerance Event Driven Architecture (EDA)`]),n(`AWS describes microservices as a streamlined approach to software development that accelerates deployment, encourages innovation, enhances maintainability and boosts scalability.`,`msa-whitepaper`,[`Microservices offer a streamlined approach to software development that accelerates deployment, encourages innovation, enhances maintainability, and boosts scalability.`])],services:[`Amazon EventBridge`,`Amazon SQS`,`AWS Lambda`,`Amazon API Gateway`],design:[n(`Matching a requirement to a pattern: services owned and deployed by autonomous teams point to microservices; several decoupled services reacting to something that happened point to event-driven (see Message Quay); a conventional application with distinct presentation, application and data layers fits a multi-tier layout.`,[`msa-whitepaper`,`eda-overview`,`msa-simple`],[`msa-whitepaper|This method relies on small, loosely coupled services that communicate through well-defined APIs, which are managed by autonomous teams.`,`eda-overview|An event-driven architecture uses events to trigger and communicate between decoupled services and is common in modern applications built with microservices.`,`msa-simple|Typical monolithic applications consist of different layers: a presentation layer, an application layer, and a data layer.`]),n(`Whichever pattern you choose, keep the components decoupled through an intermediate layer and keep the compute stateless; those decisions are what make the rest scale and survive failures.`,[`waf-loose-coupling`,`waf-stateless`],[`waf-loose-coupling|Implementing loose coupling between dependencies isolates a failure in one from impacting another.`,`waf-stateless|there is no dependence on locally stored data on disk and in memory.`])]}],cues:[e(`users lose their session/shopping cart when an instance is replaced or scaled in`,`Make the application stateless: offload session data to a shared store, such as an in-memory cache (ElastiCache) or a key-value table (DynamoDB, see Purpose-Built Workshop)`,n(`A stateless architecture decouples user data and offloads the session data; in-memory caches are popular stores for session data, and AWS also suggests DynamoDB for session stores.`,[`waf-stateless`,`msa-data-store`,`database-decision`],[`waf-stateless|A stateless architecture decouples user data and offloads the session data.`,`msa-data-store|Popular stores for session data are in-memory caches such as Memcached or Redis.`,`database-decision|Amazon DynamoDB Key-value Use for workloads such as session stores or shopping carts.`])),e(`scale horizontally / any server can handle any request`,`Stateless design`,n(`Stateless applications can scale horizontally because any available compute resource can service any request.`,`waf-stateless`,[`they can scale horizontally because any of the available compute resources (such as EC2 instances and AWS Lambda functions) can service any request.`])),e(`each team owns and deploys its own service / independent deployment`,`Microservices`,n(`Microservices are small, loosely coupled services with well-defined APIs managed by autonomous teams.`,`msa-whitepaper`,[`This method relies on small, loosely coupled services that communicate through well-defined APIs, which are managed by autonomous teams.`])),e(`services should not share one database`,`Database per microservice (decentralised data)`,n(`Each component of a microservices-based application maintains its own data.`,`msa-distributed-data`,[`each component of a microservices-based application maintains its own data, promoting independence and decentralization.`])),e(`reduce read load on the database / improve latency for repeated reads`,`Add a cache layer (ElastiCache) between the app and the database`,n(`A cache between application servers and a database reduces the database read load and can improve latency.`,`msa-data-store`,[`Putting a cache between application servers and a database is a common mechanism for reducing the read load on the database, which, in turn, may allow resources to be used to support more writes.`]))],examples:[],confuse:[]},i={building:`cache-edge-breakwater`,overview:[n(`A cache keeps a copy of data closer to the consumer or in faster storage, so repeated requests are answered without going back to the source. On AWS the same idea appears at several layers: at the edge (CloudFront), in front of an API (API Gateway cache), between the application and the database (ElastiCache) and in front of DynamoDB (DAX).`,[`cloudfront-what-is`,`apigw-caching`,`msa-data-store`,`dax`,`dax-concepts`],[`cloudfront-what-is|copies of your files (also known as objects) are now held (or cached) in multiple edge locations around the world.`,`apigw-caching|With caching, you can reduce the number of calls made to your endpoint and also improve the latency of requests to your API.`,`msa-data-store|Putting a cache between application servers and a database is a common mechanism for reducing the read load on the database, which, in turn, may allow resources to be used to support more writes.`,`dax|DAX is a DynamoDB-compatible caching service that enables you to benefit from fast in-memory performance for demanding applications.`,`dax-concepts|At runtime, the DAX client directs all of your application's DynamoDB API requests to the DAX cluster.`])],beyondProject:[n(`You have already put CloudFront in front of a static site. The SAA question is which layer a cache belongs in: edge caching for static and dynamic web content close to users, an in-memory cache such as ElastiCache for repeated database reads, DAX for DynamoDB, and the API Gateway stage cache for API responses. It also asks you to tell CloudFront from AWS Global Accelerator, which improves performance over TCP or UDP by proxying packets at the edge to applications in AWS Regions.`,[`cloudfront-what-is`,`ga-what-is`,`ga-faq`],[`cloudfront-what-is|Amazon CloudFront is a web service that speeds up distribution of your static and dynamic web content, such as .html, .css, .js, and image files, to your users.`,`ga-faq|Global Accelerator improves performance for a wide range of applications over TCP or UDP by proxying packets at the edge to applications running in one or more AWS Regions.`,`ga-what-is|AWS Global Accelerator is a service in which you create accelerators to improve the performance of your applications for local and global users.`])],bullets:[{id:`2.1-K3`,concepts:[n(`Lazy loading loads data into the cache only when necessary. Its advantages are that only requested data is cached and node failures are not fatal for the application. Its disadvantages are a cache-miss penalty (three trips: the cache request, the database query and the cache write) and stale data.`,`elasticache-strategies`,[`Lazy loading As the name implies, lazy loading is a caching strategy that loads data into the cache only when necessary.`,`The advantages of lazy loading are as follows: Only requested data is cached.`,`Node failures aren't fatal for your application.`,`The disadvantages of lazy loading are as follows: There is a cache miss penalty.`,`Each cache miss results in three trips: Initial request for data from the cacheQuery of the database for the dataWriting the data to the cache`,`If data is written to the cache only when there is a cache miss, data in the cache can become stale.`]),n(`Write-through adds or updates data in the cache whenever data is written to the database, so data in the cache is never stale. Its disadvantage is missing data: if you spin up a new node, data is missing until it is added or updated on the database. Adding a time to live (TTL) value to cached items minimises wasted space.`,`elasticache-strategies`,[`The advantages of write-through are as follows: Data in the cache is never stale.`,`The disadvantages of write-through are as follows: Missing data.`,`If you spin up a new node, whether due to a node failure or scaling out, there is missing data.`,`This data continues to be missing until it's added or updated on the database.`,`By adding a time to live (TTL) value, you can minimize wasted space.`]),n(`Amazon ElastiCache is a web service for setting up, managing and scaling a distributed in-memory data store or cache in the cloud. It is fully managed, handling hardware provisioning, monitoring, node replacement and software patching, and works with the Valkey, Memcached and Redis OSS engines.`,`elasticache-what-is`,[`Amazon ElastiCache is a web service that makes it easy to set up, manage, and scale a distributed in-memory data store or cache environment in the cloud.`,`Since ElastiCache is a fully-managed service, it automatically manages hardware provisioning, monitoring, node replacements, and software patching for your cluster.`,`NoteAmazon ElastiCache works with the Valkey, Memcached, and Redis OSS engines.`],{newer:[`elasticache-valkey`]}),n(`Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) is a DynamoDB-compatible caching service. As an in-memory cache, it reduces the response times of eventually consistent read workloads from single-digit milliseconds to microseconds.`,`dax`,[`DAX is a DynamoDB-compatible caching service that enables you to benefit from fast in-memory performance for demanding applications.`,`DAX addresses three core scenarios: As an in-memory cache, DAX reduces the response times of eventually consistent read workloads by an order of magnitude from single-digit milliseconds to microseconds.`]),n(`DAX only serves eventually consistent reads from its cache: if a request specifies strongly consistent reads, DAX passes it through to DynamoDB. AWS also lists applications that require strongly consistent reads, and write-intensive applications, as not ideal for DAX.`,[`dax-concepts`,`dax`],[`dax-concepts|If the request specifies strongly consistent reads, DAX passes the request through to DynamoDB.`,`dax|DAX is not ideal for the following types of applications: Applications that require strongly consistent reads (or that cannot tolerate eventually consistent reads).`,`dax|Applications that are write-intensive.`]),n(`API Gateway can cache responses for a REST API stage for a time-to-live period, which reduces calls to your endpoint and improves latency.`,`apigw-caching`,[`When you enable caching for a stage, API Gateway caches responses from your endpoint for a specified time-to-live (TTL) period, in seconds.`])],services:[`Amazon ElastiCache`,`Amazon CloudFront`,`Amazon DynamoDB`,`Amazon API Gateway`],design:[n(`A cache between the application servers and the database reduces the database read load, which may free resources for writes, and can also improve latency. Popular stores for session data are in-memory caches such as Memcached or Redis.`,`msa-data-store`,[`Putting a cache between application servers and a database is a common mechanism for reducing the read load on the database, which, in turn, may allow resources to be used to support more writes.`,`Popular stores for session data are in-memory caches such as Memcached or Redis.`]),n(`The strategy depends on the data and its access pattern: lazy loading caches only requested items but its cached data can become stale; write-through keeps cached data from going stale but can leave data missing. A TTL on cached items minimises wasted space.`,`elasticache-strategies`,[`What strategies to implement for populating and maintaining your cache depend upon what data you cache and the access patterns to that data.`,`If data is written to the cache only when there is a cache miss, data in the cache can become stale.`,`The advantages of write-through are as follows: Data in the cache is never stale.`,`The disadvantages of write-through are as follows: Missing data.`,`By adding a time to live (TTL) value, you can minimize wasted space.`])]},{id:`2.1-K7`,concepts:[n(`Amazon CloudFront is a content delivery network (CDN) web service that speeds up distribution of static and dynamic web content. It delivers content through a worldwide network of data centres called edge locations. A request is routed to the edge location with the lowest latency; if the content is already there CloudFront delivers it immediately, otherwise it retrieves it from the origin, such as an S3 bucket or an HTTP server.`,`cloudfront-what-is`,[`Amazon CloudFront is a web service that speeds up distribution of your static and dynamic web content, such as .html, .css, .js, and image files, to your users.`,`CloudFront delivers your content through a worldwide network of data centers called edge locations.`,`If the content is already in the edge location with the lowest latency, CloudFront delivers it immediately.`,`CloudFront retrieves it from an origin that you've defined—such as an Amazon S3 bucket, a MediaPackage channel, or an HTTP server`]),n(`You control how long objects stay in CloudFront's cache. After a file expires, the next request makes the edge location forward the request to the origin to verify that the cache has the latest version. Longer cache duration reduces the load on your origin.`,`cloudfront-expiration`,[`After it expires, the next time the edge location gets a request for the file, CloudFront forwards the request to the origin to verify that the cache contains the latest version of the file.`,`A longer duration also reduces the load on your origin.`]),n(`AWS Global Accelerator also uses the AWS global network and edge locations, but it does a different job from CloudFront. It improves performance over TCP or UDP by proxying packets at the edge to applications in one or more AWS Regions. With a standard accelerator it directs traffic over the AWS global network to endpoints in the nearest Region to the client, using static IP addresses that are anycast from the AWS edge network. The CloudFront vs Global Accelerator comparison is in Edge Link Terminal.`,[`ga-what-is`,`ga-faq`],[`ga-what-is|With a standard accelerator, Global Accelerator directs traffic over the AWS global network to endpoints in the nearest Region to the client.`,`ga-what-is|The static IP addresses are anycast from the AWS edge network.`,`ga-faq|Global Accelerator improves performance for a wide range of applications over TCP or UDP by proxying packets at the edge to applications running in one or more AWS Regions.`,`ga-faq|AWS Global Accelerator and Amazon CloudFront are separate services that use the AWS global network and its edge locations around the world.`])],services:[`Amazon CloudFront`,`AWS Global Accelerator`],design:[n(`Use a CDN when many users in many places fetch the same content: users get lower latency and higher data transfer rates, and copies held at multiple edge locations also raise reliability and availability.`,`cloudfront-what-is`,[`Users get lower latency—the time it takes to load the first byte of the file—and higher data transfer rates.`,`You also get increased reliability and availability because copies of your files (also known as objects) are now held (or cached) in multiple edge locations around the world.`])]}],cues:[e(`serve static and dynamic web content to global users with low latency`,`Amazon CloudFront`,n(`CloudFront speeds up distribution of static and dynamic content through edge locations.`,`cloudfront-what-is`,[`Amazon CloudFront is a web service that speeds up distribution of your static and dynamic web content, such as .html, .css, .js, and image files, to your users.`])),e(`reduce load on the origin / database read-heavy`,`A cache (CloudFront at the edge, ElastiCache before the database)`,n(`A longer cache duration reduces the load on the origin, and a cache before the database reduces its read load.`,[`cloudfront-expiration`,`msa-data-store`],[`cloudfront-expiration|A longer duration also reduces the load on your origin.`,`msa-data-store|Putting a cache between application servers and a database is a common mechanism for reducing the read load on the database, which, in turn, may allow resources to be used to support more writes.`])),e(`in-memory cache for DynamoDB, microsecond reads`,`DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)`,n(`DAX reduces eventually consistent read response times from single-digit milliseconds to microseconds.`,`dax`,[`As an in-memory cache, DAX reduces the response times of eventually consistent read workloads by an order of magnitude from single-digit milliseconds to microseconds.`])),e(`in-memory data store/cache, session store, Redis or Memcached compatible`,`Amazon ElastiCache`,n(`ElastiCache is a managed distributed in-memory data store or cache that works with Valkey, Memcached and Redis OSS.`,`elasticache-what-is`,[`NoteAmazon ElastiCache works with the Valkey, Memcached, and Redis OSS engines.`],{newer:[`elasticache-valkey`]})),e(`cached data must never be stale`,`Write-through caching`,n(`With write-through, data in the cache is never stale.`,`elasticache-strategies`,[`The advantages of write-through are as follows: Data in the cache is never stale.`])),e(`only cache what is actually requested`,`Lazy loading`,n(`With lazy loading only requested data is cached.`,`elasticache-strategies`,[`The advantages of lazy loading are as follows: Only requested data is cached.`])),e(`static IP addresses, deterministic fast regional failover, or non-HTTP traffic such as gaming (UDP), IoT (MQTT) or voice over IP`,`AWS Global Accelerator`,n(`AWS describes Global Accelerator as a good fit for non-HTTP use cases such as gaming (UDP), IoT (MQTT) or voice over IP, and for HTTP use cases that specifically require static IP addresses or deterministic, fast regional failover.`,`ga-faq`,[`Global Accelerator is a good fit for non-HTTP use cases, such as gaming (UDP), IoT (MQTT), or Voice over IP, as well as for HTTP use cases that specifically require static IP addresses or deterministic, fast regional failover.`]))],examples:[],confuse:[],azure:[{concept:`Azure Front Door`,aws:`Amazon CloudFront`,mapping:n(`The Learn comparison pairs CloudFront with Azure Front Door, a content delivery network service; it also lists Azure Front Door as a counterpart to AWS Global Accelerator.`,[`learn-networking`,`cloudfront-what-is`],[`learn-networking|Content delivery networks CloudFront Azure Front Door Azure Front Door is a modern cloud content delivery network (CDN) service that provides high performance, scalability, and secure user experiences for your content and applications.`,`learn-networking|Global traffic acceleration (anycast on backbone) AWS Global Accelerator Azure Front Door / Azure cross-region load balancer`,`cloudfront-what-is|CloudFront delivers your content through a worldwide network of data centers called edge locations.`]),breaks:n(`One Azure service (Front Door) appears on both sides of the Microsoft Learn comparison, but AWS keeps two separate products: CloudFront (cache content at the edge) and Global Accelerator (route traffic over the AWS network with static anycast IPs). Microsoft Learn also notes that Front Door works at layer 7 with integrated CDN and WAF, while the closest match for Global Accelerator is the layer-4 cross-region load balancer.`,[`learn-networking`,`ga-what-is`],[`learn-networking|Azure Front Door operates at layer 7 (HTTP/HTTPS) with integrated CDN and WAF.`,`learn-networking|Azure cross-region Load Balancer operates at layer 4 for TCP/UDP, which closely matches AWS Global Accelerator's layer-4 behavior.`,`ga-what-is|The static IP addresses are anycast from the AWS edge network.`],{allow:[`two`]})},{concept:`Azure Managed Redis`,aws:`Amazon ElastiCache`,mapping:n(`Microsoft Learn pairs Amazon ElastiCache with Azure Managed Redis as the in-memory cache and temporary data store, for caching, sessions and other low-latency temporary-data patterns.`,[`learn-databases`,`elasticache-what-is`],[`learn-databases|In-memory cache and temporary data store Amazon ElastiCache Azure Managed Redis Use Azure Managed Redis for caching, sessions, messaging, and other low-latency temporary-data patterns.`,`elasticache-what-is|Amazon ElastiCache is a web service that makes it easy to set up, manage, and scale a distributed in-memory data store or cache environment in the cloud.`]),breaks:n(`Microsoft Learn describes Azure Managed Redis as a cache and temporary data store, and lists no direct Azure equivalent for Amazon MemoryDB, which is designed as a durable primary database.`,[`learn-databases`,`memorydb`],[`learn-databases|Azure Managed Redis is a cache and temporary data store.`,`learn-databases|Durable Redis-compatible database Amazon MemoryDB No direct equivalent Amazon MemoryDB is designed as a durable primary database.`,`memorydb|Delivering both in-memory performance and Multi-AZ durability, MemoryDB can be used as a high-performance primary database for your microservices applications, eliminating the need to separately manage both a cache and durable database.`])}]},a={building:`cargo-hall`,overview:[n(`AWS storage comes in three types: block, file and object. The type determines how data is accessed, so identify the type first and the service second. Amazon EBS is block storage, Amazon EFS and Amazon FSx are file storage, and Amazon S3 is object storage.`,`storage-decision`,[`You can select from block, file, and object storage services as well as cloud data migration options for your workload.`,`Protocols play a crucial role when considering AWS storage services as they determine how data is accessed, transferred, and managed within the storage environment.`],{allow:[`three`]}),n(`This building teaches the three types and how AWS decision guides help you choose compute, storage and database technologies from requirements. The detailed comparisons of EBS, EFS and FSx, and of instance store and EBS, are in Storage Market and Block Warehouse.`,[`storage-decision`,`compute-decision`,`database-decision`],[`storage-decision|You can select from block, file, and object storage services as well as cloud data migration options for your workload.`,`compute-decision|These include Amazon EC2 for resizable virtual servers, Elastic Beanstalk for deploying web applications on your choice of a number of popular platforms, AWS Lambda for serverless computing, Amazon ECS and EKS for container orchestration, and AWS Fargate for serverless containers.`,`database-decision|AWS offers a growing number of database options (15+) with diverse data models to support a variety of workloads.`],{allow:[`three`]})],bullets:[{id:`2.1-K13`,concepts:[n(`Object storage stores and manages data in an unstructured format called objects, and provides access through an application programming interface (API) over the internet. It is well suited to read-heavy workloads such as streaming applications. Amazon S3 is an object storage service that stores data as objects within buckets.`,[`storage-decision`,`s3-what-is`],[`storage-decision|Object — Object storage is a technology that stores and manages data in an unstructured format called objects.`,`storage-decision|Object storage provides easy access to data through an application programming interface (API) over the internet and is well-suited to read-heavy workloads (such as streaming applications and services).`,`s3-what-is|Amazon S3 is an object storage service that stores data as objects, hierarchical data, or tabular data within buckets.`]),n(`Block storage divides data into blocks of equal size and stores each block on underlying physical storage in a manner optimised for fast access. It offers high-performance storage direct-attached to a compute instance with low-latency access. Amazon EBS provides block storage volumes that you attach to Amazon EC2 instances and then use like a local hard drive.`,[`storage-decision`,`ebs-what-is`],[`storage-decision|It takes any data, like a file or database entry, and divides it into blocks of equal sizes.`,`storage-decision|Block storage offers high-performance storage that is direct-attached to a compute instance with low-latency access, making it suitable for applications that require fast and consistent I/O operations.`,`ebs-what-is|Amazon EBS volumes — These are storage volumes that you attach to Amazon EC2 instances.`,`ebs-what-is|After you attach a volume to an instance, you can use it in the same way you would use a local hard drive attached to a computer, for example to store files or to install applications.`]),n(`File storage keeps data in a hierarchical structure of files and folders and is natively mountable from virtually any operating system using industry-standard protocols like NFS and SMB. Amazon EFS provides serverless, fully elastic file storage that supports the Network File System (NFS) protocol, so multiple clients can share file data; Amazon FSx offers managed file systems that align to widely used file systems and protocols.`,[`storage-decision`,`efs-what-is`],[`storage-decision|File system — File systems store data in a hierarchical structure of files and folders.`,`storage-decision|File-based storage is natively mountable from virtually any operating system using industry-standard protocols like NFS and SMB.`,`efs-what-is|Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS) provides serverless, fully elastic file storage so that you can share file data without provisioning or managing storage capacity and performance.`,`efs-what-is|Amazon EFS supports the Network File System version 4 (NFSv4.1 and NFSv4.0) protocol, so the applications and tools that you use today work seamlessly with Amazon EFS.`]),n(`AWS summarises the match between workload and type: block for applications requiring low-latency, high-performance durable storage attached to single EC2 instances or containers, such as databases; file for applications requiring shared read and write access across multiple EC2 instances or containers, or from multiple on-premises servers, such as team file shares.`,`storage-decision`,[`Storage services or tools Block Applications requiring low-latency, high-performance durable storage attached to single Amazon EC2 instances or containers, such as databases and general-purpose local instance storage.`,`Applications and workloads requiring shared read and write access across multiple Amazon EC2 instances or containers, or from multiple on-premises servers, such as team file shares, highly available enterprise applications, analytics workloads, and ML training.`]),n(`Block storage is usually attached to a single instance, but EBS Multi-Attach is an exception: it lets you attach a single Provisioned IOPS SSD (io1 or io2) volume to multiple instances in the same Availability Zone.`,`ebs-multi-attach`,[`Amazon EBS Multi-Attach enables you to attach a single Provisioned IOPS SSD (io1 or io2) volume to multiple instances that are in the same Availability Zone.`]),n(`EBS snapshots are point-in-time backups of EBS volumes that persist independently from the volume itself.`,`ebs-what-is`,[`Amazon EBS snapshots — These are point-in-time backups of Amazon EBS volumes that persist independently from the volume itself.`])],services:[`Amazon S3`,`Amazon EFS`,`Amazon EBS`,`Amazon EC2`],design:[n(`Ask what the access pattern is: a disk attached to an instance (block, EBS), a file system mounted and shared by many clients (file, for example Amazon EFS), or objects stored and fetched by API (S3). AWS says that aligning a storage service with your access patterns is critical to achieving the performance, cost, efficiency, security, and resiliency you want.`,[`storage-decision`,`efs-what-is`],[`storage-decision|When selecting a storage service, ensuring that it aligns with your access patterns will be critical to achieving the performance, cost, efficiency, security, and resiliency you want.`,`efs-what-is|Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS) provides serverless, fully elastic file storage so that you can share file data without provisioning or managing storage capacity and performance.`,`storage-decision|Protocols play a crucial role when considering AWS storage services as they determine how data is accessed, transferred, and managed within the storage environment.`,`storage-decision|Storage services or tools Block Applications requiring low-latency, high-performance durable storage attached to single Amazon EC2 instances or containers, such as databases and general-purpose local instance storage.`,`storage-decision|File-based storage is natively mountable from virtually any operating system using industry-standard protocols like NFS and SMB.`,`storage-decision|Object storage provides easy access to data through an application programming interface (API) over the internet and is well-suited to read-heavy workloads (such as streaming applications and services).`])]},{id:`2.1-S6`,concepts:[n(`To recommend technology from requirements, AWS publishes decision guides. For compute, the guide lists Amazon EC2 for resizable virtual servers, Elastic Beanstalk for deploying web applications, AWS Lambda for serverless computing, Amazon ECS and EKS for container orchestration, and AWS Fargate for serverless containers.`,`compute-decision`,[`These include Amazon EC2 for resizable virtual servers, Elastic Beanstalk for deploying web applications on your choice of a number of popular platforms, AWS Lambda for serverless computing, Amazon ECS and EKS for container orchestration, and AWS Fargate for serverless containers.`]),n(`For databases, AWS offers many database options with diverse data models: relational, key-value, document, in-memory, graph, time series, vector and wide-column. The decision guide's OLTP databases fall into two categories, relational and non-relational. Purpose-Built Workshop covers the data-model matching in detail.`,`database-decision`,[`AWS offers a growing number of database options (15+) with diverse data models to support a variety of workloads.`,`These include relational, key-value, document, in-memory, graph, time series, vector, and wide-column.`,`There are two high-level categories of AWS OLTP databases—relational and non-relational.`],{allow:[`two`]}),n(`For storage, match block, file and object to the workload (see the previous bullet). The networking technologies are covered in their own buildings: Elastic Load Balancing in Scaling Floodgate, and CloudFront and Global Accelerator in Cache & Edge Breakwater.`,`storage-decision`,[`You can select from block, file, and object storage services as well as cloud data migration options for your workload.`]),n(`The decision guides expect you to weigh requirements, not just features: for example, for databases, performance (does it need to support a high concurrency of transactions, or deploy in multiple geographic Regions), and for storage, choosing right-sized storage classes and tiering to reduce the storage footprint.`,[`database-decision`,`storage-decision`],[`database-decision|Performance - Consider whether your database will need to support a high concurrency of transactions (10,000 or more), and whether it needs to be deployed in multiple geographic regions.`,`storage-decision|For storage, choosing right-sized storage classes and using tiering such as Intelligent-Tiering and Infrequent Access or Archive tiers helps reduce your storage footprint.`])],services:[`Amazon EC2`,`AWS Lambda`,`Amazon S3`,`Amazon EBS`,`Amazon EFS`],design:[n(`A good answer names the technology and the reason in the question's own terms: shared file system for many Linux servers → EFS; attached low-latency disk for a database on EC2 → EBS; objects fetched by API → S3; no servers to manage → Lambda or Fargate.`,[`storage-decision`,`efs-what-is`,`lambda-welcome`,`fargate-ecs`],[`storage-decision|Storage services or tools Block Applications requiring low-latency, high-performance durable storage attached to single Amazon EC2 instances or containers, such as databases and general-purpose local instance storage.`,`storage-decision|Object storage provides easy access to data through an application programming interface (API) over the internet and is well-suited to read-heavy workloads (such as streaming applications and services).`,`efs-what-is|Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS) provides serverless, fully elastic file storage so that you can share file data without provisioning or managing storage capacity and performance.`,`lambda-welcome|With Lambda, you can run code without provisioning or managing servers.`,`fargate-ecs|With AWS Fargate, you no longer have to provision, configure, or scale clusters of virtual machines to run containers.`])]}],cues:[e(`shared file system mounted by many EC2 instances, NFS`,`Amazon EFS (Linux, NFS)`,n(`EFS provides serverless, elastic file storage for sharing file data and supports the NFS protocol.`,`efs-what-is`,[`Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS) provides serverless, fully elastic file storage so that you can share file data without provisioning or managing storage capacity and performance.`])),e(`SMB / Windows file shares / NetApp ONTAP / OpenZFS / Lustre`,`Amazon FSx (matching variant)`,n(`FSx offers managed file systems for Windows (SMB), NetApp ONTAP (NFS and SMB), OpenZFS (NFS) and Lustre (speed-sensitive workloads such as HPC).`,[`storage-decision`,`fsx-windows`,`fsx-openzfs`,`fsx-lustre`],[`storage-decision|Amazon FSx for NetApp ONTAP supports both NFS and SMB protocols, including concurrent multi-protocol access to the same data.`,`fsx-windows|Amazon FSx has native support for Windows file system features and for the industry-standard Server Message Block (SMB) protocol to access file storage over a network.`,`fsx-openzfs|Amazon FSx for OpenZFS file systems are broadly accessible from Linux, Windows, and macOS compute instances and containers using the industry-standard NFS protocol (v3, v4.0, v4.1, v4.2).`,`fsx-lustre|You use Lustre for workloads where speed matters, such as machine learning, high performance computing (HPC), video processing, and financial modeling.`])),e(`boot volume / low-latency disk attached to one instance / database on EC2`,`Amazon EBS`,n(`EBS volumes attach to EC2 instances and behave like a local hard drive.`,`ebs-what-is`,[`After you attach a volume to an instance, you can use it in the same way you would use a local hard drive attached to a computer, for example to store files or to install applications.`])),e(`store unstructured data as objects, access over an API / static website / data lake`,`Amazon S3`,n(`Object storage provides access to data through an API over the internet and suits read-heavy workloads such as streaming.`,`storage-decision`,[`Object storage provides easy access to data through an application programming interface (API) over the internet and is well-suited to read-heavy workloads (such as streaming applications and services).`])),e(`point-in-time backup of a disk`,`EBS snapshot`,n(`EBS snapshots are point-in-time backups of volumes that persist independently from the volume.`,`ebs-what-is`,[`Amazon EBS snapshots — These are point-in-time backups of Amazon EBS volumes that persist independently from the volume itself.`]))],examples:[],confuse:[],azure:[{concept:`Azure Blob storage`,aws:`Amazon S3`,mapping:n(`Learn describes Azure Blob storage as storing any type of text or binary data, such as a document, media file or application installer, and compares it with AWS S3.`,[`learn-storage`,`s3-what-is`],[`learn-storage|In Azure Storage, subscription-bound storage accounts allow you to create and manage the following storage services: Azure Blob Storage stores any type of text or binary data, such as a document, media file, or application installer.`,`s3-what-is|Amazon S3 is an object storage service that stores data as objects, hierarchical data, or tabular data within buckets.`]),breaks:n(`Learn pairs Blob Storage with AWS S3 and says the Azure equivalent of EBS is managed disks, which are not part of a storage account. On AWS, S3 and EBS are different storage types: S3 is object storage accessed through an API, and EBS is block storage attached to an EC2 instance.`,[`learn-storage`,`storage-decision`,`ebs-what-is`],[`learn-storage|Blob Storage is object storage, so it serves the same purpose as AWS S3.`,`learn-storage|The Azure equivalent of EBS is managed disks, which aren't part of a storage account.`,`storage-decision|Object storage provides easy access to data through an application programming interface (API) over the internet and is well-suited to read-heavy workloads (such as streaming applications and services).`,`storage-decision|Block storage offers high-performance storage that is direct-attached to a compute instance with low-latency access, making it suitable for applications that require fast and consistent I/O operations.`,`ebs-what-is|Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS) provides scalable, high-performance block storage resources that can be used with Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) instances.`])},{concept:`Azure Files`,aws:`Amazon EFS and Amazon FSx`,mapping:n(`Learn groups the shared AWS storage services (Amazon EFS and the FSx family of managed file systems) as the file-sharing side of AWS storage, which Azure serves with Azure Files.`,[`learn-storage`,`efs-what-is`],[`learn-storage|Various shared storage services that AWS provides, like Amazon Elastic File System (EFS) and the Amazon FSx family of managed file systems.`,`learn-storage|Azure Files can be deployed in two main ways: by directly mounting the serverless Azure file shares or by caching Azure file shares on-premises with Azure File Sync.`,`efs-what-is|Amazon EFS supports the Network File System version 4 (NFSv4.1 and NFSv4.0) protocol, so the applications and tools that you use today work seamlessly with Amazon EFS.`],{allow:[`two`]}),breaks:n(`AWS splits file storage across services by protocol and file system: EFS for NFS, and FSx variants for Windows (SMB), NetApp ONTAP (NFS and SMB), OpenZFS (NFS) and Lustre. The Learn comparison groups EFS and the FSx family together on the AWS side.`,[`storage-decision`,`fsx-windows`,`fsx-openzfs`,`efs-what-is`,`learn-storage`],[`efs-what-is|Amazon EFS supports the Network File System version 4 (NFSv4.1 and NFSv4.0) protocol, so the applications and tools that you use today work seamlessly with Amazon EFS.`,`storage-decision|Amazon FSx for Lustre (for high-performance file systems) is designed for use with Unix/Linux-based file systems.`,`storage-decision|Amazon FSx for NetApp ONTAP supports both NFS and SMB protocols, including concurrent multi-protocol access to the same data.`,`fsx-windows|Amazon FSx has native support for Windows file system features and for the industry-standard Server Message Block (SMB) protocol to access file storage over a network.`,`fsx-openzfs|Amazon FSx for OpenZFS file systems are broadly accessible from Linux, Windows, and macOS compute instances and containers using the industry-standard NFS protocol (v3, v4.0, v4.1, v4.2).`,`learn-storage|Various shared storage services that AWS provides, like Amazon Elastic File System (EFS) and the Amazon FSx family of managed file systems.`])},{concept:`Azure managed disks`,aws:`Amazon EBS`,mapping:n(`Learn pairs Amazon EBS with Azure managed disks, SSD storage that is optimised for I/O-intensive read/write operations, and its table matches EBS volume types to Azure disk types.`,[`learn-storage`,`ebs-what-is`],[`learn-storage|Virtual server disks AWS service Azure service Description Elastic Block Store (EBS) Managed disks SSD storage that's optimized for I/O-intensive read/write operations.`,`learn-storage|AWS EBS volume type Azure managed disk Use for Can this managed disk be used as an OS disk? gp2/gp3 Standard SSD`,`ebs-what-is|Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS) provides scalable, high-performance block storage resources that can be used with Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) instances.`]),breaks:n(`Learn notes that for higher-performing disks, on both clouds you need to match the storage performance of the VM with the provisioned disk type and configuration. EBS volume-type choices are covered in Block Warehouse.`,[`learn-storage`,`ebs-volume-types`],[`learn-storage|For higher-performing disks, on both AWS and Azure you need to match the storage performance of the VM with the provisioned disk type and configuration.`,`ebs-volume-types|Amazon EBS provides the following volume types, which differ in performance characteristics and price, so that you can tailor your storage performance and cost to the needs of your applications.`])}]},o={building:`container-dock`,overview:[n(`A container packages an application with its dependencies so it runs the same way anywhere a container runtime exists. AWS describes the benefits as portability (a consistent runtime environment on any platform that supports the container runtime) and gives you a layered choice: a registry to store images, an orchestrator to schedule them, and compute to run them.`,`container-decision`,[`Portability: Benefit from a consistent runtime environment that can run on any platform that supports the container runtime.`,`The orchestration layer schedules and scales your environment.`])],bullets:[{id:`2.1-K8`,concepts:[n(`To migrate an existing application into containers you build a container image, store it in a registry, and run it on an orchestrator. Amazon ECR is the AWS managed container image registry; you push, pull and manage Docker and OCI images with your preferred CLI, and private repositories use resource-based permissions with IAM.`,`ecr-what-is`,[`Amazon Elastic Container Registry (Amazon ECR) is an AWS managed container image registry service that is secure, scalable, and reliable.`,`Amazon ECR supports private repositories with resource-based permissions using AWS IAM.`,`You can use your preferred CLI to push, pull, and manage Docker images, Open Container Initiative (OCI) images, and OCI compatible artifacts.`]),n(`AWS App2Container was a command line tool to lift and shift applications from on-premises data centres or virtual machines into containers managed by Amazon ECS, Amazon EKS or AWS App Runner, without needing the application's source code. AWS now states that it is no longer open to new customers, so treat it as background, not as the answer to a new design. It is also not on the exam guide's in-scope list.`,`a2c`,[`AWS App2Container (A2C) is a command line tool to help you lift and shift applications that run in your on-premises data centers or on virtual machines, so that they run in containers that are managed by Amazon ECS, Amazon EKS, or AWS App Runner.`,`App2Container does not need source code for the application to containerize it.`,`AWS .NET Modernization Tools Porting Assistant (PA) for .NET, AWS App2Container (A2C), AWS Toolkit for .NET Refactoring (TR), and AWS Microservice Extractor (ME) for .NET is no longer open to new customers.`],{status:`closed`}),n(`AWS lists the benefits of containerisation as reduced operational overhead and infrastructure costs, increased development and deployment agility, and standardised build and deployment processes across an organisation.`,`a2c`,[`There are many benefits to containerization: Reduces operational overhead and infrastructure costs Increases development and deployment agility Standardizes build and deployment processes across an organization`])],services:[`Amazon ECR`,`Amazon ECS`,`Amazon EKS`,`AWS Fargate`],design:[n(`Migration to containers is easier when the application is already stateless: state held on the instance has to move to a managed store before any container can be replaced freely (see Blueprint Hall).`,`waf-stateless`,[`Systems should either not require state, or should offload state such that between different client requests, there is no dependence on locally stored data on disk and in memory.`])]},{id:`2.1-K14`,concepts:[n(`Amazon ECS is a fully managed container orchestration service that helps you deploy, manage and scale containerised applications. A task definition is the blueprint for the application; a task is an application such as a batch job that performs work and then stops; a service is a long-running stateless application; a cluster is the infrastructure your application runs on.`,`ecs-what-is`,[`Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS) is a fully managed container orchestration service that helps you easily deploy, manage, and scale containerized applications.`,`Task definition The blueprint for the application.`,`Task An application such as a batch job that performs work, and then stops.`,`Service A long running stateless application.`,`Cluster The infrastructure your application runs on.`]),n(`ECS capacity, the infrastructure where containers run, can be Fargate (a serverless, pay-as-you-go compute engine where you do not manage servers), Amazon EC2 instances (you choose the instance type and number and manage the capacity), ECS Managed Instances (EC2 instance types with AWS handling provisioning, patching and scaling), or on-premises servers through ECS Anywhere.`,`ecs-what-is`,[`The capacity is the infrastructure where your containers run.`,`Serverless in the AWS cloud Fargate is a serverless, pay-as-you-go compute engine.`,`Amazon EC2 instances in the AWS cloud You choose the instance type, the number of instances, and manage the capacity.`,`Amazon ECS Managed Instances is a compute option for Amazon ECS that enables you to run containerized workloads on a range of Amazon EC2 instance types while offloading infrastructure management to AWS.`,`Amazon ECS Anywhere provides support for registering an external instance such as an on-premises server or virtual machine (VM), to your Amazon ECS cluster.`]),n(`Amazon EKS is a fully managed Kubernetes service. It is certified Kubernetes-conformant, so Kubernetes-compatible applications deploy without refactoring. With EKS standard, AWS manages the Kubernetes control plane; EKS Auto Mode extends that to managing nodes as well.`,`eks-what-is`,[`Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS) provides a fully managed Kubernetes service that eliminates the complexity of operating Kubernetes clusters.`,`Amazon EKS is certified Kubernetes-conformant, so you can deploy Kubernetes-compatible applications without refactoring and use Kubernetes community tooling and plugins.`,`EKS standard: AWS manages the Kubernetes control plane when you create a cluster with EKS.`,`EKS Auto Mode: Using the EKS Auto Mode feature, EKS extends its control to manage Nodes (Kubernetes data plane) as well.`],{newer:[`eks-auto-mode`]}),n(`Fargate is not a separate orchestrator: you can use Fargate with both Amazon ECS and Amazon EKS, and EC2 instances can be the compute layer for both too.`,`container-decision`,[`You can use Fargate with both Amazon ECS and Amazon EKS.`,`EC2 instances can be used as the compute layer for both Amazon ECS and Amazon EKS.`]),n(`AWS App Runner is a fully managed service that simplifies deploying containerised web applications and APIs: you provide a container image and App Runner builds, deploys and scales the application. AWS now states that App Runner is no longer open to new customers, so treat it as background, not as the answer to a new design. It is also not on the exam guide's in-scope list.`,[`container-decision`,`apprunner-what-is`],[`container-decision|AWS App Runner is a fully managed service designed to simplify the deployment and use of containerized web applications and APIs.`,`container-decision|You provide your container image, and App Runner automatically builds, deploys, and scales your application.`,`apprunner-what-is|AWS App Runner is no longer open to new customers.`],{status:`closed`})],services:[`Amazon ECS`,`Amazon EKS`,`AWS Fargate`,`Amazon ECR`],design:[n(`One of the first considerations AWS names is your operating model: a serverless operational model or a Kubernetes operational model. Amazon EKS provides flexibility and control over deployment and orchestration provided by Kubernetes technology.`,`container-decision`,[`One of the first considerations is your preference for either a serverless operational model or a Kubernetes operation model.`,`Amazon EKS provides flexibility and control over application deployment and orchestration provided by Kubernetes technology.`])]},{id:`2.1-S4`,concepts:[n(`Use containers when you want portability and a consistent runtime, or when the application does not fit a function's limits but you still want to avoid managing servers (Fargate). Being packaged as an image does not rule out Lambda, because Lambda also accepts container images as a deployment package; Lambda is still aimed at request-response or event-driven work (see Serverless Mill). Choose EC2 capacity when you want to choose the instance type and manage the capacity yourself.`,[`container-decision`,`fargate-ecs`,`lambda-images`,`lambda-welcome`,`ecs-what-is`],[`container-decision|Portability: Benefit from a consistent runtime environment that can run on any platform that supports the container runtime.`,`fargate-ecs|With AWS Fargate, you no longer have to provision, configure, or scale clusters of virtual machines to run containers.`,`lambda-images|Lambda supports two types of deployment packages: container images and .zip file archives.`,`lambda-welcome|Request-response or event-driven workloads (APIs, data processing, automation)`,`ecs-what-is|Amazon EC2 instances in the AWS cloud You choose the instance type, the number of instances, and manage the capacity.`]),n(`With Fargate you do not need to manage servers, handle capacity planning, or isolate container workloads for security; with the EC2 option you choose the instance type, the number of instances and manage the capacity.`,`ecs-what-is`,[`With Fargate you don't need to manage servers, handle capacity planning, or isolate container workloads for security.`,`Amazon EC2 instances in the AWS cloud You choose the instance type, the number of instances, and manage the capacity.`])],services:[`Amazon ECS`,`Amazon EKS`,`AWS Fargate`,`AWS Lambda`,`Amazon EC2`],design:[n(`Typical answer shapes: a containerised microservice where you want minimal server management → ECS on Fargate; existing Kubernetes workloads or Kubernetes tooling required → EKS; short event-driven tasks → Lambda.`,[`ecs-what-is`,`eks-what-is`,`lambda-welcome`],[`ecs-what-is|With Fargate you don't need to manage servers, handle capacity planning, or isolate container workloads for security.`,`eks-what-is|Amazon EKS is certified Kubernetes-conformant, so you can deploy Kubernetes-compatible applications without refactoring and use Kubernetes community tooling and plugins.`,`lambda-welcome|Request-response or event-driven workloads (APIs, data processing, automation)`])]}],cues:[e(`run containers without managing servers or clusters of instances`,`AWS Fargate (with ECS or EKS)`,n(`With Fargate you no longer provision, configure or scale clusters of virtual machines to run containers.`,`fargate-ecs`,[`With AWS Fargate, you no longer have to provision, configure, or scale clusters of virtual machines to run containers.`])),e(`existing Kubernetes workloads / must use Kubernetes APIs and tooling`,`Amazon EKS`,n(`EKS is certified Kubernetes-conformant, so Kubernetes-compatible applications deploy without refactoring.`,`eks-what-is`,[`Amazon EKS is certified Kubernetes-conformant, so you can deploy Kubernetes-compatible applications without refactoring and use Kubernetes community tooling and plugins.`])),e(`managed container orchestration (not Kubernetes)`,`Amazon ECS`,n(`Amazon ECS is a fully managed container orchestration service.`,`ecs-what-is`,[`Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS) is a fully managed container orchestration service that helps you easily deploy, manage, and scale containerized applications.`])),e(`store and manage container images privately`,`Amazon ECR`,n(`ECR is a managed container image registry with private repositories and IAM permissions.`,`ecr-what-is`,[`Amazon ECR supports private repositories with resource-based permissions using AWS IAM.`])),e(`containers must run on your own on-premises servers under ECS`,`Amazon ECS Anywhere`,n(`ECS Anywhere lets you register an on-premises server or VM to your ECS cluster.`,`ecs-what-is`,[`Amazon ECS Anywhere provides support for registering an external instance such as an on-premises server or virtual machine (VM), to your Amazon ECS cluster.`]))],examples:[],confuse:[],azure:[{concept:`Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS)`,aws:`Amazon EKS`,mapping:n(`The Learn comparison pairs Amazon EKS with Azure Kubernetes Service: both orchestrate containerised application deployments with Kubernetes.`,[`learn-compute`,`eks-what-is`],[`learn-compute|Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS) Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS) You can use Amazon EKS and Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS) to orchestrate Docker containerized application deployments with Kubernetes.`,`eks-what-is|Amazon EKS is certified Kubernetes-conformant, so you can deploy Kubernetes-compatible applications without refactoring and use Kubernetes community tooling and plugins.`]),breaks:n(`AKS has two modes (Standard and Automatic) and EKS also has two approaches (standard EKS and EKS Auto Mode), but AWS has a second, non-Kubernetes orchestrator, Amazon ECS. The Learn comparison pairs ECS with Azure Container Apps, not with AKS.`,[`learn-compute`,`eks-what-is`,`ecs-what-is`],[`learn-compute|Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS) provides two modes: AKS Standard for full control and customization and AKS Automatic for simplified, production-ready deployments with built-in best practices.`,`learn-compute|AWS service Azure service Description Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS) Azure Container Apps Amazon ECS is a container orchestration service for deploying and managing containerized applications.`,`eks-what-is|Two of the main approaches to using Amazon EKS are as follows: EKS standard: AWS manages the Kubernetes control plane when you create a cluster with EKS.`],{newer:[`eks-auto-mode`],allow:[`two`]})},{concept:`Azure Container Registry`,aws:`Amazon ECR`,mapping:n(`The Learn comparison pairs Amazon ECR with Azure Container Registry: container registries that store Docker-formatted images for container deployments.`,[`learn-compute`,`ecr-what-is`],[`learn-compute|Amazon Elastic Container Registry (Amazon ECR) Azure Container Registry Container registries store Docker-formatted images and create all types of container deployments in the cloud.`,`ecr-what-is|You can use your preferred CLI to push, pull, and manage Docker images, Open Container Initiative (OCI) images, and OCI compatible artifacts.`]),breaks:n(`The Microsoft Learn comparison describes both as registries for Docker-formatted images and says nothing about access control. On the AWS side, private ECR repositories have resource-based permissions using IAM.`,[`ecr-what-is`,`learn-compute`],[`ecr-what-is|Amazon ECR supports private repositories with resource-based permissions using AWS IAM.`,`learn-compute|Amazon Elastic Container Registry (Amazon ECR) Azure Container Registry Container registries store Docker-formatted images and create all types of container deployments in the cloud.`])},{concept:`Azure Container Instances / Azure Container Apps`,aws:`AWS Fargate`,mapping:n(`The Learn comparison pairs AWS Fargate with Azure Container Instances and Azure Container Apps: serverless container compute.`,[`learn-compute`,`fargate-ecs`],[`learn-compute|AWS Fargate Azure Container Instances, Azure Container Apps AWS Fargate is a serverless compute engine that provides compute capacity for Amazon ECS tasks and Amazon EKS pods.`,`fargate-ecs|With AWS Fargate, you no longer have to provision, configure, or scale clusters of virtual machines to run containers.`]),breaks:n(`On AWS, Fargate is one capacity option that you use with either ECS or EKS (EC2 instances are the other), so the orchestrator (ECS or EKS) and the capacity (Fargate or EC2) are chosen separately. The Microsoft Learn comparison pairs the one AWS service with two Azure services, Container Instances and Container Apps.`,[`container-decision`,`learn-compute`],[`container-decision|You can use Fargate with both Amazon ECS and Amazon EKS.`,`container-decision|EC2 instances can be used as the compute layer for both Amazon ECS and Amazon EKS.`,`learn-compute|AWS Fargate Azure Container Instances, Azure Container Apps AWS Fargate is a serverless compute engine that provides compute capacity for Amazon ECS tasks and Amazon EKS pods.`],{allow:[`two`]})}]},s={building:`disaster-bunker`,overview:[n(`Disaster recovery (DR) is planning for how to get back after a disaster. The decision is driven by the {{term:rto}} (how long you can be down) and the {{term:rpo}} (how much data you can lose). Read both in the question, then pick the strategy that meets them without more cost and complexity than needed.`,`dr-objectives`,[`When creating a Disaster Recovery (DR) strategy, organizations most commonly plan for the Recovery Time Objective (RTO) and Recovery Point Objective (RPO).`]),n(`Your DR plan should be a subset of the organization's business continuity plan, not a standalone document. The four strategies, compared in the Don't confuse section below, range from the low cost and low complexity of making backups to more complex strategies using multiple active Regions.`,[`dr-objectives`,`dr-options`],[`dr-objectives|Your disaster recovery plan should be a subset of your organization’s business continuity plan (BCP), it should not be a standalone document.`,`dr-options|Disaster recovery strategies available to you within AWS can be broadly categorized into four approaches, ranging from the low cost and low complexity of making backups to more complex strategies using multiple active Regions.`],{allow:[`four`]})],bullets:[{id:`2.2-K4`,concepts:[n(`RTO is the maximum acceptable delay between the interruption of service and restoration of service; RPO is the maximum acceptable amount of time since the last data recovery point. RTO is about downtime and RPO is about data loss.`,`dr-objectives`,[`Recovery Time Objective (RTO) is the maximum acceptable delay between the interruption of service and restoration of service.`,`Recovery Point Objective (RPO) is the maximum acceptable amount of time since the last data recovery point.`]),n(`Backup and restore: take periodic or continuous backups and restore them after a disaster. Suitable for mitigating data loss or corruption. How often you run your backup determines the recovery point you can achieve.`,`dr-options`,[`Backup and restore is a suitable approach for mitigating against data loss or corruption.`,`How often you run your backup will determine your achievable recovery point (which should align to meet your RPO).`]),n(`Pilot light: replicate data to another Region and provision a copy of the core infrastructure, with application servers switched off until failover. Warm standby: a scaled-down but fully functional copy that can handle traffic at reduced capacity immediately. Multi-site active/active: run in multiple Regions at once, serving traffic from all of them.`,`dr-options`,[`With the pilot light approach, you replicate your data from one Region to another and provision a copy of your core workload infrastructure.`,`The warm standby approach involves ensuring that there is a scaled down, but fully functional, copy of your production environment in another Region.`,`Multi-site active/active serves traffic from all regions to which it is deployed, whereas hot standby serves traffic only from a single region, and the other Region(s) are only used for disaster recovery.`]),n(`AWS provides continuous, cross-region, asynchronous replication through services including S3 Replication, RDS read replicas, Aurora global databases and DynamoDB global tables. Point-in-time backups are available through EBS snapshots, DynamoDB backup and RDS snapshots, and AWS Backup can copy backups across accounts and to other Regions.`,`dr-options`,[`AWS provides continuous, cross-region, asynchronous data replication for data using the following services and resources: Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) Replication Amazon RDS read replicas Amazon Aurora global databases Amazon DynamoDB global tables Amazon DocumentDB global clusters Global Datastore for Amazon ElastiCache (Redis OSS)`,`Or, you can use AWS Backup to copy backups across accounts and to other AWS Regions.`,`Backup with point-in-time recovery is available through the following services and resources: Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS) snapshot Amazon DynamoDB backup Amazon RDS snapshot`]),n(`AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery is a service for recovery of on-premises and cloud-based applications: it minimizes downtime and data loss using affordable storage, minimal compute, and point-in-time recovery, by replicating the applications running on supported operating systems.`,`drs-what-is`,[`AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery minimizes downtime and data loss with fast, reliable recovery of on-premises and cloud-based applications using affordable storage, minimal compute, and point-in-time recovery.`,`You can increase IT resilience when you use AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery to replicate on-premises or cloud-based applications running on supported operating systems.`]),n(`Back up configuration and infrastructure as well as data. In addition to data, you must back up the configuration and infrastructure necessary to redeploy the workload to meet your RTO; without infrastructure as code, restoring in the recovery Region may be complex and exceed the RTO.`,`dr-options`,[`In addition to data, you must also back up the configuration and infrastructure necessary to redeploy your workload and meet your Recovery Time Objective (RTO).`,`Without IaC, it may be complex to restore workloads in the recovery Region, which will lead to increased recovery times and possibly exceed your RTO.`])],services:[`AWS Backup`,`Amazon Route 53`],design:[n(`Choose by RTO and RPO: use your RTO and RPO needs to choose between the four approaches, which run from the low cost and low complexity of making backups to more complex strategies using multiple active Regions.`,`dr-options`,[`Use your RTO and RPO needs to help you choose between these approaches.`,`Disaster recovery strategies available to you within AWS can be broadly categorized into four approaches, ranging from the low cost and low complexity of making backups to more complex strategies using multiple active Regions.`],{allow:[`four`]})]},{id:`2.2-K6`,concepts:[n(`Failover is the switch of traffic from the failed environment to the recovery one. In active/passive designs the passive site does not actively serve traffic until a failover event is triggered; in active/active designs all sites serve traffic.`,`dr-options`,[`The passive site does not actively serve traffic until a failover event is triggered.`,`Multi-site active/active serves traffic from all regions to which it is deployed`]),n(`Route 53 does the traffic part: Route 53 health checks monitor the health and performance of web applications and other resources; active-passive failover uses the failover routing policy and active-active uses any other routing policy.`,[`route53-failover`,`route53-failover-types`],[`route53-failover|Amazon Route 53 health checks monitor the health and performance of your web applications, web servers, and other resources.`,`route53-failover-types|You configure active-active failover using any routing policy (or combination of routing policies) other than failover, and you configure active-passive failover using the failover routing policy.`]),n(`Prefer data plane operations during failover. AWS divides services into a data plane, responsible for delivering real-time service, and a control plane, used to configure the environment. For maximum resiliency, use only data plane operations as part of your failover operation, because data planes typically have higher availability design goals than control planes.`,`dr-options`,[`The data plane is responsible for delivering real-time service while control planes are used to configure the environment.`,`For maximum resiliency, you should use only data plane operations as part of your failover operation.`,`This is because the data planes typically have higher availability design goals than the control planes.`])],services:[`Amazon Route 53`],design:[n(`Automate the failover path (health check → DNS failover → scale out with Auto Scaling) and test it; for warm standby, the scaled-down environment relies on Auto Scaling to scale out to support production traffic.`,`dr-options`,[`To scale-out the infrastructure to support production traffic, see Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling in the Warm Standby section.`])]},{id:`2.2-S6`,concepts:[n(`Selecting a strategy is a matching exercise on RTO, RPO, cost and the size of the disaster. For a disaster that is only the loss of one data centre in an already highly available workload, backup and restore may suffice. If the definition of a disaster includes a whole Region, or regulation requires it, consider pilot light, warm standby or multi-site active/active.`,`dr-options`,[`For a disaster event based on disruption or loss of one physical data center for a well-architected, highly available workload, you may only require a backup and restore approach to disaster recovery.`,`If your definition of a disaster goes beyond the disruption or loss of a physical data center to that of a Region or if you are subject to regulatory requirements that require it, then you should consider Pilot Light, Warm Standby, or Multi-Site Active/Active.`]),n(`Multi-site active/active is the most complex and costly approach, but can reduce recovery time to near zero for most disasters; data corruption may still need backups, so the recovery point is usually non-zero.`,`dr-options`,[`This approach is the most complex and costly approach to disaster recovery, but it can reduce your recovery time to near zero for most disasters with the correct technology choices and implementation (however data corruption may need to rely on backups, which usually results in a non-zero recovery point).`]),n(`For pilot light, continuous data replication to live databases and data stores in the DR Region is the best approach for a low RPO, used in addition to the point-in-time backups. Continuous replication is near zero for data loss but may not protect against corruption or malicious deletion as well as point-in-time backups.`,`dr-options`,[`For pilot light, continuous data replication to live databases and data stores in the DR region is the best approach for low RPO (when used in addition to the point-in-time backups discussed previously).`,`Continuous replication of data has the advantage of being the shortest time (near zero) to back up your data, but may not protect against disaster events such as data corruption or malicious attack (such as unauthorized data deletion) as well as point-in-time backups.`])],services:[`AWS Backup`,`Amazon Route 53`],design:[n(`Quick selection, following the whitepaper's ordering: lowest cost and complexity → backup and restore; minimal ongoing cost with core infrastructure already in place → pilot light; must serve traffic immediately at reduced capacity → warm standby; near-zero recovery time with the most complexity and cost accepted → multi-site active/active.`,`dr-options`,[`Disaster recovery strategies available to you within AWS can be broadly categorized into four approaches, ranging from the low cost and low complexity of making backups to more complex strategies using multiple active Regions.`,`A pilot light approach minimizes the ongoing cost of disaster recovery by minimizing the active resources, and simplifies recovery at the time of a disaster because the core infrastructure requirements are all in place.`,`The distinction is that pilot light cannot process requests without additional action taken first, whereas warm standby can handle traffic (at reduced capacity levels) immediately.`,`This approach is the most complex and costly approach to disaster recovery, but it can reduce your recovery time to near zero for most disasters with the correct technology choices and implementation (however data corruption may need to rely on backups, which usually results in a non-zero recovery point).`])]}],cues:[e(`lowest cost and complexity, recovery from backups is acceptable`,`Backup and restore`,n(`Backup and restore is at the low-cost, low-complexity end of the DR strategies.`,`dr-options`,[`Disaster recovery strategies available to you within AWS can be broadly categorized into four approaches, ranging from the low cost and low complexity of making backups to more complex strategies using multiple active Regions.`])),e(`core infrastructure ready in another Region but servers switched off`,`Pilot light`,n(`Pilot light replicates data and provisions a copy of the core infrastructure.`,`dr-options`,[`With the pilot light approach, you replicate your data from one Region to another and provision a copy of your core workload infrastructure.`])),e(`scaled-down but fully functional environment that can take traffic immediately`,`Warm standby`,n(`Warm standby can handle traffic at reduced capacity immediately, unlike pilot light.`,`dr-options`,[`The distinction is that pilot light cannot process requests without additional action taken first, whereas warm standby can handle traffic (at reduced capacity levels) immediately.`])),e(`near-zero downtime, all Regions serve traffic`,`Multi-site active/active`,n(`Multi-site active/active serves traffic from all deployed Regions and can reduce recovery time to near zero.`,`dr-options`,[`Multi-site active/active serves traffic from all regions to which it is deployed, whereas hot standby serves traffic only from a single region, and the other Region(s) are only used for disaster recovery.`])),e(`maximum acceptable data loss expressed as time`,`RPO`,n(`RPO is the maximum acceptable amount of time since the last data recovery point.`,`dr-objectives`,[`Recovery Point Objective (RPO) is the maximum acceptable amount of time since the last data recovery point.`])),e(`maximum acceptable downtime`,`RTO`,n(`RTO is the maximum acceptable delay between the interruption of service and restoration of service.`,`dr-objectives`,[`Recovery Time Objective (RTO) is the maximum acceptable delay between the interruption of service and restoration of service.`])),e(`automatically send users to the standby site when the primary fails`,`Route 53 health checks with failover routing`,n(`Active-passive failover uses the failover routing policy.`,`route53-failover-types`,[`You configure active-active failover using any routing policy (or combination of routing policies) other than failover, and you configure active-passive failover using the failover routing policy.`]))],examples:[],confuse:[`dr-strategies`],azure:[{concept:`Azure Backup`,aws:`AWS Backup`,mapping:n(`The Learn storage comparison pairs AWS Backup with Azure Backup, used to back up and recover files, databases, disks and virtual machines.`,[`learn-storage`,`backup-what-is`],[`learn-storage|Azure Backup This option is used to back up and recover files, databases, disks, and virtual machines.`,`backup-what-is|AWS Backup is a fully-managed service that makes it easy to centralize and automate data protection across AWS services, in the cloud, and on premises.`]),breaks:n(`The service and the strategy share a name but are different things. AWS Backup is a service; "backup and restore" is the first of four AWS DR strategies, which range from making backups to more complex strategies using multiple active Regions. The Azure Backup entry in the comparison describes backing up and recovering files, databases, disks and virtual machines, and Learn also says Azure Backup supports backing up compatible on-premises Windows systems.`,[`dr-options`,`learn-storage`],[`dr-options|Disaster recovery strategies available to you within AWS can be broadly categorized into four approaches, ranging from the low cost and low complexity of making backups to more complex strategies using multiple active Regions.`,`learn-storage|Azure Backup This option is used to back up and recover files, databases, disks, and virtual machines.`,`learn-storage|Azure Backup also supports backing up compatible on-premises Windows systems.`],{allow:[`four`]})},{concept:`Azure Site Recovery`,aws:`AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery`,mapping:n(`Azure Site Recovery provides continuous replication, failover and recovery capabilities for virtual machines, supporting an Azure disaster recovery strategy. AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery has a comparable purpose: it replicates on-premises or cloud-based applications so that you can recover them. The Microsoft Learn comparison pages do not list this pairing, so it is drawn from each product's own pages.`,[`learn-azure-asr`,`drs-what-is`],[`learn-azure-asr|Azure Site Recovery (ASR) provides infrastructure resiliency for your business-critical workloads by providing continuous replication, failover, and recovery capabilities.`,`drs-what-is|You can increase IT resilience when you use AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery to replicate on-premises or cloud-based applications running on supported operating systems.`]),breaks:n(`Each product describes itself differently, and the Learn comparison pages do not list either product: Site Recovery protects virtual machines against disruptions ranging from zonal failures to regional outages, and Elastic Disaster Recovery replicates on-premises or cloud-based applications on supported operating systems.`,[`learn-azure-asr`,`drs-what-is`],[`learn-azure-asr|ASR supports your disaster recovery (DR) strategy by protecting virtual machines against planned and unplanned disruptions, ranging from zonal failures to regional outages, while meeting your recovery objectives (RPO and RTO).`,`drs-what-is|You can increase IT resilience when you use AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery to replicate on-premises or cloud-based applications running on supported operating systems.`],{allow:[`two`]})}]},c={building:`durable-dock`,overview:[n(`Storage services protect data in different ways: S3 Standard and the other multi-zone classes store objects across a minimum of three Availability Zones, EFS offers Regional or One Zone file systems, and EBS volumes live in a single Availability Zone, so snapshots and backups matter more there.`,[`s3-durability`,`efs-features`,`ebs-volumes`,`ebs-what-is`],[`s3-durability|S3 Standard, S3 Intelligent-Tiering, S3 Standard-IA, S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval, S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, and S3 Glacier Deep Archive redundantly store objects on multiple devices across a minimum of three Availability Zones in an AWS Region.`,`efs-features|Regional – Regional file systems (recommended) store data redundantly across multiple geographically separated Availability Zones within the same AWS Region.`,`ebs-volumes|The volume and instance must be in the same Availability Zone.`,`ebs-what-is|Additionally, volume data is automatically replicated across multiple servers in an Availability Zone to prevent the loss of data from the failure of any single component.`])],bullets:[{id:`2.2-K11`,concepts:[n(`Amazon S3 provides a highly durable storage infrastructure designed for mission-critical and primary data storage. Most storage classes redundantly store objects on multiple devices across a minimum of three Availability Zones in an AWS Region. S3 is designed for very high durability, and the multi-AZ classes are designed to sustain data through the loss of an entire Availability Zone.`,`s3-durability`,[`Amazon S3 provides a highly durable storage infrastructure designed for mission-critical and primary data storage.`,`S3 Standard, S3 Intelligent-Tiering, S3 Standard-IA, S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval, S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, and S3 Glacier Deep Archive redundantly store objects on multiple devices across a minimum of three Availability Zones in an AWS Region.`,`S3 Standard, S3 Intelligent-Tiering, S3 Standard-IA, S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval, S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, and S3 Glacier Deep Archive are all designed to sustain data in the event of the loss of an entire Amazon S3 Availability Zone.`]),n(`A single-zone S3 class is different: with S3 Express One Zone your data is redundantly stored on multiple devices within a single Availability Zone, so unlike the multi-AZ classes it does not spread data across zones.`,`s3-storage-classes`,[`With S3 Express One Zone, your data is redundantly stored on multiple devices within a single Availability Zone.`],{newer:[`s3-express-one-zone`]}),n(`Amazon EBS volumes are durable, block-level storage devices that you attach to instances, and the volume and instance must be in the same Availability Zone. How durable a volume is depends on the volume type: io2 Block Express volumes have the highest durability, and other volume types are less durable, according to AWS. Snapshots are point-in-time backups that persist independently from the volume.`,[`ebs-volumes`,`ebs-what-is`],[`ebs-volumes|An Amazon EBS volume is a durable, block-level storage device that you can attach to your instances.`,`ebs-volumes|The volume and instance must be in the same Availability Zone.`,`ebs-what-is|Data availability and durability — io2 Block Express volumes provide 99.999% durability with an annual failure rate of 0.001%.`,`ebs-what-is|Other volume types provide 99.8% to 99.9% durability with an annual failure rate of 0.1% to 0.2%.`,`ebs-what-is|Amazon EBS snapshots — These are point-in-time backups of Amazon EBS volumes that persist independently from the volume itself.`]),n(`Amazon EFS has two file system types: Regional file systems (recommended) store data redundantly across multiple geographically separated Availability Zones within the same Region, and One Zone file systems store data within a single Availability Zone. For added protection EFS automatically backs up One Zone file systems with AWS Backup.`,`efs-features`,[`Regional – Regional file systems (recommended) store data redundantly across multiple geographically separated Availability Zones within the same AWS Region.`,`One Zone – One Zone file systems store data within a single Availability Zone.`,`For added data protection, Amazon EFS automatically backs up One Zone file systems with AWS Backup.`],{allow:[`two`]}),n(`Replication copies data to a second location: S3 replication can have destination buckets in a different Region or the same Region as the source bucket, and EFS replication automatically and transparently replicates data and metadata to a destination file system, letting you fail over in a disaster or game-day exercise.`,[`s3-replication`,`efs-replication`],[`s3-replication|The destination buckets can be in different AWS Regions or within the same Region as the source bucket.`,`efs-replication|When you enable replication on an EFS file system, Amazon EFS automatically and transparently replicates the data and metadata on the source file system to a destination file system.`,`efs-replication|In the event of a disaster or when performing game day exercises, you can fail over to your replica file system.`])],services:[`Amazon S3`,`Amazon EBS`,`Amazon EFS`],design:[n(`Read the failure the question describes: loss of one AZ → multi-AZ storage (S3 classes spread across AZs, EFS Regional) or a copy elsewhere; loss of a Region → cross-Region replication or copied backups; accidental deletion or corruption → versioning, snapshots and backups, because replication also copies bad changes.`,[`s3-durability`,`dr-options`],[`s3-durability|With versioning, you can easily recover from both unintended user actions and application failures.`,`dr-options|Continuous replication of data has the advantage of being the shortest time (near zero) to back up your data, but may not protect against disaster events such as data corruption or malicious attack (such as unauthorized data deletion) as well as point-in-time backups.`])]},{id:`2.2-S5`,concepts:[n(`S3 versioning preserves, retrieves and restores every version of every object in a bucket, which helps you recover from both unintended user actions and application failures.`,`s3-durability`,[`You can use versioning to preserve, retrieve, and restore every version of every object that is stored in your Amazon S3 bucket.`,`With versioning, you can easily recover from both unintended user actions and application failures.`]),n(`EBS snapshots back up volumes at a point in time, independently of the volume. AWS Backup centralises and automates data protection across AWS services using backup plans (policies) that you apply to the resources you want to protect.`,[`ebs-what-is`,`backup-what-is`],[`ebs-what-is|You can create snapshots to back up the data on your Amazon EBS volumes.`,`backup-what-is|With AWS Backup, you can create backup policies known as backup plans.`,`backup-what-is|Use these backup plans to define your backup requirements and then apply them to the AWS resources that you want to protect across the AWS services that you use.`]),n(`AWS Backup can copy backups to multiple different AWS Regions on demand or automatically as part of a scheduled backup plan, which is valuable when you must keep backups a minimum distance away from production data.`,`backup-what-is`,[`Using AWS Backup, you can copy backups to multiple different AWS Regions on demand or automatically as part of a scheduled backup plan.`,`Cross-Region backup is particularly valuable if you have business continuity or compliance requirements to store backups a minimum distance away from your production data.`]),n(`Backup and restore is a suitable approach for mitigating data loss or corruption. S3 Object Lock stores objects with write-once-read-many (WORM) protection; the immutability options are covered in Archive Annex, and the disaster recovery strategies that use these backups are in Disaster Bunker.`,[`dr-options`,`s3-object-lock`],[`dr-options|Backup and restore is a suitable approach for mitigating against data loss or corruption.`,`s3-object-lock|Object Lock uses a write-once-read-many (WORM) model to store objects.`])],services:[`AWS Backup`,`Amazon S3`,`Amazon EBS`,`Amazon EFS`],design:[n(`Typical answer: protect against deletion with versioning and backups, against AZ loss with multi-AZ or replicated data, and against Region loss with cross-Region replication or cross-Region backup copies, then match the choice to the stated RPO.`,[`dr-options`,`backup-what-is`],[`dr-options|How often you run your backup will determine your achievable recovery point (which should align to meet your RPO).`,`backup-what-is|Using AWS Backup, you can copy backups to multiple different AWS Regions on demand or automatically as part of a scheduled backup plan.`])]}],disagreements:[n(`Two Microsoft Learn pages describe Azure geo-redundant storage (GRS) differently. The comparison page says GRS and GZRS replicate data synchronously to a secondary region; the Azure Storage redundancy page says the data is copied synchronously within the primary region and then asynchronously to the secondary region. The notes above follow the dedicated redundancy page, and no question depends on the difference.`,[`learn-storage`,`learn-storage-redundancy`],[`learn-storage|GRS and GZRS synchronously replicate data to a secondary region without requiring a replication configuration.`,`learn-storage-redundancy|It then copies your data asynchronously to the secondary region.`],{disputed:`c-azure-grs-sync-async`,allow:[`two`]})],cues:[e(`objects must survive the loss of an Availability Zone`,`S3 Standard or other multi-AZ classes (not One Zone)`,n(`Most S3 classes store objects across a minimum of three Availability Zones.`,`s3-durability`,[`S3 Standard, S3 Intelligent-Tiering, S3 Standard-IA, S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval, S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, and S3 Glacier Deep Archive redundantly store objects on multiple devices across a minimum of three Availability Zones in an AWS Region.`])),e(`shared file system must survive an AZ failure`,`EFS Regional file system`,n(`Regional EFS file systems store data redundantly across multiple Availability Zones.`,`efs-features`,[`Regional – Regional file systems (recommended) store data redundantly across multiple geographically separated Availability Zones within the same AWS Region.`])),e(`recover from accidental overwrite or deletion of objects`,`S3 versioning`,n(`Versioning lets you restore every version of every object.`,`s3-durability`,[`With versioning, you can easily recover from both unintended user actions and application failures.`])),e(`point-in-time backup of an EBS volume`,`EBS snapshot`,n(`Snapshots are point-in-time backups that persist independently from the volume.`,`ebs-what-is`,[`Amazon EBS snapshots — These are point-in-time backups of Amazon EBS volumes that persist independently from the volume itself.`])),e(`centrally schedule and enforce backups across services, copy to another Region`,`AWS Backup (backup plans)`,n(`Backup plans define backup requirements and apply to resources across services; backups can be copied to other Regions.`,`backup-what-is`,[`Use these backup plans to define your backup requirements and then apply them to the AWS resources that you want to protect across the AWS services that you use.`,`Using AWS Backup, you can copy backups to multiple different AWS Regions on demand or automatically as part of a scheduled backup plan.`])),e(`copy data to another Region continuously`,`S3 cross-Region replication / EFS replication`,n(`S3 replication destinations can be in a different Region; EFS replicates to a destination file system.`,[`s3-replication`,`efs-replication`],[`s3-replication|The destination buckets can be in different AWS Regions or within the same Region as the source bucket.`,`efs-replication|When you enable replication on an EFS file system, Amazon EFS automatically and transparently replicates the data and metadata on the source file system to a destination file system.`]))],examples:[],confuse:[],azure:[{concept:`Azure storage redundancy (LRS, ZRS, GRS)`,aws:`Amazon S3 storage classes, EFS Regional and One Zone, and replication`,mapping:n(`Azure Storage offers locally redundant storage (LRS), which replicates data to a single physical datacenter in the primary region, and zone-redundant storage (ZRS), which copies data synchronously across three or more availability zones. For comparison, most S3 storage classes store objects across a minimum of three Availability Zones in an AWS Region.`,[`learn-storage-redundancy`,`s3-durability`],[`learn-storage-redundancy|Locally redundant storage (LRS) replicates the data within your storage accounts to a single physical datacenter located in the primary region of your choice.`,`learn-storage-redundancy|Zone-redundant storage (ZRS) copies your data synchronously across three or more Azure availability zones in the primary region.`,`s3-durability|S3 Standard, S3 Intelligent-Tiering, S3 Standard-IA, S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval, S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, and S3 Glacier Deep Archive redundantly store objects on multiple devices across a minimum of three Availability Zones in an AWS Region.`]),breaks:n(`Two things differ. First, on Azure you control how data is replicated by choosing locally or zone-redundant storage, while on AWS the multi-AZ storage is built into most S3 classes, and you choose between S3 classes, EFS file system types and EBS volumes. EBS volumes live in a single Availability Zone, and so do EFS One Zone file systems, while EFS Regional file systems store data across multiple Availability Zones. Second, Azure geo-redundant storage copies data to a secondary region asynchronously as part of the redundancy option, whereas on AWS you set up cross-Region replication or cross-Region backup copies yourself.`,[`learn-storage-redundancy`,`learn-storage`,`s3-durability`,`efs-features`,`ebs-what-is`,`ebs-volumes`,`s3-replication`,`backup-what-is`],[`learn-storage-redundancy|It then copies your data asynchronously to the secondary region.`,`learn-storage|On Azure, you control how data is replicated by using locally redundant storage (LRS) or zone-redundant storage (ZRS).`,`s3-durability|S3 Standard, S3 Intelligent-Tiering, S3 Standard-IA, S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval, S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, and S3 Glacier Deep Archive redundantly store objects on multiple devices across a minimum of three Availability Zones in an AWS Region.`,`efs-features|One Zone – One Zone file systems store data within a single Availability Zone.`,`efs-features|Regional – Regional file systems (recommended) store data redundantly across multiple geographically separated Availability Zones within the same AWS Region.`,`ebs-what-is|Additionally, volume data is automatically replicated across multiple servers in an Availability Zone to prevent the loss of data from the failure of any single component.`,`ebs-volumes|The volume and instance must be in the same Availability Zone.`,`s3-replication|The destination buckets can be in different AWS Regions or within the same Region as the source bucket.`,`backup-what-is|Using AWS Backup, you can copy backups to multiple different AWS Regions on demand or automatically as part of a scheduled backup plan.`],{allow:[`two`]})}]},l={building:`island-charts`,overview:[n(`Resilient designs start from AWS geography: {{term:availability-zone|Availability Zones}} are physically separated locations inside a {{term:region}}, so spreading a workload across them avoids correlated failures; spreading across Regions protects against a whole-Region problem and is also part of disaster recovery. Amazon Route 53 is the DNS service whose routing policies and health checks steer users to endpoints, and route tables decide where traffic goes inside the network.`,[`waf-multi-location`,`route53-what-is`,`vpc-route-tables`],[`waf-multi-location|Availability Zones are resource hosting locations that are physically separated from each other to avoid correlated failures due to risks such as fires, floods, and tornadoes.`,`waf-multi-location|Distribute workload data and resources across multiple Availability Zones or, where necessary, across AWS Regions.`,`route53-what-is|Amazon Route 53 is a highly available and scalable Domain Name System (DNS) web service.`,`vpc-route-tables|Each route table contains a set of rules, called routes, that determine where network traffic from your subnet or gateway is directed.`])],bullets:[{id:`2.2-K1`,concepts:[n(`Production workloads should be distributed among multiple Availability Zones or Regions to achieve fault tolerance and high availability. All compute associated with your workload should be distributed among multiple Availability Zones, and you can replicate data synchronously between Availability Zones for most workloads without significantly impacting user experience.`,`waf-multi-location`,[`Desired outcome: Production workloads are distributed among multiple Availability Zones (AZs) or Regions to achieve fault tolerance and high availability.`,`All compute associated with your workload should be distributed among multiple Availability Zones.`,`You can replicate data synchronously between Availability Zones for most workloads without significantly impacting user experience.`]),n(`Whether to stay in one Region (multi-AZ) or go multi-Region is a requirements decision: evaluate whether resilience needs can be met by a multi-AZ approach or require a multi-Region approach, and consider multiple Regions as part of the disaster recovery strategy even if you do not run active in more than one.`,`waf-multi-location`,[`Work with business and technical stakeholders to evaluate your workload, and determine whether its resilience needs can be met by a multi-AZ approach (single AWS Region) or if they require a multi-Region approach (if multiple Regions are permitted).`,`Even if you choose not to operate in multiple Regions for high availability, consider multiple Regions as part of your disaster recovery (DR) strategy.`]),n(`Amazon Route 53 is a highly available and scalable DNS web service that can perform three main functions in any combination: domain registration, DNS routing and health checking.`,`route53-what-is`,[`Amazon Route 53 is a highly available and scalable Domain Name System (DNS) web service.`,`You can use Route 53 to perform three main functions in any combination: domain registration, DNS routing, and health checking.`]),n(`When you create a record you choose a routing policy, which determines how Route 53 responds to queries: simple (a single resource), failover (active-passive), geolocation (by user location), geoproximity (by resource location, with optional shifting), latency (to the Region with the best latency), IP-based, multivalue answer (up to eight healthy records chosen at random) and weighted (in proportions you specify).`,`route53-routing`,[`When you create a record, you choose a routing policy, which determines how Amazon Route 53 responds to queries: Simple routing policy – Use for a single resource that performs a given function for your domain, for example, a web server that serves content for the example.com website.`,`Failover routing policy – Use when you want to configure active-passive failover.`,`Geolocation routing policy – Use when you want to route traffic based on the location of your users.`,`Geoproximity routing policy – Use when you want to route traffic based on the location of your resources and, optionally, shift traffic from resources in one location to resources in another location.`,`Latency routing policy – Use when you have resources in multiple AWS Regions and you want to route traffic to the Region that provides the best latency.`,`Multivalue answer routing policy – Use when you want Route 53 to respond to DNS queries with up to eight healthy records selected at random.`,`Weighted routing policy – Use to route traffic to multiple resources in proportions that you specify.`],{allow:[`eight`]}),n(`Geolocation records need a fallback. You can create a default record that handles both queries from unmapped IP addresses and queries from locations where you have not created geolocation records; without one, Route 53 returns a "no answer" response for queries from those locations.`,`route53-geo`,[`You can create a default record that handles both queries from unmapped IP addresses and queries from locations where you haven't created geolocation records.`,`If you don't create a default record, Route 53 returns a "no answer" response for queries from those locations.`]),n(`Alias records are a Route 53-specific extension to DNS that route traffic to selected AWS resources, such as CloudFront distributions and S3 buckets. Unlike a CNAME record, you can create an alias record at the zone apex (the top node of a DNS namespace): you cannot create a CNAME record for example.com, but you can create an alias record for example.com.`,`route53-alias`,[`Amazon Route 53 alias records provide a Route 53–specific extension to DNS.`,`Alias records let you route traffic to selected AWS resources, such as CloudFront distributions and Amazon S3 buckets.`,`Unlike a CNAME record, you can create an alias record at the top node of a DNS namespace, also known as the zone apex.`,`You can't create a CNAME record for example.com, but you can create an alias record for example.com that routes traffic to www.example.com`]),n(`Route 53 health checks monitor the health and performance of web applications, web servers and other resources, and enable failover. Active-passive failover uses the failover routing policy; active-active failover uses any other routing policy (or a combination) so that all resources are available most of the time unless Route 53 considers them unhealthy.`,[`route53-failover`,`route53-failover-types`],[`route53-failover|Amazon Route 53 health checks monitor the health and performance of your web applications, web servers, and other resources.`,`route53-failover-types|You configure active-active failover using any routing policy (or combination of routing policies) other than failover, and you configure active-passive failover using the failover routing policy.`,`route53-failover-types|In active-active failover, all the records that have the same name, the same type (such as A or AAAA), and the same routing policy (such as weighted or latency) are active unless Route 53 considers them unhealthy.`])],services:[`Amazon Route 53`,`Elastic Load Balancing (ELB)`],design:[n(`Choose the routing policy from the requirement: the Region with the best latency → latency; the location of your users → geolocation; the location of your resources, with optional shifting → geoproximity; primary and standby → failover; proportions that you specify → weighted; and for a name at the zone apex that points at an AWS resource, use an alias record, which routes traffic to selected AWS resources and is chosen separately from the routing policy.`,[`route53-routing`,`route53-alias`],[`route53-routing|Latency routing policy – Use when you have resources in multiple AWS Regions and you want to route traffic to the Region that provides the best latency.`,`route53-routing|Geolocation routing policy – Use when you want to route traffic based on the location of your users.`,`route53-routing|Geoproximity routing policy – Use when you want to route traffic based on the location of your resources and, optionally, shift traffic from resources in one location to resources in another location.`,`route53-routing|Failover routing policy – Use when you want to configure active-passive failover.`,`route53-routing|Weighted routing policy – Use to route traffic to multiple resources in proportions that you specify.`,`route53-alias|Unlike a CNAME record, you can create an alias record at the top node of a DNS namespace, also known as the zone apex.`,`route53-alias|Alias records let you route traffic to selected AWS resources, such as CloudFront distributions and Amazon S3 buckets.`])]},{id:`2.2-K3`,concepts:[n(`Each route table contains a set of rules, called routes, that determine where network traffic from your subnet or gateway is directed. A route specifies a destination (a CIDR block or prefix list) and a target such as an internet gateway, NAT gateway, VPC peering connection or VPN connection; traffic is routed based on its destination IP address.`,`vpc-route-tables`,[`Each route table contains a set of rules, called routes, that determine where network traffic from your subnet or gateway is directed.`,`Each route specifies a destination (CIDR block or prefix list) and a target (such as an internet gateway, NAT gateway, VPC peering connection, or VPN connection).`,`Traffic is routed to targets based on its destination IP address.`]),n(`When you create a VPC, a main route table is created for it. When more than one route matches, AWS directs traffic using the most specific route that matches the traffic.`,[`vpc-route-tables`,`vpc-route-priority`],[`vpc-route-tables|When you create a VPC, we also create the main route table for the VPC.`,`vpc-route-priority|In general, we direct traffic using the most specific route that matches the traffic.`]),n(`A public subnet is one with a direct route to an internet gateway. Resources in a public subnet can reach the public internet. Route tables are what make a subnet public, private, VPN-only or isolated.`,[`vpc-subnets`,`vpc-route-tables`],[`vpc-subnets|Public subnet – The subnet has a direct route to an internet gateway.`,`vpc-subnets|Resources in a public subnet can access the public internet.`,`vpc-route-tables|Route tables enable you to create complex networking architectures that include public subnets, private subnets, VPN-only subnets, and isolated subnets.`])],services:[`Amazon VPC`],design:[n(`When an instance in a subnet cannot reach the internet, check the route table: does the subnet's route table have a route to an internet gateway (public subnet), or to a NAT gateway for outbound access from a private subnet? Routing is only part of it. An instance in a public subnet also needs a public IP address or Elastic IP address, a public NAT gateway sits in a public subnet and needs an Elastic IP address, and security groups and network ACLs also filter the traffic (see Gatehouse).`,[`vpc-route-tables`,`vpc-igw`,`vpc-nat-gateway`],[`vpc-route-tables|Each route specifies a destination (CIDR block or prefix list) and a target (such as an internet gateway, NAT gateway, VPC peering connection, or VPN connection).`,`vpc-igw|The instances in the public subnet must have public IP addresses or Elastic IP addresses to enable communication with the internet over the internet gateway.`,`vpc-nat-gateway|You create a public NAT gateway in a public subnet and must associate an Elastic IP address with the NAT gateway at creation.`,`vpc-nat-gateway|You can use a NAT gateway so that instances in a private subnet can connect to services outside your VPC but external services can't initiate a connection with those instances.`])]},{id:`2.2-S2`,concepts:[n(`Within one Region, a highly available design spreads instances across Availability Zones: an Auto Scaling group can span multiple Availability Zones and balances instances evenly across them as it scales, and a load balancer distributes traffic to healthy targets in one or more Availability Zones.`,[`asg-what-is`,`elb-what-is`],[`asg-what-is|You can specify multiple Availability Zones for your Auto Scaling group, and Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling balances your instances evenly across the Availability Zones as the group scales.`,`elb-what-is|Elastic Load Balancing automatically distributes your incoming traffic across multiple targets, such as EC2 instances, containers, and IP addresses, in one or more Availability Zones.`]),n(`Across Regions, Route 53 provides the routing: health checks and DNS failover or latency routing send users to a healthy Region. To ease multi-Region deployments and keep consistency, AWS CloudFormation StackSets can replicate infrastructure across multiple Regions.`,[`route53-failover-types`,`waf-multi-location`],[`route53-failover-types|You can use Route 53 health checking to configure active-active and active-passive failover configurations.`,`waf-multi-location|To ease multi-Regional deployments and maintain consistency, AWS CloudFormation StackSets can replicate your entire AWS infrastructure across multiple Regions.`]),n(`Fault isolation: common points of failure such as generators and cooling equipment are not shared across Availability Zones.`,`fault-isolation-az`,[`Common points of failure, like generators and cooling equipment, are not shared across Availability Zones and are designed to be supplied by different power substations.`])],services:[`Amazon Route 53`,`Amazon VPC`,`Elastic Load Balancing (ELB)`],design:[n(`Pattern: a load balancer and an Auto Scaling group across multiple Availability Zones within a Region for high availability; Route 53 health checks with failover or latency routing in front of a second Region when the requirement is Region-level fault tolerance or global latency.`,[`waf-multi-location`,`route53-routing`,`asg-what-is`,`elb-what-is`,`route53-failover-types`],[`waf-multi-location|Desired outcome: Production workloads are distributed among multiple Availability Zones (AZs) or Regions to achieve fault tolerance and high availability.`,`waf-multi-location|Distribute workload data and resources across multiple Availability Zones or, where necessary, across AWS Regions.`,`asg-what-is|You can specify multiple Availability Zones for your Auto Scaling group, and Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling balances your instances evenly across the Availability Zones as the group scales.`,`elb-what-is|Elastic Load Balancing automatically distributes your incoming traffic across multiple targets, such as EC2 instances, containers, and IP addresses, in one or more Availability Zones.`,`route53-failover-types|You can use Route 53 health checking to configure active-active and active-passive failover configurations.`,`route53-routing|Latency routing policy – Use when you have resources in multiple AWS Regions and you want to route traffic to the Region that provides the best latency.`])]}],cues:[e(`survive the failure of one data centre / Availability Zone`,`Deploy across multiple Availability Zones (load balancer + Auto Scaling group)`,n(`Production workloads should be distributed among multiple Availability Zones to achieve fault tolerance and high availability.`,`waf-multi-location`,[`Desired outcome: Production workloads are distributed among multiple Availability Zones (AZs) or Regions to achieve fault tolerance and high availability.`])),e(`route users to the Region with the lowest latency`,`Route 53 latency routing`,n(`Latency routing sends traffic to the Region that provides the best latency.`,`route53-routing`,[`Latency routing policy – Use when you have resources in multiple AWS Regions and you want to route traffic to the Region that provides the best latency.`])),e(`primary site with a standby that takes over on failure`,`Route 53 failover routing (active-passive) with health checks`,n(`Failover routing is for active-passive failover.`,`route53-routing`,[`Failover routing policy – Use when you want to configure active-passive failover.`])),e(`route by the user's country or location`,`Route 53 geolocation routing (with a default record for unmapped locations)`,n(`Geolocation routing is based on the location of your users; a default record handles queries from locations without a geolocation record.`,[`route53-routing`,`route53-geo`],[`route53-routing|Geolocation routing policy – Use when you want to route traffic based on the location of your users.`,`route53-geo|If you don't create a default record, Route 53 returns a "no answer" response for queries from those locations.`])),e(`apex / root domain name (example.com) must point at an AWS resource such as CloudFront`,`Route 53 alias record (a CNAME cannot be used at the zone apex)`,n(`You can create an alias record at the zone apex; you cannot create a CNAME record there.`,`route53-alias`,[`You can't create a CNAME record for example.com, but you can create an alias record for example.com that routes traffic to www.example.com`])),e(`send specified proportions of traffic to different resources`,`Route 53 weighted routing`,n(`Weighted routing sends traffic to multiple resources in proportions that you specify.`,`route53-routing`,[`Weighted routing policy – Use to route traffic to multiple resources in proportions that you specify.`])),e(`subnet has a route to an internet gateway`,`Public subnet`,n(`A public subnet has a direct route to an internet gateway.`,`vpc-subnets`,[`Public subnet – The subnet has a direct route to an internet gateway.`]))],examples:[],confuse:[],azure:[{concept:`Azure DNS and Azure Traffic Manager`,aws:`Amazon Route 53`,mapping:n(`Learn explains that in AWS, Route 53 provides both DNS name management and DNS-level traffic routing and failover, while Azure splits these across two services: Azure DNS for domain and DNS management and Traffic Manager for DNS-level traffic routing, load balancing and failover.`,[`learn-networking`,`route53-what-is`],[`learn-networking|Route 53, Azure DNS, and Azure Traffic Manager In AWS, Route 53 provides both DNS name management and DNS-level traffic routing and failover services.`,`learn-networking|Traffic Manager provides DNS-level traffic routing, load balancing, and failover capabilities.`,`route53-what-is|You can use Route 53 to perform three main functions in any combination: domain registration, DNS routing, and health checking.`],{allow:[`two`]}),breaks:n(`On AWS one service covers both, and you choose a routing policy per record (simple, failover, geolocation, geoproximity, latency, IP-based, multivalue, weighted). On the Azure side, Microsoft Learn lists the DNS-level routing, load balancing and failover under the separate Traffic Manager service.`,[`route53-routing`,`learn-networking`],[`route53-routing|When you create a record, you choose a routing policy, which determines how Amazon Route 53 responds to queries:`,`learn-networking|Route 53, Azure DNS, and Azure Traffic Manager In AWS, Route 53 provides both DNS name management and DNS-level traffic routing and failover services.`,`learn-networking|Traffic Manager provides DNS-level traffic routing, load balancing, and failover capabilities.`])}]},u={building:`legacy-wharf`,overview:[n(`Pre-built AWS AI services such as Amazon Comprehend and Amazon Polly give an application a capability through an API, using pre-trained models. This building also covers reliability features for keeping an application available: load balancing with sticky sessions, automatic instance recovery, Multi-AZ databases and DNS failover.`,[`comprehend-what-is`,`polly-what-is`,`alb-target-attrs`,`ec2-recover`,`rds-multi-az-instance`,`route53-failover`],[`comprehend-what-is|You can use the pre-trained models that Amazon Comprehend provides, or you can train your own custom models for classification and entity recognition.`,`comprehend-what-is|You can access Amazon Comprehend document analysis capabilities using the Amazon Comprehend console or using the Amazon Comprehend APIs.`,`polly-what-is|Amazon Polly is a cloud service that converts text into lifelike speech.`,`alb-target-attrs|This feature is useful for servers that maintain state information in order to provide a continuous experience to clients.`,`ec2-recover|These recovery mechanisms are designed to restore instance availability when AWS detects an underlying hardware or software issue that causes a system status check to fail.`,`rds-multi-az-instance|In a Multi-AZ DB instance deployment, Amazon RDS automatically provisions and maintains a synchronous standby replica in a different Availability Zone.`,`route53-failover|Configuring DNS failover If you have multiple resources that perform the same function, you can configure DNS failover so that Route 53 routes your traffic from an unhealthy resource to a healthy resource.`])],bullets:[{id:`2.2-K2`,concepts:[n(`Amazon Comprehend uses natural language processing (NLP) to extract insights about the content of documents. You can use the pre-trained models it provides or train custom models for classification and entity recognition, and access it through the console or APIs.`,`comprehend-what-is`,[`Amazon Comprehend uses natural language processing (NLP) to extract insights about the content of documents.`,`You can use the pre-trained models that Amazon Comprehend provides, or you can train your own custom models for classification and entity recognition.`,`You can access Amazon Comprehend document analysis capabilities using the Amazon Comprehend console or using the Amazon Comprehend APIs.`]),n(`Amazon Polly is a cloud service that converts text into lifelike speech. It supports multiple languages and includes a variety of lifelike voices, and can be used to develop applications that increase engagement and accessibility.`,`polly-what-is`,[`Amazon Polly is a cloud service that converts text into lifelike speech.`,`Amazon Polly supports multiple languages and includes a variety of lifelike voices.`,`You can use Amazon Polly to develop applications that increase engagement and accessibility.`]),n(`{{rename:sagemaker-ai}} is the managed service for building, training and deploying your own machine learning models, in contrast to calling a pre-trained service like Comprehend or Polly.`,`sagemaker-what-is`,[`Amazon SageMaker AI is a fully managed machine learning (ML) service.`]),n(`Other pre-built AI services cover other inputs. Amazon Rekognition analyses images and video. Amazon Textract detects text, forms and tables in documents. Amazon Transcribe converts audio to text. Amazon Translate translates text. Amazon Lex builds conversational interfaces using voice and text.`,[`rekognition-what-is`,`textract-what-is`,`transcribe-what-is`,`translate-what-is`,`lex-what-is`],[`rekognition-what-is|Amazon Rekognition is a cloud-based image and video analysis service that makes it easy to add advanced computer vision capabilities to your applications.`,`textract-what-is|Amazon Textract helps you add document text detection and analysis to your applications.`,`textract-what-is|Extract text, forms, and tables from documents with structured data, using the Amazon Textract Document Analysis API.`,`transcribe-what-is|Amazon Transcribe is an automatic speech recognition service that uses machine learning models to convert audio to text.`,`translate-what-is|Amazon Translate is a text translation service that uses advanced machine learning technologies to provide high-quality translation on demand.`,`lex-what-is|Amazon Lex V2 is an AWS service for building conversational interfaces for applications using voice and text.`])],services:[`Amazon Comprehend`,`Amazon Polly`,`Amazon Rekognition`,`Amazon Textract`,`Amazon Transcribe`,`Amazon Translate`,`Amazon Lex`],design:[n(`Match the task to the managed AI service: extract entities, sentiment or key phrases from text → Comprehend; read text aloud → Polly; analyse images or video → Rekognition; pull text, forms and tables out of documents → Textract; speech to text → Transcribe; translate text → Translate; chatbot → Lex. Calling a service like these avoids building and hosting a model yourself.`,[`comprehend-what-is`,`polly-what-is`,`rekognition-what-is`,`textract-what-is`,`transcribe-what-is`,`translate-what-is`,`lex-what-is`],[`comprehend-what-is|For example, using Amazon Comprehend you can search social networking feeds for mentions of products or scan an entire document repository for key phrases.`,`polly-what-is|Amazon Polly is a cloud service that converts text into lifelike speech.`,`rekognition-what-is|Amazon Rekognition is a cloud-based image and video analysis service that makes it easy to add advanced computer vision capabilities to your applications.`,`textract-what-is|Extract text, forms, and tables from documents with structured data, using the Amazon Textract Document Analysis API.`,`transcribe-what-is|Amazon Transcribe is an automatic speech recognition service that uses machine learning models to convert audio to text.`,`translate-what-is|Amazon Translate is a text translation service that uses advanced machine learning technologies to provide high-quality translation on demand.`,`lex-what-is|Amazon Lex V2 is an AWS service for building conversational interfaces for applications using voice and text.`])]},{id:`2.2-S7`,concepts:[n(`{{term:sticky-sessions|Sticky sessions}} let an Application Load Balancer bind a user's {{term:session}} to a specific target so all requests in the session go to the same target. AWS says this is useful for servers that maintain state information. It needs client cookie support, and is enabled at the target group level.`,`alb-target-attrs`,[`you can use the sticky session feature (also known as session affinity) to enable the load balancer to bind a user's session to a specific target.`,`This feature is useful for servers that maintain state information in order to provide a continuous experience to clients.`,`To use sticky sessions, the client must support cookies.`,`Sticky sessions are enabled at the target group level.`]),n(`Automatic instance recovery restores availability when AWS detects an underlying hardware or software issue that fails a system status check, by moving the instance to a different host. The instance keeps its instance ID, IP addresses (including Elastic IP), instance metadata, attached EBS volumes and Availability Zone, but data in memory (and in instance store volumes for CloudWatch action based recovery) is lost.`,`ec2-recover`,[`These recovery mechanisms are designed to restore instance availability when AWS detects an underlying hardware or software issue that causes a system status check to fail.`,`During the instance recovery process, AWS will attempt to move your instance from the host with the underlying hardware or software issue to a different host.`,`Preserved elements: Instance ID Public, private, and Elastic IP addresses Instance metadata Placement group Attached EBS volumes Availability Zone Lost elements: Data stored in volatile memory (RAM) Data stored on instance store volumes (applicable to CloudWatch action based recovery only) Operating system uptime resets to zero`]),n(`A Multi-AZ RDS deployment gives a database high availability and failover support: RDS automatically provisions and maintains a synchronous standby replica in a different Availability Zone.`,`rds-multi-az-instance`,[`In a Multi-AZ DB instance deployment, Amazon RDS automatically provisions and maintains a synchronous standby replica in a different Availability Zone.`,`Amazon RDS provides high availability and failover support for DB instances using Multi-AZ deployments with a single standby DB instance.`]),n(`A load balancer plus an Auto Scaling group spreads traffic and replaces failed instances, and Route 53 failover with health checks can route traffic from an unhealthy resource to a healthy one.`,[`elb-what-is`,`asg-what-is`,`route53-failover`],[`elb-what-is|Elastic Load Balancing automatically distributes your incoming traffic across multiple targets, such as EC2 instances, containers, and IP addresses, in one or more Availability Zones.`,`asg-what-is|Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling automatically monitors the health and availability of your instances using EC2 health checks and replaces terminated or impaired instances to maintain your desired capacity.`,`route53-failover|Configuring DNS failover If you have multiple resources that perform the same function, you can configure DNS failover so that Route 53 routes your traffic from an unhealthy resource to a healthy resource.`])],services:[`Elastic Load Balancing (ELB)`,`Amazon Route 53`,`Amazon RDS`],design:[n(`When a scenario says the application cannot be changed, infrastructure-level options fit that constraint: Multi-AZ for the database, a load balancer (with sticky sessions if the app keeps session state), Auto Scaling or automatic instance recovery for the servers, and Route 53 failover for the site. An option that requires rewriting the application does not meet that constraint. When changes are allowed, externalising state is the stronger long-term fix (see Blueprint Hall).`,[`alb-target-attrs`,`rds-multi-az-instance`,`asg-what-is`,`ec2-recover`,`route53-failover-types`],[`alb-target-attrs|This ensures that all requests from the user during the session are sent to the same target.`,`rds-multi-az-instance|In a Multi-AZ DB instance deployment, Amazon RDS automatically provisions and maintains a synchronous standby replica in a different Availability Zone.`,`asg-what-is|Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling automatically monitors the health and availability of your instances using EC2 health checks and replaces terminated or impaired instances to maintain your desired capacity.`,`ec2-recover|These recovery mechanisms are designed to restore instance availability when AWS detects an underlying hardware or software issue that causes a system status check to fail.`,`route53-failover-types|Use an active-passive failover configuration when you want a primary resource or group of resources to be available the majority of the time and you want a secondary resource or group of resources to be on standby in case all the primary resources become unavailable.`])]},{id:`2.2-S8`,concepts:[n(`Use purpose-built services for the workload: AWS offers a growing number of database options with diverse data models. See Purpose-Built Workshop for the database matching.`,`database-decision`,[`AWS offers a growing number of database options (15+) with diverse data models to support a variety of workloads.`,`This might involve migrating your applications to use purpose-built cloud databases.`]),n(`Examples from this building: text understanding → Comprehend; speech output → Polly; a managed relational database that needs to survive an AZ failure → RDS Multi-AZ.`,[`comprehend-what-is`,`polly-what-is`,`rds-multi-az-instance`],[`comprehend-what-is|You can use the pre-trained models that Amazon Comprehend provides, or you can train your own custom models for classification and entity recognition.`,`polly-what-is|Amazon Polly supports multiple languages and includes a variety of lifelike voices.`,`rds-multi-az-instance|Amazon RDS provides high availability and failover support for DB instances using Multi-AZ deployments with a single standby DB instance.`])],services:[`Amazon Comprehend`,`Amazon Polly`,`Amazon RDS`],design:[n(`Rule of thumb for scenario questions: when a purpose-built AWS service exists for the task, such as a purpose-built NoSQL database like Amazon DynamoDB, consider it for the design.`,`database-decision`,[`For example, you could use Amazon Aurora or purpose-built NoSQL databases such as Amazon DynamoDB, Amazon Neptune, or Amazon DocumentDB (with MongoDB compatibility).`])]}],cues:[e(`extract sentiment, entities or key phrases from text without building a model`,`Amazon Comprehend`,n(`Comprehend uses NLP to extract insights from documents with pre-trained models.`,`comprehend-what-is`,[`Amazon Comprehend uses natural language processing (NLP) to extract insights about the content of documents.`])),e(`convert text to spoken audio`,`Amazon Polly`,n(`Polly converts text into lifelike speech.`,`polly-what-is`,[`Amazon Polly is a cloud service that converts text into lifelike speech.`])),e(`analyse images or video / detect objects or faces`,`Amazon Rekognition`,n(`Rekognition is an image and video analysis service for adding computer vision to applications.`,`rekognition-what-is`,[`Amazon Rekognition is a cloud-based image and video analysis service that makes it easy to add advanced computer vision capabilities to your applications.`])),e(`extract text, forms or tables from scanned documents`,`Amazon Textract`,n(`Textract extracts text, forms and tables from documents.`,`textract-what-is`,[`Extract text, forms, and tables from documents with structured data, using the Amazon Textract Document Analysis API.`])),e(`convert recorded speech or audio to text`,`Amazon Transcribe`,n(`Transcribe is an automatic speech recognition service that converts audio to text.`,`transcribe-what-is`,[`Amazon Transcribe is an automatic speech recognition service that uses machine learning models to convert audio to text.`])),e(`translate text between languages`,`Amazon Translate`,n(`Translate is a text translation service.`,`translate-what-is`,[`Amazon Translate is a text translation service that uses advanced machine learning technologies to provide high-quality translation on demand.`])),e(`application keeps session state on the server and cannot be modified`,`ALB sticky sessions`,n(`Sticky sessions send all requests in a session to the same target and suit servers that maintain state.`,`alb-target-attrs`,[`This feature is useful for servers that maintain state information in order to provide a continuous experience to clients.`])),e(`instance impaired by underlying hardware, keep the same instance ID and IP`,`EC2 automatic instance recovery`,n(`Recovery preserves the instance ID, IP addresses, metadata and attached EBS volumes.`,`ec2-recover`,[`Preserved elements: Instance ID Public, private, and Elastic IP addresses Instance metadata Placement group Attached EBS volumes Availability Zone Lost elements: Data stored in volatile memory (RAM)`])),e(`legacy database needs high availability, no application change`,`RDS Multi-AZ`,n(`RDS provisions and maintains a synchronous standby in a different Availability Zone.`,`rds-multi-az-instance`,[`In a Multi-AZ DB instance deployment, Amazon RDS automatically provisions and maintains a synchronous standby replica in a different Availability Zone.`]))],examples:[],confuse:[]},d={building:`message-quay`,overview:[n(`When one part of a system calls another directly, a slow or failed callee slows or breaks the caller. {{term:loose-coupling}} avoids that by putting something in between: a queue, a topic, an event bus, a stream or a workflow. AWS's reliability guidance says components usually should not integrate through direct point-to-point interaction but through an intermediate durable layer such as an Amazon SQS queue, a streaming platform such as Amazon Kinesis, or AWS Step Functions.`,`waf-loose-coupling`,[`Implementing loose coupling between dependencies isolates a failure in one from impacting another.`,`The two components do not integrate through direct point-to-point interaction but usually through an intermediate durable storage layer, such as an Amazon SQS queue, a streaming data platform such as Amazon Kinesis, or AWS Step Functions.`]),n(`An event-driven architecture uses events to trigger and communicate between decoupled services. The Don't confuse section below compares SQS, SNS, EventBridge and Kinesis Data Streams.`,`eda-overview`,[`An event-driven architecture uses events to trigger and communicate between decoupled services`])],bullets:[{id:`2.1-K5`,concepts:[n(`An event-driven architecture uses events to trigger and communicate between decoupled services. It has three key components: event producers, event routers and event consumers. Events can carry state (the item purchased, its price and a delivery address) or be identifiers (a notification that an order was shipped).`,`eda-overview`,[`An event-driven architecture uses events to trigger and communicate between decoupled services and is common in modern applications built with microservices.`,`Event-driven architectures have three key components: event producers, event routers, and event consumers.`,`Events can either carry the state (the item purchased, its price, and a delivery address) or events can be identifiers (a notification that an order was shipped).`],{allow:[`three`]}),n(`Amazon EventBridge is a serverless service that uses events to connect application components, making it easier to build scalable event-driven applications. A rule specifies which events go to which targets, and an event pattern selects events by source, type or attributes.`,[`eventbridge-what-is`,`eventbridge-rules`,`eventbridge-patterns`],[`eventbridge-what-is|EventBridge is a serverless service that uses events to connect application components together, making it easier for you to build scalable event-driven applications.`,`eventbridge-rules|A rule specifies which events to send to which targets for processing.`,`eventbridge-patterns|you'll likely want to select a subset of all the events delivered, based on the source of the event, the event type, and/or attributes of those events.`]),n(`A single EventBridge rule can send an event to multiple targets, which then run in parallel. Rules either match on event data or run on a defined schedule, although scheduled rules are a legacy feature and EventBridge Scheduler is the newer way to schedule tasks. AWS describes a Custom Event Bus (which retains events for a period you set and supports ordered delivery and replay) alongside the classic bus, which remains available, and says to start new applications with the Custom Event Bus.`,[`eventbridge-what-is`,`eventbridge-rules`],[`eventbridge-rules|A single rule can send an event to multiple targets, which then run in parallel.You can create two types of rules: rules that match on event data as events are delivered, and rules that run on a defined schedule.`,`eventbridge-rules|Scheduled rules are a legacy feature of EventBridge.EventBridge offers a more flexible and powerful way to create, run, and manage scheduled tasks centrally, at scale: EventBridge Scheduler.`,`eventbridge-what-is|The Custom Event Bus routes events to subscribers that you create, retains events for a period that you set, and supports ordered delivery, replay, and non-JSON payloads.`,`eventbridge-what-is|For new applications, start with the Custom Event Bus.`,`eventbridge-what-is|Custom Event Bus - Classic remains available, and this guide describes both.`],{newer:[`eventbridge-scheduler`]})],services:[`Amazon EventBridge`,`AWS Lambda`,`Amazon SNS`,`Amazon SQS`],design:[n(`Choose events when something happens and several independent parts of the system may need to react. The producer does not know who the consumers are, so you can add or change consumers without touching it.`,`eda-overview`,[`An event-driven architecture uses events to trigger and communicate between decoupled services`])]},{id:`2.1-K11`,concepts:[n(`Amazon SQS offers a secure, durable and available hosted queue that lets you integrate and decouple distributed software systems and components. A producer sends a message and a consumer takes it when ready, so the two can work at different speeds.`,`sqs-what-is`,[`Amazon SQS decouples and scales distributed software systems and components as a queue service.`],{allow:[`two`]}),n(`Standard queues are the default queue type. They ensure at-least-once delivery, so more than one copy of a message might be delivered and messages may occasionally arrive out of order, although they make a best-effort attempt to maintain the order in which messages are sent. SQS stores each message redundantly in multiple Availability Zones before acknowledging it.`,`sqs-standard`,[`Amazon SQS provides standard queues as the default queue type`,`Standard queues ensure at-least-once message delivery, but due to the highly distributed architecture, more than one copy of a message might be delivered, and messages may occasionally arrive out of order.`,`Despite this, standard queues make a best-effort attempt to maintain the order in which messages are sent.`,`Amazon SQS redundantly stores the message in multiple availability zones (AZs) before acknowledging it.`],{allow:[`two`]}),n(`A dead-letter queue (DLQ) is a queue that source queues can target for messages that are not processed successfully, so you can examine and analyse them. It must be in the same account and Region as the source queue.`,`sqs-dlq`,[`Amazon SQS supports dead-letter queues (DLQs), which source queues can target for messages that are not processed successfully.`,`The dead-letter queue must be in the same AWS account and Region as the source queue.`]),n(`Amazon SNS is publish/subscribe: publishers send messages to a topic, and the topic delivers them to multiple subscribers. Subscribers can be SQS queues, Lambda functions, HTTP(S) endpoints, email, mobile push and SMS.`,`sns-what-is`,[`Publishers communicate asynchronously with subscribers by sending messages to a topic, which is a logical access point and communication channel.`,`Subscribers to an SNS topic can receive messages through different endpoints, depending on their use case, such as: Amazon SQS Lambda HTTP(S) endpoints Email Mobile push notifications Mobile text messages (SMS)`]),n(`Amazon MQ is the managed broker option in the same family as SQS and SNS. AWS lists SQS, SNS and Amazon MQ as managed messaging services each designed for specific roles within distributed systems. Amazon MQ is a managed message broker service for Apache ActiveMQ Classic and RabbitMQ, and you can migrate existing message brokers to it without rewriting messaging code.`,[`sqs-what-is`,`mq-what-is`],[`sqs-what-is|Amazon SQS, Amazon SNS, and Amazon MQ offer highly scalable and easy-to-use managed messaging services, each designed for specific roles within distributed systems.`,`mq-what-is|Amazon MQ is a managed message broker service for Apache ActiveMQ Classic and RabbitMQ that manages the setup, operation, and maintenance of message brokers.`,`mq-what-is|You can create a new Amazon MQ broker using industry standard messaging protocols, or migrate existing message brokers to Amazon MQ without rewriting messaging code.`])],services:[`Amazon SQS`,`Amazon SNS`,`Amazon MQ`],design:[n(`A common shape: a queue between a web tier and a processing tier so request spikes are buffered, messages that cannot be processed go to a dead-letter queue, and the consumers work at their own speed. Add SNS in front for fanout to several queues.`,[`sqs-what-is`,`sns-what-is`,`sqs-dlq`],[`sqs-what-is|Scalability – Amazon SQS can process each buffered request independently, scaling transparently to handle any load increases or spikes without any provisioning instructions.`,`sqs-dlq|Amazon SQS supports dead-letter queues (DLQs), which source queues can target for messages that are not processed successfully.`,`sqs-what-is|For wider distribution, integrating Amazon SQS with Amazon SNS enables a fanout messaging pattern, effectively pushing messages to multiple subscribers at once.`,`sns-what-is|The Fanout scenario is when a message published to an SNS topic is replicated and pushed to multiple endpoints`])]},{id:`2.1-K16`,concepts:[n(`With AWS Step Functions you create workflows; in Step Functions a state machine is called a workflow, made of event-driven steps called states, and running workflows are executions. Step Functions can call AWS services such as Lambda to perform tasks.`,`stepfunctions`,[`In Step Functions, state machines are called workflows, which are a series of event-driven steps.`,`Each step in a workflow is called a state.`,`Depending on your use case, you can have Step Functions call AWS services, such as Lambda, to perform tasks.`]),n(`There are two workflow types. Standard workflows are ideal for long-running, auditable workflows, as they show execution history and visual debugging; they have exactly-once workflow execution and can run for up to one year. Express workflows are ideal for high-event-rate workloads such as streaming data processing and IoT data ingestion; they have at-least-once workflow execution, so a step can potentially run more than once, and can run for up to five minutes.`,`stepfunctions`,[`Step Functions has two workflow types: Standard workflows are ideal for long-running, auditable workflows, as they show execution history and visual debugging.`,`Standard workflows have exactly-once workflow execution and can run for up to one year.`,`Express workflows are ideal for high-event-rate workloads, such as streaming data processing and IoT data ingestion.`,`Express workflows have at-least-once workflow execution and can run for up to five minutes.`,`This means that one or more steps in an Express Workflow can potentially run more than once, while each step in the workflow executes at least once.`],{allow:[`two`]}),n(`Step Functions is one of the intermediate layers AWS names for loose coupling, alongside queues and streams.`,`waf-loose-coupling`,[`usually through an intermediate durable storage layer, such as an Amazon SQS queue, a streaming data platform such as Amazon Kinesis, or AWS Step Functions.`])],services:[`AWS Step Functions`,`AWS Lambda`],design:[n(`Step Functions fits work with ordered steps and branching: it orchestrates a series of tasks in a specific order, a Choice state can pick the next step from the input data, and failed tasks can be retried or caught and sent to alternative steps.`,`stepfunctions`,[`Orchestrate tasks – You can create workflows that orchestrate a series of tasks, or steps, in a specific order.`,`Choose tasks based on data – Using a Choice state, you can have Step Functions make decisions based on the state’s input.`,`Error handling (Retry / Catch) – You can retry failed tasks, or catch failed tasks and automatically run alternative steps.`])]},{id:`2.1-S3`,concepts:[n(`Making dependencies loosely coupled means decoupling queuing systems, streaming systems and workflows, which minimises the impact of changes or failure on a system and isolates a failure in one component from affecting another.`,`waf-loose-coupling`,[`Decoupling dependencies, such as queuing systems, streaming systems, and workflows, help minimize the impact of changes or failure on a system.`,`Implementing loose coupling between dependencies isolates a failure in one from impacting another.`]),n(`Consumers must also be safe to retry. An idempotent service processes a request so that making multiple identical requests has the same effect as making a single request, which AWS achieves with an idempotency token.`,`waf-idempotent`,[`An idempotent service promises that each request is processed exactly once, such that making multiple identical requests has the same effect as making a single request.`,`To do this, clients can issue API requests with an idempotency token, which is used whenever the request is repeated.`])],services:[`Amazon SQS`,`Amazon SNS`,`Amazon EventBridge`,`AWS Step Functions`,`Amazon Kinesis`],design:[n(`Match the requirement: buffer and decouple speeds (SQS), notify many subscribers (SNS), route events by content with rules (EventBridge), process a continuous stream (Kinesis Data Streams), coordinate a multi-step process (Step Functions).`,[`sqs-what-is`,`sns-what-is`,`eventbridge-what-is`,`kinesis-streams`,`stepfunctions`],[`sqs-what-is|Amazon SQS decouples and scales distributed software systems and components as a queue service.`,`sns-what-is|The topic acts as a logical access point, ensuring messages are delivered to multiple subscribers across different platforms.`,`eventbridge-what-is|EventBridge provides simple and consistent ways to ingest, filter, transform, and deliver events so you can build applications quickly.`,`kinesis-streams|You can use Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to collect and process large streams of data records in real time.`,`stepfunctions|In Step Functions, state machines are called workflows, which are a series of event-driven steps.`])]}],cues:[e(`decouple the producer and consumer / absorb traffic spikes / buffer requests`,`Amazon SQS`,n(`SQS is a hosted queue that decouples and scales distributed systems and components.`,`sqs-what-is`,[`Amazon SQS decouples and scales distributed software systems and components as a queue service.`])),e(`send one message to multiple subscribers / fanout / email, SMS, mobile push`,`Amazon SNS`,n(`SNS delivers messages from publishers to multiple subscribers, including SQS, Lambda, email, push and SMS.`,`sns-what-is`,[`The Fanout scenario is when a message published to an SNS topic is replicated and pushed to multiple endpoints`])),e(`react to events from AWS services or SaaS applications, route by rules`,`Amazon EventBridge`,n(`A rule specifies which events to send to which targets, and one rule can send an event to multiple targets.`,`eventbridge-rules`,[`A rule specifies which events to send to which targets for processing.`])),e(`real-time streaming data read by several independent consumers, records kept in order within a shard`,`Kinesis Data Streams`,n(`Kinesis Data Streams collects and processes large streams of data records in real time; several applications can consume the same stream independently, and each shard holds a sequence of data records.`,[`kinesis-streams`,`kinesis-concepts`],[`kinesis-streams|You can use Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to collect and process large streams of data records in real time.`,`kinesis-streams|Multiple Kinesis Data Streams applications can consume data from a stream, so that multiple actions, like archiving and processing, can take place concurrently and independently.`,`kinesis-concepts|Each shard has a sequence of data records.`])),e(`coordinate multiple steps with retries and branching / visual workflow`,`AWS Step Functions`,n(`Step Functions creates workflows of event-driven steps, lets you visualize, edit and debug them, and can retry failed tasks or catch them and run alternative steps.`,`stepfunctions`,[`In the Step Functions' console, you can visualize, edit, and debug your application’s workflow.`,`Error handling (Retry / Catch) – You can retry failed tasks, or catch failed tasks and automatically run alternative steps.`])),e(`migrate an existing ActiveMQ or RabbitMQ message broker without rewriting messaging code`,`Amazon MQ`,n(`Amazon MQ is a managed message broker service for Apache ActiveMQ Classic and RabbitMQ, and existing brokers can be migrated to it without rewriting messaging code.`,`mq-what-is`,[`You can create a new Amazon MQ broker using industry standard messaging protocols, or migrate existing message brokers to Amazon MQ without rewriting messaging code.`])),e(`failed messages should be set aside for analysis`,`SQS dead-letter queue`,n(`A dead-letter queue holds messages that are not processed successfully so you can analyse them.`,`sqs-dlq`,[`Analyze the contents of messages moved to the dead-letter queue to diagnose application issues.`]))],examples:[t(`EventBridge event pattern for new objects in one bucket`,`other`,`
{
  "source": ["aws.s3"],
  "detail-type": ["Object Created"],
  "detail": {
    "bucket": { "name": ["example-uploads-bucket"] }
  }
}
`,[n(`An event pattern defines the data EventBridge uses to decide whether to send an event to a target. You write the fields you want to match; events that do not match are not delivered to that rule's targets.`,`eventbridge-patterns`,[`To specify which events to send to a target, you create an event pattern.`,`An event pattern defines the data EventBridge uses to determine whether to send the event to the target.`]),n("`source` and `detail-type` select events by the source of the event and the event type; the `detail` section selects on attributes of the event, here one bucket name.",`eventbridge-patterns`,[`based on the source of the event, the event type, and/or attributes of those events.`]),n(`The pattern belongs in a rule, and the rule names the targets, which can be several and run in parallel. Illustrative only: the bucket name is a placeholder.`,`eventbridge-rules`,[`A rule specifies which events to send to which targets for processing.`,`A single rule can send an event to multiple targets, which then run in parallel.`])])],confuse:[`sqs-sns-eventbridge-kinesis`],azure:[{concept:`Azure Event Grid`,aws:`Amazon EventBridge`,mapping:n(`Microsoft Learn pairs Azure Event Grid with EventBridge as the managed event routing service for event-driven architectures.`,[`learn-compute`,`eventbridge-what-is`],[`learn-compute|Managed event routing for event-driven architectures.`,`eventbridge-what-is|EventBridge is a serverless service that uses events to connect application components together, making it easier for you to build scalable event-driven applications.`]),breaks:n(`Learn's messaging page pairs Amazon SQS with Azure queue services and Amazon SNS with Azure Service Bus topics and Event Grid, among other options. On AWS, queues, topics and event routing are separate services: SQS is the queue, SNS is publish/subscribe, and EventBridge routes events by rules.`,[`learn-messaging`,`sqs-what-is`,`sns-what-is`,`eventbridge-rules`],[`learn-messaging|Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) Azure Queue Storage or Azure Service Bus queues Use Queue Storage for basic, high-scale work queues.`,`learn-messaging|Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) Azure Service Bus topics, Azure Event Grid, Microsoft 365 High Volume Email, or a specialized communications provider`,`sqs-what-is|Amazon SQS decouples and scales distributed software systems and components as a queue service.`,`sns-what-is|Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) is a fully managed service that provides message delivery from publishers (producers) to subscribers (consumers).`,`eventbridge-rules|A rule specifies which events to send to which targets for processing.`])},{concept:`Azure Durable Functions and Azure Logic Apps`,aws:`AWS Step Functions`,mapping:n(`The Learn comparison pairs Step Functions with Durable Functions and Logic Apps: stateful workflow orchestration.`,[`learn-compute`,`stepfunctions`],[`learn-compute|AWS Step Functions Azure durable functions, Azure Logic Apps`,`stepfunctions|With AWS Step Functions, you can create workflows, also called State machines, to build distributed applications, automate processes, orchestrate microservices, and create data and machine learning pipelines.`]),breaks:n(`The Microsoft Learn comparison names the pairing but not workflow types. Step Functions workflows are defined as state machines of states, and there are two types: Standard (long-running, auditable, exactly-once) and Express (high-event-rate, at-least-once).`,[`stepfunctions`,`learn-compute`],[`stepfunctions|Standard workflows are ideal for long-running, auditable workflows, as they show execution history and visual debugging.`,`stepfunctions|Express workflows are ideal for high-event-rate workloads, such as streaming data processing and IoT data ingestion.`,`learn-compute|AWS Step Functions Azure durable functions, Azure Logic Apps`],{allow:[`two`]})},{concept:`Azure Service Bus`,aws:`Amazon SQS and Amazon SNS`,mapping:n(`Azure Service Bus is a fully managed enterprise message broker with message queues and publish-subscribe topics. Learn pairs Amazon SQS with Azure Queue Storage or Service Bus queues, and pairs Amazon SNS with Service Bus topics among other options. On AWS the queue and topic roles are split across SQS and SNS.`,[`learn-azure-servicebus`,`learn-messaging`,`sqs-what-is`,`sns-what-is`],[`learn-azure-servicebus|Azure Service Bus is a fully managed enterprise message broker with message queues and publish-subscribe topics.`,`learn-messaging|Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) Azure Queue Storage or Azure Service Bus queues Use Queue Storage for basic, high-scale work queues.`,`learn-messaging|Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) Azure Service Bus topics, Azure Event Grid, Microsoft 365 High Volume Email, or a specialized communications provider`,`sqs-what-is|Amazon SQS decouples and scales distributed software systems and components as a queue service.`,`sns-what-is|Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) is a fully managed service that provides message delivery from publishers (producers) to subscribers (consumers).`]),breaks:n(`Service Bus puts queues and topics in one service, while AWS splits the roles across SQS and SNS, and offers Amazon MQ as a managed broker for ActiveMQ and RabbitMQ.`,[`learn-azure-servicebus`,`mq-what-is`],[`learn-azure-servicebus|Azure Service Bus is a fully managed enterprise message broker with message queues and publish-subscribe topics.`,`mq-what-is|Amazon MQ is a managed message broker service for Apache ActiveMQ Classic and RabbitMQ that manages the setup, operation, and maintenance of message brokers.`])}]},f={building:`purpose-built-workshop`,overview:[n(`A purpose-built service is shaped for one data model or job. Examples in this building: a managed file transfer service for moving files into AWS storage, a secrets service instead of hard-coded credentials, and a database whose model fits the data, such as a graph database for connected data.`,[`transfer-what-is`,`secrets-intro`,`database-decision`],[`transfer-what-is|AWS Transfer Family is a secure transfer service that enables you to transfer files into and out of AWS storage services.`,`secrets-intro|Secrets Manager helps you improve your security posture, because you no longer need hard-coded credentials in application source code.`,`database-decision|AWS offers a growing number of database options (15+) with diverse data models to support a variety of workloads.`])],bullets:[{id:`2.1-K2`,concepts:[n(`AWS Transfer Family offers fully managed support for transferring files over SFTP, AS2, FTPS and FTP, and web browser-based transfers, directly into and out of AWS storage services. Use it when partners or legacy systems must keep using a file transfer protocol but you want the files to land in AWS storage.`,`transfer-what-is`,[`AWS Transfer Family offers fully managed support for the transfer of files over SFTP, AS2, FTPS, FTP, and web browser-based transfers directly into and out of AWS storage services.`]),n(`Amazon SQS offers a secure, durable and available hosted queue that lets you decouple distributed software systems and components. (Details are in Message Quay.)`,`sqs-what-is`,[`Amazon SQS decouples and scales distributed software systems and components as a queue service.`]),n(`AWS Secrets Manager helps you manage, retrieve and rotate database credentials, application credentials, OAuth tokens, API keys and other secrets throughout their lifecycles. You replace hard-coded credentials with a runtime call to retrieve them dynamically, and you can configure an automatic rotation schedule.`,`secrets-intro`,[`AWS Secrets Manager helps you manage, retrieve, and rotate database credentials, application credentials, OAuth tokens, API keys, and other secrets throughout their lifecycles`,`You replace hard-coded credentials with a runtime call to the Secrets Manager service to retrieve credentials dynamically when you need them.`,`With Secrets Manager, you can configure an automatic rotation schedule for your secrets.`])],services:[`AWS Transfer Family`,`Amazon SQS`,`AWS Secrets Manager`],design:[n(`Typical match-ups: a partner needs SFTP into your S3 bucket → Transfer Family; components should not call each other directly → SQS; application needs a database password without storing it in code → Secrets Manager (with rotation). The Secrets Manager vs Parameter Store comparison is in Warden's Lodge.`,[`transfer-what-is`,`secrets-intro`],[`transfer-what-is|AWS Transfer Family is a secure transfer service that enables you to transfer files into and out of AWS storage services.`,`secrets-intro|Storing the credentials in Secrets Manager helps avoid possible compromise by anyone who can inspect your application or the components.`])]},{id:`2.1-S7`,concepts:[n(`After a re-platform to a managed database, AWS suggests you might refactor the workload to be cloud focused: you could use Amazon Aurora or purpose-built NoSQL databases such as Amazon DynamoDB, Amazon Neptune or Amazon DocumentDB. The database models it lists are relational, key-value, document, in-memory, graph, time series, vector and wide-column.`,`database-decision`,[`You might consider this an opportunity to refactor your workload to be cloud focused.`,`For example, you could use Amazon Aurora or purpose-built NoSQL databases such as Amazon DynamoDB, Amazon Neptune, or Amazon DocumentDB (with MongoDB compatibility).`,`These include relational, key-value, document, in-memory, graph, time series, vector, and wide-column.`]),n(`Key-value: Amazon DynamoDB is a serverless, fully managed, distributed NoSQL database with single-digit millisecond performance at any scale. AWS suggests it for workloads such as session stores or shopping carts.`,[`dynamodb-what-is`,`database-decision`],[`dynamodb-what-is|Amazon DynamoDB is a serverless, fully managed, distributed NoSQL database with single-digit millisecond performance at any scale.`,`database-decision|Amazon DynamoDB Key-value Use for workloads such as session stores or shopping carts.`]),n(`Document: Amazon DocumentDB (with MongoDB compatibility) is for storing JSON-like documents with rich querying across the fields of the documents.`,`database-decision`,[`Amazon DocumentDB (with MongoDB compatibility) Document Use when you want to store JSON-like documents with rich querying abilities across the fields of the documents.`]),n(`Graph: Amazon Neptune is a fast, reliable, fully managed graph database for applications that work with highly connected datasets, with use cases such as recommendation engines, fraud detection, knowledge graphs, drug discovery and network security.`,`neptune-what-is`,[`Amazon Neptune is a fast, reliable, fully managed graph database service that makes it easy to build and run applications that work with highly connected datasets.`,`Neptune powers graph use cases such as recommendation engines, fraud detection, knowledge graphs, drug discovery, and network security.`]),n(`Time series: Amazon Timestream is for large amounts of time series data from many sources, such as IoT data, application metrics and asset tracking. Timestream is not on the exam guide's in-scope list, so treat it as context only. Wide-column: Amazon Keyspaces (for Apache Cassandra) is for migrating on-premises Cassandra workloads.`,[`database-decision`],[`database-decision|Amazon Timestream Time series Use when you have a large amount of time series data, potentially from a number of sources, such as Internet of Things (IoT) data, application metrics, and asset tracking.`,`database-decision|Amazon Keyspaces (for Apache Cassandra) Wide-column Use when you need to migrate your on-premises Cassandra workloads`]),n(`AWS has closed new customer access to Amazon Timestream for LiveAnalytics and recommends that new customers evaluate Amazon Timestream for InfluxDB.`,[`timestream-availability`],[`After careful consideration, we have made the decision to close new customer access to Amazon Timestream for LiveAnalytics`,`We recommend that new customers evaluate Amazon Timestream for InfluxDB as an alternative due to its similar functionality.`],{status:`closed`}),n(`In-memory: for response times in microseconds, use an in-memory caching solution such as Amazon ElastiCache alongside your database, or Amazon MemoryDB when you need a fully durable, persistent in-memory database.`,`database-decision`,[`If your workload requires extremely high read performance with a response time measured in microseconds (rather than single-digit milliseconds), you might want to consider using in- memory caching solutions such as Amazon ElastiCache alongside your database, or a fully durable, persistent in-memory database such as Amazon MemoryDB.`]),n(`Relational: Amazon RDS is a fully managed relational database with a choice of engines (PostgreSQL, MySQL, MariaDB, SQL Server, Oracle and Db2); Amazon Aurora is for migrating or modernising on-premises relational workloads, or workloads with less predictable query patterns. Analytics at scale is Amazon Redshift, a fully managed data warehousing service. (The Aurora vs RDS comparison is in Database Registry.)`,`database-decision`,[`Amazon RDS Relational Use when you need a fully managed relational database with your choice of six popular engines: PostgreSQL, MySQL, MariaDB, SQL Server, Oracle, and Db2.`,`Amazon Aurora Relational Use when you're migrating or modernizing an on-premises relational workload, or if your workload has less predictable query patterns.`,`Amazon Redshift is a fully managed, cloud-based data warehousing service that is designed to handle large-scale analytics workloads.`])],services:[`Amazon DynamoDB`,`Amazon Neptune`,`Amazon ElastiCache`,`Amazon RDS`],design:[n(`Read the data shape in the question: highly connected relationships → graph (Neptune); JSON documents with rich queries → DocumentDB; simple key lookups at massive scale → DynamoDB; relational → RDS or Aurora; microsecond reads in front of a database → an in-memory cache such as ElastiCache; metrics or IoT data over time → a time series database.`,[`database-decision`,`neptune-what-is`],[`database-decision|Key-value databases can scale to large amounts of data and extremely high throughput of requests, while servicing millions of simultaneous users through distributed processing and storage.`,`neptune-what-is|Neptune powers graph use cases such as recommendation engines, fraud detection, knowledge graphs, drug discovery, and network security.`,`database-decision|Amazon DocumentDB (with MongoDB compatibility) Document Use when you want to store JSON-like documents with rich querying abilities across the fields of the documents.`,`database-decision|Amazon RDS Relational Use when you need a fully managed relational database with your choice of six popular engines: PostgreSQL, MySQL, MariaDB, SQL Server, Oracle, and Db2.`,`database-decision|If your workload requires extremely high read performance with a response time measured in microseconds (rather than single-digit milliseconds), you might want to consider using in- memory caching solutions such as Amazon ElastiCache alongside your database, or a fully durable, persistent in-memory database such as Amazon MemoryDB.`,`database-decision|Amazon Timestream Time series Use when you have a large amount of time series data, potentially from a number of sources, such as Internet of Things (IoT) data, application metrics, and asset tracking.`])]}],cues:[e(`partners upload files over SFTP/FTPS to AWS storage`,`AWS Transfer Family`,n(`Transfer Family supports SFTP, AS2, FTPS and FTP into and out of AWS storage.`,`transfer-what-is`,[`AWS Transfer Family offers fully managed support for the transfer of files over SFTP, AS2, FTPS, FTP, and web browser-based transfers directly into and out of AWS storage services.`])),e(`no hard-coded database credentials / rotate credentials automatically`,`AWS Secrets Manager`,n(`Secrets Manager rotates secrets and removes hard-coded credentials.`,`secrets-intro`,[`With Secrets Manager, you can configure an automatic rotation schedule for your secrets.`])),e(`highly connected data, relationships, recommendation or fraud detection`,`Amazon Neptune`,n(`Neptune is a graph database for highly connected datasets.`,`neptune-what-is`,[`Neptune powers graph use cases such as recommendation engines, fraud detection, knowledge graphs, drug discovery, and network security.`])),e(`session store or shopping cart at massive scale, key-value`,`Amazon DynamoDB (a serverless key-value table); when microsecond in-memory reads are the need, an in-memory cache such as ElastiCache`,n(`AWS suggests DynamoDB for session stores or shopping carts, and suggests an in-memory cache such as ElastiCache when response times need to be measured in microseconds.`,`database-decision`,[`Amazon DynamoDB Key-value Use for workloads such as session stores or shopping carts.`,`If your workload requires extremely high read performance with a response time measured in microseconds (rather than single-digit milliseconds), you might want to consider using in- memory caching solutions such as Amazon ElastiCache alongside your database, or a fully durable, persistent in-memory database such as Amazon MemoryDB.`])),e(`JSON documents with rich queries, MongoDB compatible`,`Amazon DocumentDB`,n(`DocumentDB stores JSON-like documents with rich querying across fields.`,`database-decision`,[`Amazon DocumentDB (with MongoDB compatibility) Document Use when you want to store JSON-like documents with rich querying abilities across the fields of the documents.`])),e(`IoT or metrics time series data`,`A time series database (Amazon Timestream, outside the exam guide's in-scope list: context only)`,n(`Timestream is for large amounts of time series data from IoT, metrics and asset tracking.`,[`database-decision`],[`database-decision|Amazon Timestream Time series Use when you have a large amount of time series data, potentially from a number of sources, such as Internet of Things (IoT) data, application metrics, and asset tracking.`])),e(`migrate on-premises Cassandra`,`Amazon Keyspaces`,n(`Keyspaces is the wide-column service for migrating on-premises Cassandra workloads.`,`database-decision`,[`Amazon Keyspaces (for Apache Cassandra) Wide-column Use when you need to migrate your on-premises Cassandra workloads`])),e(`data warehouse, large-scale analytics (OLAP)`,`Amazon Redshift`,n(`Redshift is a fully managed data warehousing service for large-scale analytics.`,`database-decision`,[`Amazon Redshift is a fully managed, cloud-based data warehousing service that is designed to handle large-scale analytics workloads.`]))],examples:[],confuse:[]},p={building:`rebuild-yard`,overview:[n(`Instead of repairing servers by hand, rebuild them from a known definition. {{term:immutable-infrastructure}} means you never change running production resources in place: you deploy replacements built from a version-controlled configuration. Automation (CloudFormation for provisioning, Systems Manager for operating, Auto Scaling for replacing) makes the rebuild repeatable.`,[`waf-immutable`,`waf-automate-changes`],[`waf-immutable|Immutable infrastructure is a model that mandates that no updates, security patches, or configuration changes happen in-place on production workloads.`,`waf-automate-changes|Today, this means the use of automation wherever practical in operations, including testing and deploying changes, adding or removing capacity, and migrating data.`])],bullets:[{id:`2.2-K7`,concepts:[n(`With immutable infrastructure no in-place modifications are allowed on infrastructure resources within a workload. The Well-Architected benefits are reliability, consistency and reproducibility of deployments; fewer configuration drifts, because replacing resources with a known, version-controlled configuration returns them to a known, tested state; safer deployments with fast rollback, because the previous working version is not changed; and an enhanced security posture, because remote access mechanisms such as SSH can be disabled.`,`waf-immutable`,[`Follow an immutable infrastructure deployment strategy to increase the reliability, consistency, and reproducibility in your workload deployments.`,`Desired outcome: With immutable infrastructure, no in-place modifications are allowed to run infrastructure resources within a workload.`,`Reduction in configuration drifts: By replacing infrastructure resources with a known and version-controlled configuration, the infrastructure is set to a known, tested, and trusted state, avoiding configuration drifts.`,`Safer deployments with fast rollback and recovery processes: Deployments are safer because the previous working version is not changed.`,`Enhanced security posture: By not allowing changes to infrastructure, remote access mechanisms (such as SSH) can be disabled.`]),n(`Two deployment strategies for immutable infrastructure: a canary deployment directs a small number of customers to the new version, usually on a single instance (the canary); a blue/green deployment is similar except that a full fleet of the application is deployed in parallel.`,`waf-immutable`,[`There are two main strategies for this deployment: Canary deployment: The practice of directing a small number of your customers to the new version, usually running on a single service instance (the canary).`,`Blue/green deployment: Similar to the canary deployment, except that a full fleet of the application is deployed in parallel.`],{allow:[`two`]}),n(`An {{term:ami}} provides the software required to set up and boot an EC2 instance, so a new AMI is the natural unit of an immutable release. An Auto Scaling group instance refresh updates the instances in a group, for example to deploy a new AMI or user data script across the group.`,[`ami`,`asg-instance-refresh`],[`ami|An Amazon Machine Image (AMI) is an image that provides the software that is required to set up and boot an Amazon EC2 instance.`,`asg-instance-refresh|Some situations where an instance refresh can help include: Deploying a new Amazon Machine Image (AMI) or user data script across your Auto Scaling group.`])],services:[`Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling`,`AWS CloudFormation`],design:[n(`The immutable approach to deploying an update: build a new image or template and deploy it as new resources instead of changing the running ones, using a canary or blue/green deployment. Deployments are safer with fast rollback because the previous working version is not changed. Use automation as much as possible to increase reproducibility and minimise human error.`,`waf-immutable`,[`Canary deployment: The practice of directing a small number of your customers to the new version, usually running on a single service instance (the canary).`,`Blue/green deployment: Similar to the canary deployment, except that a full fleet of the application is deployed in parallel.`,`Safer deployments with fast rollback and recovery processes: Deployments are safer because the previous working version is not changed.`,`When defining an immutable infrastructure deployment strategy, it is recommended to use automation as much as possible to increase reproducibility and minimize the potential of human error.`])]},{id:`2.2-S1`,concepts:[n(`AWS CloudFormation helps you model and set up AWS resources: you create a {{term:cloudformation-stack|template}} describing the resources, and CloudFormation provisions and configures them as a stack. Because templates are text files, you track changes to your infrastructure the same way developers control revisions to source code; deleting the stack deletes all its resources.`,`cfn-what-is`,[`You create a template that describes all the AWS resources that you want (like Amazon EC2 instances or Amazon RDS DB instances), and CloudFormation takes care of provisioning and configuring those resources for you.`,`Because these templates are text files, you simply track differences in your templates to track changes to your infrastructure, similar to the way developers control revisions to source code.`,`You can delete the stack just as easily, which deletes all the resources in the stack.`]),n(`Infrastructure integrity can be checked: drift detection identifies stack resources whose configuration was changed outside CloudFormation, so you can bring them back in sync with the template.`,`cfn-drift`,[`You can use drift detection to identify stack resources to which configuration changes have been made outside of CloudFormation management.`,`You can then take corrective action so that your stack resources are again in sync with their definitions in the stack template, such as updating the drifted resources directly so that they agree with their template definition.`]),n(`AWS Systems Manager helps you centrally view, manage and operate nodes at scale. Its tools include Run Command, Session Manager, Automation and Parameter Store. Patch Manager automates the process of patching managed nodes with security-related and other updates; nodes must be managed, which means SSM Agent is installed and can communicate with Systems Manager.`,[`ssm-what-is`,`ssm-patch`],[`ssm-what-is|AWS Systems Manager helps you centrally view, manage, and operate nodes at scale in AWS, on-premises, and multicloud environments.`,`ssm-what-is|Tools consist of the individual capabilities of Systems Manager and their features such as Run Command, Session Manager, Automation, and Parameter Store.`,`ssm-what-is|To use Systems Manager, nodes must be managed, which means SSM Agent is installed on the machine and the agent can communicate with the Systems Manager service.`,`ssm-patch|Patch Manager automates the process of patching managed nodes with both security-related updates and other types of updates.`]),n(`Systems Manager Session Manager lets you securely connect to nodes without opening inbound ports, which fits the immutable idea of disabling SSH.`,`ssm-what-is`,[`With Systems Manager tools you can do the following: Just-in-time access node access Patch nodes at scale Securely connect to nodes without opening inbound ports Run commands remotely on nodes Securely store data referenced by applications Automate common systems administration tasks`]),n(`Deployments and patching should be automated to eliminate negative impact; Auto Scaling monitors instance health and replaces terminated or impaired instances to maintain desired capacity.`,[`waf-automate-changes`,`asg-what-is`],[`waf-automate-changes|Deployments and patching are automated to eliminate negative impact.`,`asg-what-is|Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling automatically monitors the health and availability of your instances using EC2 health checks and replaces terminated or impaired instances to maintain your desired capacity.`])],services:[`AWS CloudFormation`,`AWS Systems Manager`,`Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling`],design:[n(`Picking the tool: provision whole environments repeatably, including across Regions with CloudFormation StackSets → CloudFormation; patch and operate existing instances at scale without inbound ports → Systems Manager; roll a new image across a fleet → Auto Scaling instance refresh; detect manual changes → CloudFormation drift detection.`,[`cfn-what-is`,`ssm-patch`,`asg-instance-refresh`,`ssm-what-is`,`cfn-drift`,`waf-multi-location`],[`cfn-what-is|By using CloudFormation, you easily manage a collection of resources as a single unit.`,`waf-multi-location|To ease multi-Regional deployments and maintain consistency, AWS CloudFormation StackSets can replicate your entire AWS infrastructure across multiple Regions.`,`ssm-what-is|With Systems Manager tools you can do the following: Just-in-time access node access Patch nodes at scale Securely connect to nodes without opening inbound ports Run commands remotely on nodes Securely store data referenced by applications Automate common systems administration tasks`,`cfn-drift|You can use drift detection to identify stack resources to which configuration changes have been made outside of CloudFormation management.`,`ssm-patch|Patch Manager automates the process of patching managed nodes with both security-related updates and other types of updates.`,`asg-instance-refresh|You can use an instance refresh to update the instances in your Auto Scaling group.`])]}],cues:[e(`no in-place changes to production servers / replace rather than patch`,`Immutable infrastructure (new AMI or template, canary or blue/green)`,n(`Immutable infrastructure mandates no in-place updates, patches or configuration changes on production workloads.`,`waf-immutable`,[`Immutable infrastructure is a model that mandates that no updates, security patches, or configuration changes happen in-place on production workloads.`])),e(`repeatable, version-controlled environment definition`,`AWS CloudFormation`,n(`Templates are text files whose changes you can track like source code.`,`cfn-what-is`,[`Because these templates are text files, you simply track differences in your templates to track changes to your infrastructure, similar to the way developers control revisions to source code.`])),e(`someone changed a resource manually outside the template`,`CloudFormation drift detection`,n(`Drift detection identifies resources changed outside CloudFormation.`,`cfn-drift`,[`You can use drift detection to identify stack resources to which configuration changes have been made outside of CloudFormation management.`])),e(`patch instances at scale / run commands without SSH or inbound ports`,`AWS Systems Manager (Patch Manager, Session Manager, Run Command)`,n(`Systems Manager patches nodes at scale and connects securely without opening inbound ports.`,`ssm-what-is`,[`With Systems Manager tools you can do the following: Just-in-time access node access Patch nodes at scale Securely connect to nodes without opening inbound ports Run commands remotely on nodes Securely store data referenced by applications Automate common systems administration tasks`])),e(`roll out a new AMI to every instance in an Auto Scaling group`,`Auto Scaling instance refresh`,n(`An instance refresh can deploy a new AMI or user data script across the group.`,`asg-instance-refresh`,[`Some situations where an instance refresh can help include: Deploying a new Amazon Machine Image (AMI) or user data script across your Auto Scaling group.`])),e(`send a small share of users to the new version first`,`Canary deployment`,n(`A canary deployment directs a small number of customers to the new version.`,`waf-immutable`,[`Canary deployment: The practice of directing a small number of your customers to the new version, usually running on a single service instance (the canary).`]))],examples:[],confuse:[],azure:[{concept:`Azure Resource Manager / Bicep`,aws:`AWS CloudFormation`,mapping:n(`Learn pairs AWS CloudFormation with Azure Resource Manager and Bicep (and other tools): ways to build and deploy repeatable cloud environments by using declarative syntax to define infrastructure as code.`,[`learn-hub`,`cfn-what-is`],[`learn-hub|These services provide ways for developers and cloud admins to build and deploy repeatable cloud environments by using declarative syntax or common programming languages to define infrastructure as code.`,`cfn-what-is|You create a template that describes all the AWS resources that you want (like Amazon EC2 instances or Amazon RDS DB instances), and CloudFormation takes care of provisioning and configuring those resources for you.`]),breaks:n(`The Microsoft Learn comparison describes only the shared purpose (declarative or code-based infrastructure as code). On the AWS side, the CloudFormation unit of management is the stack, which manages a collection of resources as a single unit, and drift detection identifies resources changed outside CloudFormation.`,[`cfn-what-is`,`cfn-drift`,`learn-hub`],[`cfn-what-is|By using CloudFormation, you easily manage a collection of resources as a single unit.`,`cfn-drift|You can use drift detection to identify stack resources to which configuration changes have been made outside of CloudFormation management.`,`learn-hub|These services provide ways for developers and cloud admins to build and deploy repeatable cloud environments by using declarative syntax or common programming languages to define infrastructure as code.`])},{concept:`Azure Automation and Azure Update Manager`,aws:`AWS Systems Manager`,mapping:n(`Learn pairs AWS Systems Manager with Azure Automation and Azure Update Manager. Azure Update Manager helps manage updates for machines that run Windows and Linux across Azure, on-premises and other clouds.`,[`learn-hub`,`ssm-what-is`],[`learn-hub|AWS Systems Manager Azure Automation Azure Update Manager Automation configures and operates applications of all shapes and sizes.`,`learn-hub|Azure Update Manager is a service that helps manage updates for all your machines, including machines that run on Windows and Linux, across Azure, on-premises, and on other cloud platforms.`,`ssm-what-is|AWS Systems Manager helps you centrally view, manage, and operate nodes at scale in AWS, on-premises, and multicloud environments.`]),breaks:n(`The Microsoft Learn comparison pairs one AWS service with two Azure services. Systems Manager is one AWS service with many tools (Run Command, Session Manager, Automation and Parameter Store), and its nodes must be managed through SSM Agent.`,[`ssm-what-is`,`learn-hub`],[`ssm-what-is|Tools consist of the individual capabilities of Systems Manager and their features such as Run Command, Session Manager, Automation, and Parameter Store.`,`ssm-what-is|To use Systems Manager, nodes must be managed, which means SSM Agent is installed on the machine and the agent can communicate with the Systems Manager service.`,`learn-hub|AWS Systems Manager Azure Automation Azure Update Manager Automation configures and operates applications of all shapes and sizes.`],{allow:[`two`]})}]},m={building:`scaling-floodgate`,overview:[n(`When demand grows you can make one resource bigger (vertical scaling) or add more of them (horizontal scaling). Horizontal scaling needs something to spread the load across the copies, which is a load balancer, and something to add and remove copies automatically, which is Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling. For databases, horizontal scaling of reads is done with read replicas.`,[`rds-scaling-ha`,`elb-what-is`,`asg-what-is`],[`rds-scaling-ha|With Amazon RDS, you can scale vertically by modifying your DB instance size to meet your performance and capacity requirements.`,`rds-scaling-ha|Amazon RDS provides horizontal scaling by using read replicas to offload read-intensive workloads and improve database performance.`,`elb-what-is|Elastic Load Balancing automatically distributes your incoming traffic across multiple targets, such as EC2 instances, containers, and IP addresses, in one or more Availability Zones.`,`asg-what-is|Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling helps you ensure that you have the correct number of Amazon EC2 instances available to handle the load for your application.`])],bullets:[{id:`2.1-K6`,concepts:[n(`Vertical scaling makes a resource bigger. For EC2 you resize an instance by changing its instance type, and from then on you pay the rate of the new type. For RDS you modify the DB instance size.`,[`ec2-resize`,`rds-scaling-ha`],[`ec2-resize|If this is the case, you can resize your instance by changing its instance type.`,`ec2-resize|When you change the instance type, you'll start paying the rate of the new instance type.`,`rds-scaling-ha|With Amazon RDS, you can scale vertically by modifying your DB instance size to meet your performance and capacity requirements.`]),n(`Horizontal scaling adds more instances. For EC2 this means an Auto Scaling group: a collection of EC2 instances where you set a minimum and a maximum size, and EC2 Auto Scaling keeps the group within those limits. For RDS, AWS describes horizontal scaling as using read replicas for reads.`,[`asg-what-is`,`rds-scaling-ha`],[`asg-what-is|You create collections of EC2 instances, called Auto Scaling groups.`,`asg-what-is|You can specify the minimum number of instances in each Auto Scaling group, and Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling ensures that your group never goes below this size.`,`asg-what-is|You can specify the maximum number of instances in each Auto Scaling group, and Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling ensures that your group never goes above this size.`,`rds-scaling-ha|Amazon RDS provides horizontal scaling by using read replicas to offload read-intensive workloads and improve database performance.`]),n(`Horizontal scaling suits stateless components, because any of the available compute resources can service any request. That is the connection to Blueprint Hall: systems should either not require state or offload it, so that no request depends on locally stored data (see Legacy Wharf for sticky sessions).`,`waf-stateless`,[`they can scale horizontally because any of the available compute resources (such as EC2 instances and AWS Lambda functions) can service any request.`,`Systems should either not require state, or should offload state such that between different client requests, there is no dependence on locally stored data on disk and in memory.`])],services:[`Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling`,`Amazon RDS`,`Amazon EC2`],design:[n(`Auto Scaling can spread added instances across Availability Zones. A single database instance can be scaled vertically by modifying its size; for read-heavy load, read replicas add horizontal capacity.`,[`asg-what-is`,`rds-scaling-ha`],[`asg-what-is|You can specify multiple Availability Zones for your Auto Scaling group, and Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling balances your instances evenly across the Availability Zones as the group scales.`,`rds-scaling-ha|With Amazon RDS, you can scale vertically by modifying your DB instance size to meet your performance and capacity requirements.`,`rds-scaling-ha|Amazon RDS provides horizontal scaling by using read replicas to offload read-intensive workloads and improve database performance.`])]},{id:`2.1-K9`,concepts:[n(`A load balancer distributes workloads across multiple compute resources and serves as the single point of contact for clients. Using one increases the availability and fault tolerance of your applications. Elastic Load Balancing distributes incoming traffic across multiple targets such as EC2 instances, containers and IP addresses, in one or more Availability Zones, and scales the load balancer itself automatically.`,[`elb-what-is`,`alb-intro`],[`elb-what-is|Using a load balancer increases the availability and fault tolerance of your applications.`,`elb-what-is|Elastic Load Balancing scales your load balancer capacity automatically in response to changes in incoming traffic.`,`alb-intro|A load balancer serves as the single point of contact for clients.`]),n(`A load balancer monitors the health of its registered targets and routes traffic only to the healthy ones.`,`elb-what-is`,[`It monitors the health of its registered targets, and routes traffic only to the healthy targets.`]),n(`An Application Load Balancer functions at the application layer, the seventh layer of the OSI model. You add listeners that check for connection requests on a protocol and port, and rules that decide how requests route to target groups. Rules can route on host or path conditions, HTTP headers and methods, query parameters and source IP addresses.`,`alb-intro`,[`An Application Load Balancer functions at the application layer, the seventh layer of the Open Systems Interconnection (OSI) model.`,`A listener checks for connection requests from clients, using the protocol and port that you configure.`,`Each target group routes requests to one or more registered targets, such as EC2 instances, using the protocol and port number that you specify.`,`Support for routing based on fields in the request, such as HTTP header conditions and methods, query parameters, and source IP addresses.`]),n(`Elastic Load Balancing offers four types: Application Load Balancers, Network Load Balancers, Gateway Load Balancers and the previous-generation Classic Load Balancers. The comparison between the current three is in Express Interchange.`,[`alb-intro`,`elb-what-is`],[`alb-intro|Elastic Load Balancing supports the following load balancers: Application Load Balancers, Network Load Balancers, Gateway Load Balancers, and Classic Load Balancers.`,`elb-what-is|Classic Load Balancers are the previous generation of load balancers from Elastic Load Balancing.`],{allow:[`four`,`three`]}),n(`You can use AWS WAF with an Application Load Balancer to allow or block requests based on web ACL rules, and ACM certificates for HTTPS listeners.`,[`elb-what-is`,`alb-intro`],[`elb-what-is|AWS WAF — You can use AWS WAF with your Application Load Balancer to allow or block requests based on the rules in a web access control list (web ACL).`,`alb-intro|AWS Certificate Manager — When you create an HTTPS listener, you can specify certificates provided by ACM.`])],services:[`Elastic Load Balancing (ELB)`],design:[n(`Place the load balancer in front of an Auto Scaling group that spans Availability Zones: the load balancer spreads requests across healthy instances, and the group replaces instances that fail health checks.`,`asg-what-is`,[`Load balancing You can use Elastic Load Balancing load balancing and health checks to ensure an even distribution of application traffic to your healthy instances.`,`Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling automatically monitors the health and availability of your instances using EC2 health checks and replaces terminated or impaired instances to maintain your desired capacity.`])]},{id:`2.1-K15`,concepts:[n(`A read replica is a read-only copy of a DB instance. Updates to the primary are copied to it asynchronously, so you can reduce the load on the primary by routing read queries to the replica and scale out beyond the capacity of a single DB instance for read-heavy workloads.`,`rds-read-replicas`,[`A read replica is a read-only copy of a DB instance.`,`You can reduce the load on your primary DB instance by routing queries from your applications to the read replica.`,`In this way, you can elastically scale out beyond the capacity constraints of a single DB instance for read-heavy database workloads.`,`When you make updates to the primary DB instance, Amazon RDS copies them asynchronously to the read replica.`]),n(`Use cases AWS lists include scaling beyond the compute or I/O capacity of a single instance for read-heavy workloads, and business reporting or data warehousing queries that you want to run against a replica instead of production.`,`rds-read-replicas`,[`Scaling beyond the compute or I/O capacity of a single DB instance for read-heavy database workloads.`,`Business reporting or data warehousing scenarios where you might want business reporting queries to run against a read replica, rather than your production DB instance.`]),n(`A read replica can also be promoted to a standalone instance as a disaster recovery solution if the primary fails. RDS read replicas are not autoscaled, meaning replicas are not added or removed automatically as read demand changes. Aurora is different: Aurora Auto Scaling dynamically adjusts the number of Aurora Replicas (reader DB instances) in a cluster, for both Aurora MySQL and Aurora PostgreSQL.`,[`rds-read-replicas`,`aurora-autoscaling`],[`rds-read-replicas|You can promote a read replica to a standalone instance as a disaster recovery solution if the primary DB instance fails.`,`rds-read-replicas|RDS doesn't support autoscaling of read replicas, which is the automatic add or removing of read replicas as read demand changes.`,`aurora-autoscaling|To meet your connectivity and workload requirements, Aurora Auto Scaling dynamically adjusts the number of Aurora Replicas (reader DB instances) provisioned for an Aurora DB cluster.`,`aurora-autoscaling|Aurora Auto Scaling is available for both Aurora MySQL and Aurora PostgreSQL.`]),n(`Read replicas are for read scaling and do not replace Multi-AZ high availability: a read replica can be configured for an instance that also has a standby configured for high availability. The Multi-AZ vs read replica comparison is in Read Replica Annex.`,`rds-read-replicas`,[`You can configure a read replica for a DB instance that also has a standby replica configured for high availability in a Multi-AZ deployment.`])],services:[`Amazon RDS`],design:[n(`A read replica fits when the symptom is read load: slow reads, a busy primary, or reports slowing production. A replica is a read-only copy, so it takes read queries rather than writes, and because replication is asynchronous the replica can lag behind the primary.`,`rds-read-replicas`,[`A read replica is a read-only copy of a DB instance.`,`Amazon RDS then uses the asynchronous replication method for the DB engine to update the read replica whenever there is a change to the primary DB instance.`])]},{id:`2.1-S2`,concepts:[n(`A target tracking scaling policy automatically scales the capacity of an Auto Scaling group based on a target metric value: you select a metric and a target value for the ideal average utilisation or throughput level.`,`asg-target-tracking`,[`A target tracking scaling policy automatically scales the capacity of your Auto Scaling group based on a target metric value.`,`With target tracking, you select a metric and a target value to represent the ideal average utilization or throughput level for your application.`]),n(`Scheduled scaling sets up scaling based on predictable load changes: you create scheduled actions that increase or decrease desired capacity at specific times.`,`asg-scheduled`,[`With scheduled scaling, you can set up automatic scaling for your application based on predictable load changes.`,`You create scheduled actions that increase or decrease your group's desired capacity at specific times.`]),n(`Predictive scaling analyses historical load data to detect daily or weekly patterns and forecasts future capacity needs, so the group can proactively increase capacity before the load arrives.`,`asg-predictive`,[`Predictive scaling works by analyzing historical load data to detect daily or weekly patterns in traffic flows.`,`It uses this information to forecast future capacity needs so Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling can proactively increase the capacity of your Auto Scaling group to match the anticipated load.`]),n(`Auto Scaling reduces cost by removing capacity when demand is lower, and a single group can launch multiple instance types and purchase options (Spot and On-Demand) to optimise costs.`,`asg-what-is`,[`Using auto scaling allows you to maintain application availability and reduce costs by adding capacity to handle peak loads and removing capacity when demand is lower.`,`Within a single Auto Scaling group, you can launch multiple instance types and purchase options (Spot and On-Demand Instances), allowing you to optimize costs through Spot Instance usage.`])],services:[`Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling`,`Elastic Load Balancing (ELB)`],design:[n(`Pick the scaling method by how predictable the demand is: changing load that you can express as a target metric value (such as average CPU) → target tracking; known times (a weekly peak) → scheduled; recurring daily or weekly cycles that you want the group ready for in advance → predictive. The group's minimum and maximum sizes bound the result in both directions.`,[`asg-target-tracking`,`asg-scheduled`,`asg-predictive`,`asg-what-is`],[`asg-target-tracking|A target tracking scaling policy automatically scales the capacity of your Auto Scaling group based on a target metric value.`,`asg-what-is|You can specify the minimum number of instances in each Auto Scaling group, and Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling ensures that your group never goes below this size.`,`asg-what-is|You can specify the maximum number of instances in each Auto Scaling group, and Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling ensures that your group never goes above this size.`,`asg-scheduled|With scheduled scaling, you can set up automatic scaling for your application based on predictable load changes.`,`asg-predictive|Predictive scaling is well suited for situations where you have: Cyclical traffic, such as high use of resources during regular business hours and low use of resources during evenings and weekends`])]}],cues:[e(`automatically add and remove EC2 instances as demand changes`,`EC2 Auto Scaling group`,n(`Auto Scaling ensures you have the correct number of EC2 instances for the load.`,`asg-what-is`,[`Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling helps you ensure that you have the correct number of Amazon EC2 instances available to handle the load for your application.`])),e(`keep average CPU at about X percent`,`Target tracking scaling policy`,n(`You pick a metric and a target value for ideal average utilisation.`,`asg-target-tracking`,[`With target tracking, you select a metric and a target value to represent the ideal average utilization or throughput level for your application.`])),e(`traffic rises every weekday morning / known event at a known time`,`Scheduled scaling`,n(`Scheduled actions change desired capacity at specific times for predictable load changes.`,`asg-scheduled`,[`You create scheduled actions that increase or decrease your group's desired capacity at specific times.`])),e(`recurring daily/weekly patterns, capacity should be ready before load arrives`,`Predictive scaling`,n(`Predictive scaling forecasts future capacity needs from historical load data.`,`asg-predictive`,[`It uses this information to forecast future capacity needs so Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling can proactively increase the capacity of your Auto Scaling group to match the anticipated load.`])),e(`distribute traffic to healthy instances across AZs`,`Elastic Load Balancing`,n(`A load balancer routes traffic only to healthy targets in one or more Availability Zones.`,`elb-what-is`,[`It monitors the health of its registered targets, and routes traffic only to the healthy targets.`])),e(`route by URL path or host name / HTTP headers`,`Application Load Balancer`,n(`ALB rules can use host and path conditions and request fields.`,`alb-intro`,[`Support for routing based on fields in the request, such as HTTP header conditions and methods, query parameters, and source IP addresses.`])),e(`database reads are slow / reporting queries slow production`,`RDS read replica`,n(`Read replicas offload read-intensive workloads and reporting from the primary.`,`rds-read-replicas`,[`Business reporting or data warehousing scenarios where you might want business reporting queries to run against a read replica, rather than your production DB instance.`])),e(`bigger instance for the same workload without redesign`,`Vertical scaling (change instance type / DB instance size)`,n(`You can resize an instance by changing its instance type.`,`ec2-resize`,[`If this is the case, you can resize your instance by changing its instance type.`]))],examples:[],confuse:[],azure:[{concept:`Azure Virtual Machine Scale Sets`,aws:`Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling`,mapping:n(`The Learn comparison pairs AWS Auto Scaling with Azure Virtual Machine Scale Sets (and App Service autoscaling): deploy and manage identical sets of VMs that scale with demand.`,[`learn-compute`,`asg-what-is`],[`learn-compute|AWS service Azure service Description AWS Auto Scaling Azure Virtual Machine Scale Sets, Azure App Service autoscaling In Azure, you can use virtual machine scale sets to deploy and manage identical sets of VMs.`,`asg-what-is|You create collections of EC2 instances, called Auto Scaling groups.`]),breaks:n(`The Microsoft Learn comparison describes the Azure side as deploying and managing identical sets of VMs and does not name scaling methods. On the AWS side, an Auto Scaling group has distinct scaling methods, including target tracking, scheduled and predictive scaling, each with its own trigger.`,[`asg-predictive`,`asg-target-tracking`,`asg-scheduled`,`learn-compute`],[`asg-predictive|Predictive scaling works by analyzing historical load data to detect daily or weekly patterns in traffic flows.`,`asg-target-tracking|A target tracking scaling policy automatically scales the capacity of your Auto Scaling group based on a target metric value.`,`asg-scheduled|With scheduled scaling, you can set up automatic scaling for your application based on predictable load changes.`,`learn-compute|AWS service Azure service Description AWS Auto Scaling Azure Virtual Machine Scale Sets, Azure App Service autoscaling In Azure, you can use virtual machine scale sets to deploy and manage identical sets of VMs.`],{allow:[`three`]})},{concept:`Azure Load Balancer and Application Gateway`,aws:`Elastic Load Balancing`,mapping:n(`Learn pairs Elastic Load Balancing with two Azure services: Azure Load Balancer provides the layer 4 capabilities of the AWS Network Load Balancer, and Application Gateway provides application-level rule-based routing comparable to the AWS Application Load Balancer.`,[`learn-networking`,`alb-intro`],[`learn-networking|Elastic Load Balancing, Azure Load Balancer, and Azure Application Gateway The Azure equivalents of the Elastic Load Balancing services are: Load Balancer provides the same network layer 4 capabilities as the AWS Network Load Balancer, so you can distribute traffic for multiple VMs at the network level.`,`learn-networking|Application Gateway provides application-level rule-based routing comparable to that of the AWS Application Load Balancer.`,`alb-intro|An Application Load Balancer functions at the application layer, the seventh layer of the Open Systems Interconnection (OSI) model.`],{allow:[`two`]}),breaks:n(`The Learn load balancer comparison lists only Azure Load Balancer and Application Gateway, but Azure has its own Gateway Load Balancer SKU for third-party network virtual appliances, while AWS Gateway Load Balancer deploys virtual appliances such as firewalls. It is covered in Express Interchange.`,[`gwlb-intro`,`learn-azure-gwlb`],[`gwlb-intro|Gateway Load Balancers enable you to deploy, scale, and manage virtual appliances, such as firewalls, intrusion detection and prevention systems, and deep packet inspection systems.`,`learn-azure-gwlb|Gateway Load Balancer is a SKU of the Azure Load Balancer portfolio designed for high performance and high availability scenarios with third-party Network Virtual Appliances (NVAs).`])}]},h={building:`serverless-mill`,overview:[n(`Serverless means you do not manage the servers: AWS handles infrastructure, scaling and patching. This building covers the pieces the exam names: Amazon API Gateway (the front door for APIs), AWS Lambda (functions) and AWS Fargate (containers without servers to manage).`,[`lambda-welcome`,`apigw-welcome`,`fargate-ecs`],[`lambda-welcome|AWS Lambda is a serverless compute service.`,`apigw-welcome|API Gateway acts as a "front door" for applications to access data, business logic, or functionality from your backend services`,`fargate-ecs|With AWS Fargate, you no longer have to provision, configure, or scale clusters of virtual machines to run containers.`])],beyondProject:[n(`You have already built the serverless pattern: a function behind an API. The SAA-level questions are about choosing. Which API type fits (REST or HTTP), when does a function stop fitting (runtime and size limits, long-running work), and when do containers on Fargate fit better than Lambda?`,[`apigw-http-vs-rest`,`lambda-quotas`],[`apigw-http-vs-rest|REST APIs support more features than HTTP APIs, while HTTP APIs are designed with minimal features so that they can be offered at a lower price.`,`lambda-quotas|Code can run for up to 15 minutes in a single invocation`]),n(`If the API in your project is a REST API, you can use features such as AWS WAF integration, API keys, per-client throttling, request validation and private endpoints. If it is an HTTP API, AWS says to choose it when you do not need the features included with REST APIs.`,`apigw-http-vs-rest`,[`Choose REST APIs if you need features such as API keys, per-client throttling, request validation, AWS WAF integration, or private API endpoints.`,`Choose HTTP APIs if you don't need the features included with REST APIs.`])],bullets:[{id:`2.1-K1`,concepts:[n(`Amazon API Gateway is an AWS service for creating, publishing, maintaining, monitoring and securing REST, HTTP and WebSocket APIs at any scale. It handles traffic management, authorization and access control, monitoring and API version management.`,`apigw-welcome`,[`These tasks include traffic management, authorization and access control, monitoring, and API version management.`,`Together with AWS Lambda, API Gateway forms the app-facing part of the AWS serverless infrastructure.`]),n(`A REST API and an HTTP API are both RESTful API products. REST APIs support more features; HTTP APIs have minimal features so that they can be offered at a lower price. Choose REST APIs if you need API keys, per-client throttling, request validation, AWS WAF integration or private API endpoints; choose HTTP APIs if you do not need those features.`,`apigw-http-vs-rest`,[`REST APIs and HTTP APIs are both RESTful API products.`,`REST APIs support more features than HTTP APIs, while HTTP APIs are designed with minimal features so that they can be offered at a lower price.`,`Choose REST APIs if you need features such as API keys, per-client throttling, request validation, AWS WAF integration, or private API endpoints.`,`Choose HTTP APIs if you don't need the features included with REST APIs.`]),n(`WebSocket APIs follow the WebSocket protocol, which enables stateful, full-duplex communication between client and server, while HTTP and REST APIs are stateless.`,`apigw-welcome`,[`Support for stateful (WebSocket) and stateless (HTTP and REST) APIs.`]),n(`In the comparison table, only REST APIs offer edge-optimized and private endpoint types, caching and AWS WAF; resource policies are also REST only. Both types support IAM, Amazon Cognito and Lambda authorizers.`,`apigw-http-vs-rest`,[`Endpoint types REST API HTTP API Edge-optimized Yes No Regional Yes Yes Private Yes No`,`Features REST API HTTP API CORS configuration Yes Yes Test invocations Yes No Caching Yes No`,`Security features REST API HTTP API Mutual TLS authentication Yes Yes Certificates for backend authentication Yes No AWS WAF Yes No`,`Authorization options REST API HTTP API IAM Yes Yes Resource policies Yes No Amazon Cognito Yes Yes 1 Custom authorization with an AWS Lambda function Yes Yes`]),n(`API Gateway throttles requests with a token bucket algorithm: when requests exceed the steady-state rate and burst limits it begins to throttle, and clients may receive 429 Too Many Requests responses. Usage plans let you set throttles per client.`,`apigw-throttling`,[`API Gateway throttles requests to your API using the token bucket algorithm, where a token counts for a request.`,`When request submissions exceed the steady-state request rate and burst limits, API Gateway begins to throttle requests.`,`Clients may receive 429 Too Many Requests error responses at this point.`,`Alternatively, you can enable usage plans to set throttles on client request submissions based on specified requests rates and quotas.`]),n(`Caching a REST API stage reduces the number of calls to your endpoint and improves latency: API Gateway caches endpoint responses for a time-to-live (TTL) period and answers from the cache.`,`apigw-caching`,[`With caching, you can reduce the number of calls made to your endpoint and also improve the latency of requests to your API.`,`API Gateway then responds to the request by looking up the endpoint response from the cache instead of making a request to your endpoint.`])],services:[`Amazon API Gateway`,`AWS Lambda`,`Amazon Cognito`],design:[n(`API Gateway fronts backends such as workloads on EC2, code on Lambda or any web application, and forms the app-facing part of the serverless stack with Lambda. Pair it with Cognito user pools, IAM or a Lambda authorizer for authentication.`,`apigw-welcome`,[`API Gateway acts as a "front door" for applications to access data, business logic, or functionality from your backend services, such as workloads running on Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2), code running on AWS Lambda, any web application, or real-time communication applications.`,`Powerful, flexible authentication mechanisms, such as AWS Identity and Access Management policies, Lambda authorizer functions, and Amazon Cognito user pools.`])]},{id:`2.1-K12`,concepts:[n(`AWS Lambda is a serverless compute service: you run code without provisioning or managing servers, and Lambda manages server maintenance, capacity provisioning, scaling and patching. You write a handler function, connect it to a trigger such as API Gateway, Amazon S3, Amazon SQS or EventBridge, and Lambda runs it.`,`lambda-welcome`,[`With Lambda, you can run code without provisioning or managing servers.`,`Lambda automatically manages the underlying infrastructure – including server maintenance, capacity provisioning, scaling, and patching`,`You write a handler function, connect it to a trigger (API Gateway, Amazon S3, Amazon SQS, EventBridge, and 200+ other AWS services), and Lambda executes it.`]),n(`Each Lambda invocation runs independently with no shared state, scaling horizontally to match demand. That is why a function is the stateless building block of the previous section.`,`lambda-welcome`,[`Each invocation runs independently with no shared state, scaling horizontally to match demand.`]),n(`Lambda has limits that decide whether it fits. A single invocation can run for up to 15 minutes (the function timeout is 900 seconds), and function memory is configurable from 128 MB to 10,240 MB. These are documented quotas that AWS can change.`,`lambda-quotas`,[`Function timeout 900 seconds (15 minutes).`,`Code can run for up to 15 minutes in a single invocation`,`Function memory allocation 128 MB to 10,240 MB, in 1-MB increments.`]),n(`Concurrency is the number of in-flight requests your function is handling at the same time. By default an account has a concurrency limit of 1,000 concurrent executions across all functions in a Region. There are two controls: reserved concurrency and provisioned concurrency.`,`lambda-concurrency`,[`In Lambda, concurrency is the number of in-flight requests that your Event Function is handling at the same time.`,`By default, Lambda provides your account with a total concurrency limit of 1,000 concurrent executions across all Event Functions in an AWS Region.`,`There are two types of concurrency controls available: reserved concurrency and provisioned concurrency.`],{allow:[`two`]}),n(`The two controls do different jobs. Reserved concurrency reserves capacity exclusively for one function and also caps how far that function can scale, which protects critical functions and downstream resources such as database connections. Provisioned concurrency is a number of pre-initialized execution environments, which reduces cold start latency.`,[`lambda-reserved`,`lambda-provisioned`],[`lambda-reserved|Reserved concurrency is useful for ensuring that your most critical functions always have enough concurrency to handle incoming requests.`,`lambda-reserved|Additionally, reserved concurrency can be used for limiting concurrency to prevent overwhelming downstream resources, like database connections.`,`lambda-reserved|Reserved concurrency acts as both a lower and upper bound - it reserves the specified capacity exclusively for your function while also preventing it from scaling beyond that limit.`,`lambda-provisioned|Provisioned concurrency – This is the number of pre-initialized execution environments allocated to your Event Function.`,`lambda-provisioned|Provisioned concurrency is useful for reducing cold start latencies for functions and designed to make functions available with double-digit millisecond response times.`],{allow:[`two`]}),n(`AWS Fargate runs containers without servers to manage: you no longer provision, configure or scale clusters of virtual machines. You package the application in containers, specify CPU and memory, define networking and IAM policies, and launch it. Each Fargate task has its own isolation boundary.`,`fargate-ecs`,[`With AWS Fargate, you no longer have to provision, configure, or scale clusters of virtual machines to run containers.`,`When you run your tasks and services with Fargate, you package your application in containers, specify the CPU and memory requirements, define networking and IAM policies, and launch the application.`,`Each Fargate task has its own isolation boundary and does not share the underlying kernel, CPU resources, memory resources, or elastic network interface with another task.`])],services:[`AWS Lambda`,`AWS Fargate`,`Amazon API Gateway`],design:[n(`Typical serverless pattern: API Gateway receives the request, Lambda runs the logic, and a managed data store such as DynamoDB holds state. API Gateway and Lambda form the app-facing serverless part, and each Lambda invocation runs independently with no shared state, scaling horizontally.`,[`apigw-welcome`,`lambda-welcome`,`dynamodb-what-is`],[`apigw-welcome|Together with AWS Lambda, API Gateway forms the app-facing part of the AWS serverless infrastructure.`,`lambda-welcome|Each invocation runs independently with no shared state, scaling horizontally to match demand.`,`dynamodb-what-is|Amazon DynamoDB is a serverless, fully managed, distributed NoSQL database with single-digit millisecond performance at any scale.`])]},{id:`2.1-S5`,concepts:[n(`Serverless is characterised by no infrastructure to provision or manage, automatic scaling by unit of consumption, a "pay for value" billing model, built-in availability and fault tolerance, and event-driven architecture. Choose it when you want those properties and the workload fits its limits.`,`msa-whitepaper`,[`Serverless is characterized by the following principles: No infrastructure to provision or manage Automatically scaling by unit of consumption "Pay for value" billing model Built-in availability and fault tolerance Event Driven Architecture (EDA)`]),n(`Lambda fits request-response or event-driven work such as APIs, data processing and automation. A workload that needs to run longer than a Lambda invocation allows points to containers (Fargate) or to splitting the work. Being packaged as a container image is not by itself a reason to leave Lambda: Lambda supports container images as a deployment package, with a maximum uncompressed image size of 10 GB.`,[`lambda-welcome`,`lambda-quotas`,`lambda-images`],[`lambda-welcome|Request-response or event-driven workloads (APIs, data processing, automation)`,`lambda-quotas|Code can run for up to 15 minutes in a single invocation`,`lambda-images|Lambda supports two types of deployment packages: container images and .zip file archives.`,`lambda-images|Lambda supports a maximum uncompressed image size of 10 GB, including all layers.`]),n(`Serverless compute requires a stateless design: once designed to be stateless you can use AWS Lambda or AWS Fargate.`,`waf-stateless`,[`Once designed to be stateless, you can then use serverless compute services, such as AWS Lambda or AWS Fargate.`])],services:[`AWS Lambda`,`AWS Fargate`,`Amazon API Gateway`],design:[n(`Lambda is a natural fit for short, event-driven or request-driven tasks where you do not want to manage servers. Fargate fits when you want to run containers (for example a long-running service, or work that exceeds what one function invocation allows) without choosing server types or scaling clusters. Container choices are in Container Dock.`,[`lambda-welcome`,`fargate-ecs`,`lambda-quotas`],[`lambda-welcome|Lambda automatically manages the underlying infrastructure – including server maintenance, capacity provisioning, scaling, and patching`,`fargate-ecs|This removes the need to choose server types, decide when to scale your clusters, or optimize cluster packing.`,`lambda-quotas|Code can run for up to 15 minutes in a single invocation`])]}],cues:[e(`no servers to manage / run code in response to events`,`AWS Lambda`,n(`With Lambda you run code without provisioning or managing servers, in response to events or API calls.`,`lambda-welcome`,[`With Lambda, you can run code without provisioning or managing servers.`])),e(`processing takes longer than 15 minutes`,`Not a single Lambda invocation: consider containers on Fargate, or splitting the work into smaller steps`,n(`A single Lambda invocation can run for up to 15 minutes.`,`lambda-quotas`,[`Code can run for up to 15 minutes in a single invocation`])),e(`containers, but no EC2 instances to manage`,`AWS Fargate`,n(`Fargate removes the need to choose server types, decide when to scale clusters, or optimize cluster packing.`,`fargate-ecs`,[`This removes the need to choose server types, decide when to scale your clusters, or optimize cluster packing.`])),e(`publish/secure/throttle an API in front of Lambda or other backends`,`Amazon API Gateway`,n(`API Gateway is the front door and handles traffic management, authorization and access control, monitoring and version management.`,`apigw-welcome`,[`These tasks include traffic management, authorization and access control, monitoring, and API version management.`])),e(`API needs AWS WAF, API keys, per-client throttling, request validation or a private endpoint`,`API Gateway REST API (not HTTP API)`,n(`Those are the features AWS lists as reasons to choose REST APIs.`,`apigw-http-vs-rest`,[`Choose REST APIs if you need features such as API keys, per-client throttling, request validation, AWS WAF integration, or private API endpoints.`])),e(`lower-cost, minimal-feature API for a Lambda proxy`,`API Gateway HTTP API`,n(`HTTP APIs are designed with minimal features so that they can be offered at a lower price.`,`apigw-http-vs-rest`,[`REST APIs support more features than HTTP APIs, while HTTP APIs are designed with minimal features so that they can be offered at a lower price.`])),e(`cache API responses to cut backend calls and latency`,`API Gateway stage cache (REST API)`,n(`Caching reduces calls to the endpoint and improves latency.`,`apigw-caching`,[`With caching, you can reduce the number of calls made to your endpoint and also improve the latency of requests to your API.`])),e(`two-way, real-time communication such as chat`,`API Gateway WebSocket API`,n(`WebSocket APIs enable stateful, full-duplex communication between client and server.`,`apigw-welcome`,[`Adhere to the WebSocket protocol, which enables stateful, full-duplex communication between client and server.`]))],examples:[],confuse:[],azure:[{concept:`Azure Functions`,aws:`AWS Lambda`,mapping:n(`The Learn compute comparison pairs AWS Lambda with Azure Functions: both provide serverless, event-driven code execution with automatic scaling.`,[`learn-compute`,`lambda-welcome`],[`learn-compute|AWS Lambda Azure Functions Both services provide serverless, event-driven code execution with pay-per-execution pricing and automatic scaling.`,`lambda-welcome|AWS Lambda is a serverless compute service.`]),breaks:n(`The Learn comparison lists what the two share (serverless, event-driven, pay-per-execution, automatic scaling) and does not list limits or controls. On the AWS side, Lambda has a maximum invocation time of 15 minutes and two concurrency controls, reserved and provisioned concurrency, measured in in-flight requests.`,[`lambda-quotas`,`lambda-concurrency`,`learn-compute`],[`lambda-quotas|Code can run for up to 15 minutes in a single invocation`,`lambda-concurrency|There are two types of concurrency controls available: reserved concurrency and provisioned concurrency.`,`learn-compute|AWS Lambda Azure Functions Both services provide serverless, event-driven code execution with pay-per-execution pricing and automatic scaling.`],{allow:[`two`]})},{concept:`Azure API Management`,aws:`Amazon API Gateway`,mapping:n(`The Learn hub pairs Amazon API Gateway with Azure API Management as turnkey services for publishing APIs to internal and external customers.`,[`learn-hub`,`apigw-welcome`],[`learn-hub|Amazon API Gateway Azure API Management These services provide a turnkey solution for publishing APIs to internal and external customers.`,`apigw-welcome|These tasks include traffic management, authorization and access control, monitoring, and API version management.`]),breaks:n(`On AWS you choose between two API products inside the service, REST APIs and HTTP APIs, and the choice decides which features you get (for example AWS WAF integration and caching are REST API only). The Learn comparison lists one Azure service for this and does not describe that split.`,[`apigw-http-vs-rest`,`learn-hub`],[`apigw-http-vs-rest|REST APIs support more features than HTTP APIs, while HTTP APIs are designed with minimal features so that they can be offered at a lower price.`,`learn-hub|Amazon API Gateway Azure API Management These services provide a turnkey solution for publishing APIs to internal and external customers.`],{allow:[`two`]})}]},g={building:`single-point-watch`,overview:[n(`Removing a single point of failure takes redundancy, such as a second copy in another Availability Zone with a load balancer that can reroute traffic, and defensive interaction patterns: limited retries, throttling, failing fast and graceful degradation.`,[`waf-multi-location`,`waf-graceful`,`waf-retries`,`waf-throttle`,`waf-fail-fast`],[`waf-multi-location|Distribute workload data and resources across multiple Availability Zones or, where necessary, across AWS Regions.`,`waf-multi-location|AWS load balancers can reroute traffic to available instances in the event of an Availability Zone impairment.`,`waf-graceful|Application components should continue to perform their core function even if dependencies become unavailable.`,`waf-retries|Limit the maximum number of retries.`,`waf-throttle|Throttle requests to mitigate resource exhaustion due to unexpected increases in demand.`,`waf-fail-fast|When a service is unable to respond successfully to a request, fail fast.`])],bullets:[{id:`2.2-K5`,concepts:[n(`Graceful degradation: application components should continue to perform their core function even if dependencies become unavailable. Turning hard dependencies into soft ones improves the availability of the system as a whole and keeps the most important functions working during failures.`,`waf-graceful`,[`Application components should continue to perform their core function even if dependencies become unavailable.`,`Graceful degradation improves the availability of the system as a whole and maintains the functionality of the most important functions even during failures.`]),n(`Control and limit retries: use exponential backoff to retry at progressively longer intervals, add jitter to randomise the intervals, and limit the maximum number of retries. Backoff and jitter avoid artificial traffic spikes from unintentionally coordinated retries.`,`waf-retries`,[`Use exponential backoff to retry requests at progressively longer intervals between each retry.`,`Limit the maximum number of retries.`,`Introduce jitter between retries to randomize retry intervals.`,`Backoff and jitter help avoid artificial traffic spikes due to unintentionally coordinated retries at common intervals.`]),n(`Throttle requests to mitigate resource exhaustion from unexpected increases in demand: requests below the throttling rate are processed and those over the limit are rejected with a message that they were throttled.`,`waf-throttle`,[`Throttle requests to mitigate resource exhaustion due to unexpected increases in demand.`,`Requests below throttling rates are processed while those over the defined limit are rejected with a return message indicating the request was throttled.`]),n(`Fail fast and limit queues: when a service cannot respond successfully to a request, fail fast. Failing fast releases the resources associated with the request and lets a service that is running out of resources recover, and such systems are easier to debug. A queue can buffer requests when the rate is too high, but do not let long queue backlogs build up, because they can mean processing stale requests that a client has already given up on.`,`waf-fail-fast`,[`When a service is unable to respond successfully to a request, fail fast.`,`This allows resources associated with a request to be released, and permits a service to recover if it’s running out of resources.`,`Systems that fail fast are easier to debug and fix, and often expose issues in coding and configuration before releases are published into production.`,`However, do not allow a buildup of long queue backlogs that can result in processing stale requests that a client has already given up on.`]),n(`Loosely coupled dependencies (queues, streams and workflows; see Message Quay) and idempotent mutating operations are further practices in the Well-Architected reliability guidance, so that retries and duplicates are safe.`,[`waf-loose-coupling`,`waf-idempotent`],[`waf-loose-coupling|REL04-BP02 Implement loosely coupled dependencies`,`waf-idempotent|REL04-BP04 Make mutating operations idempotent`,`waf-idempotent|Using idempotency tokens in APIs, services can receive a mutating request one or more times without the need to create duplicate records or side effects.`])],services:[`Amazon SQS`,`Amazon API Gateway`,`AWS Step Functions`],design:[n(`Match the symptom to the pattern: retry storms → backoff with jitter and a retry limit; a flood of requests → throttling (for example API Gateway throttling); a dependency that is unavailable or slow while the page can still do its core job → graceful degradation; a service that fails when the request rate is too high → a queue to buffer requests, with backlogs kept short.`,[`waf-retries`,`waf-throttle`,`waf-graceful`,`waf-fail-fast`],[`waf-retries|Backoff and jitter help avoid artificial traffic spikes due to unintentionally coordinated retries at common intervals.`,`waf-throttle|Throttle requests to mitigate resource exhaustion due to unexpected increases in demand.`,`waf-graceful|Application components should continue to perform their core function even if dependencies become unavailable.`,`waf-fail-fast|When a service is able to respond successfully under normal conditions but fails when the rate of requests is too high, use a queue to buffer requests.`])]},{id:`2.2-K8`,concepts:[n(`A load balancer removes the single instance as a single point of failure: it distributes traffic across multiple targets in one or more Availability Zones, monitors target health, and sends requests only to healthy targets. Using one increases availability and fault tolerance. (Types and sizing are in Scaling Floodgate and Express Interchange.)`,`elb-what-is`,[`Elastic Load Balancing automatically distributes your incoming traffic across multiple targets, such as EC2 instances, containers, and IP addresses, in one or more Availability Zones.`,`It monitors the health of its registered targets, and routes traffic only to the healthy targets.`,`Using a load balancer increases the availability and fault tolerance of your applications.`]),n(`Pair the load balancer with an Auto Scaling group: EC2 Auto Scaling automatically monitors the health and availability of instances and replaces terminated or impaired instances to maintain the desired capacity, balancing instances evenly across the Availability Zones you specify.`,`asg-what-is`,[`Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling automatically monitors the health and availability of your instances using EC2 health checks and replaces terminated or impaired instances to maintain your desired capacity.`,`You can specify multiple Availability Zones for your Auto Scaling group, and Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling balances your instances evenly across the Availability Zones as the group scales.`]),n(`By default an Auto Scaling group replaces only instances that are unhealthy or unreachable according to EC2 health checks. If you turn on Elastic Load Balancing health checks, the group can also replace a running instance that an attached load balancer reports as unhealthy.`,`asg-attach-lb`,[`By default, Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling only replaces instances that are unhealthy or unreachable based on Amazon EC2 health checks.`,`If you turn on Elastic Load Balancing health checks, Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling can replace a running instance if any of the Elastic Load Balancing load balancers you attach to the Auto Scaling group report it as unhealthy.`])],services:[`Elastic Load Balancing (ELB)`,`Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling`],design:[n(`A load balancer and Auto Scaling group across Availability Zones spread the application tier over several instances and zones, which increases availability and fault tolerance. The load balancer itself scales automatically in response to changes in incoming traffic.`,[`elb-what-is`,`asg-what-is`],[`elb-what-is|Using a load balancer increases the availability and fault tolerance of your applications.`,`asg-what-is|You can specify multiple Availability Zones for your Auto Scaling group, and Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling balances your instances evenly across the Availability Zones as the group scales.`,`elb-what-is|Elastic Load Balancing scales your load balancer capacity automatically in response to changes in incoming traffic.`])]},{id:`2.2-K9`,concepts:[n(`Amazon RDS Proxy lets applications pool and share database connections to improve their ability to scale. It establishes a database connection pool and reuses connections, so the proxy manages a smaller number of long-lived connections to the DB instance while applications open many client connections.`,[`rds-proxy`,`rds-proxy-planning`],[`rds-proxy|By using Amazon RDS Proxy, you can allow your applications to pool and share database connections to improve their ability to scale.`,`rds-proxy|RDS Proxy establishes a database connection pool and reuses connections in this pool.`,`rds-proxy-planning|The proxy enables applications to open many client connections, while the proxy manages a smaller number of long-lived connections to the DB instance .`]),n(`RDS Proxy makes applications more resilient to database failures by automatically connecting to a standby DB instance while preserving application connections. It can also enforce IAM authentication for clients, and connect to databases using IAM database authentication or credentials stored in AWS Secrets Manager.`,`rds-proxy`,[`RDS Proxy makes applications more resilient to database failures by automatically connecting to a standby DB instance while preserving application connections.`,`By using RDS Proxy, you can enforce AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) authentication for clients connecting to the proxy, and the proxy can connect to databases using either IAM database authentication or credentials stored in AWS Secrets Manager.`]),n(`Good candidates for a proxy: any DB instance that encounters "too many connections" errors, and AWS Lambda functions, which make frequent short database connections that benefit from connection pooling; Lambda can use IAM authentication it already has instead of database credentials in code.`,`rds-proxy-planning`,[`Any DB instance that encounters "too many connections" errors is a good candidate for associating with a proxy.`,`These functions make frequent short database connections that benefit from connection pooling offered by RDS Proxy.`,`You can take advantage of any IAM authentication you already have for Lambda functions, instead of managing database credentials in your Lambda application code.`])],services:[`Amazon RDS`,`AWS Lambda`,`AWS Secrets Manager`],design:[n(`Cue pattern: unpredictable surges in database traffic that can oversubscribe connections, or failovers breaking application connections → RDS Proxy, which connects to a standby DB instance while preserving application connections.`,[`rds-proxy`],[`rds-proxy|Using RDS Proxy, you can handle unpredictable surges in database traffic.`,`rds-proxy|Otherwise, these surges might cause issues due to oversubscribing connections or new connections being created at a fast rate.`,`rds-proxy|RDS Proxy makes applications more resilient to database failures by automatically connecting to a standby DB instance while preserving application connections.`])]},{id:`2.2-S4`,concepts:[n(`Work through the tiers and ask what happens if this one thing fails: compute (instances behind a load balancer in an Auto Scaling group across AZs), database (a Multi-AZ deployment, where a standby provides failover support), connections (RDS Proxy), DNS and Regions (Route 53 health checks), and dependencies (retries, throttling, graceful degradation).`,[`elb-what-is`,`rds-multi-az`,`route53-failover`],[`elb-what-is|Using a load balancer increases the availability and fault tolerance of your applications.`,`rds-multi-az|A Multi-AZ DB instance deployment has one standby DB instance that provides failover support, but doesn't serve read traffic.`,`route53-failover|Amazon Route 53 health checks monitor the health and performance of your web applications, web servers, and other resources.`]),n(`RDS Multi-AZ comes in two shapes: a Multi-AZ DB instance deployment with one standby that provides failover support but does not serve read traffic, and a Multi-AZ DB cluster deployment whose standbys provide failover support and can also serve read traffic. (The full comparison with read replicas is in Read Replica Annex.)`,`rds-multi-az`,[`A Multi-AZ DB instance deployment has one standby DB instance that provides failover support, but doesn't serve read traffic.`,`A Multi-AZ DB cluster deployment has standby DB instances that provide failover support and can also serve read traffic.`],{allow:[`two`]})],services:[`Elastic Load Balancing (ELB)`,`Amazon RDS`,`Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling`],design:[n(`A common fix: replace the single EC2 instance with an Auto Scaling group behind a load balancer across Availability Zones, and replace the single-AZ database with a Multi-AZ deployment. Route 53 failover is a further layer: with active-passive failover, a secondary resource stands by in case all the primary resources become unavailable.`,[`asg-what-is`,`rds-multi-az`,`route53-failover-types`],[`asg-what-is|Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling automatically monitors the health and availability of your instances using EC2 health checks and replaces terminated or impaired instances to maintain your desired capacity.`,`rds-multi-az|A Multi-AZ DB instance deployment has one standby DB instance that provides failover support, but doesn't serve read traffic.`,`route53-failover-types|Use an active-passive failover configuration when you want a primary resource or group of resources to be available the majority of the time and you want a secondary resource or group of resources to be on standby in case all the primary resources become unavailable.`])]}],cues:[e(`too many database connections from Lambda or a large fleet`,`Amazon RDS Proxy`,n(`RDS Proxy pools and shares database connections, and Lambda functions are good candidates.`,`rds-proxy-planning`,[`AWS Lambda functions can also be good candidates for using a proxy.`])),e(`applications should keep their connections during database failover`,`Amazon RDS Proxy`,n(`RDS Proxy automatically connects to a standby DB instance while preserving application connections.`,`rds-proxy`,[`RDS Proxy makes applications more resilient to database failures by automatically connecting to a standby DB instance while preserving application connections.`])),e(`database must survive an Availability Zone failure`,`RDS Multi-AZ deployment`,n(`A Multi-AZ deployment includes standby instances that provide failover support.`,`rds-multi-az`,[`A Multi-AZ DB instance deployment has one standby DB instance that provides failover support, but doesn't serve read traffic.`])),e(`retries from many clients overload a recovering service`,`Exponential backoff with jitter and a maximum retry count`,n(`Backoff and jitter avoid artificial traffic spikes from coordinated retries.`,`waf-retries`,[`Backoff and jitter help avoid artificial traffic spikes due to unintentionally coordinated retries at common intervals.`])),e(`a non-critical dependency fails but the page should still load`,`Graceful degradation`,n(`Components should continue their core function even if dependencies become unavailable.`,`waf-graceful`,[`Application components should continue to perform their core function even if dependencies become unavailable.`])),e(`instances the load balancer marks unhealthy should be replaced`,`Auto Scaling group with Elastic Load Balancing health checks turned on`,n(`By default the group replaces instances based on EC2 health checks only; turning on Elastic Load Balancing health checks lets it replace instances the load balancer reports as unhealthy.`,`asg-attach-lb`,[`If you turn on Elastic Load Balancing health checks, Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling can replace a running instance if any of the Elastic Load Balancing load balancers you attach to the Auto Scaling group report it as unhealthy.`])),e(`replace failed instances automatically`,`EC2 Auto Scaling health checks`,n(`Auto Scaling replaces terminated or impaired instances to maintain desired capacity.`,`asg-what-is`,[`Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling automatically monitors the health and availability of your instances using EC2 health checks and replaces terminated or impaired instances to maintain your desired capacity.`]))],examples:[],confuse:[],azure:[{concept:`Azure Application Gateway`,aws:`Application Load Balancer`,mapping:n(`Learn pairs Azure Application Gateway with the AWS Application Load Balancer for application-level rule-based routing.`,[`learn-networking`,`alb-intro`],[`learn-networking|Application Gateway provides application-level rule-based routing comparable to that of the AWS Application Load Balancer.`,`alb-intro|An Application Load Balancer functions at the application layer, the seventh layer of the Open Systems Interconnection (OSI) model.`]),breaks:n(`The Microsoft Learn comparison compares the routing function only. The availability behaviour in this building comes from other AWS features: the load balancer sends traffic only to healthy targets, and an Auto Scaling group replaces terminated or impaired instances.`,[`elb-what-is`,`asg-what-is`,`learn-networking`],[`elb-what-is|It monitors the health of its registered targets, and routes traffic only to the healthy targets.`,`asg-what-is|Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling automatically monitors the health and availability of your instances using EC2 health checks and replaces terminated or impaired instances to maintain your desired capacity.`,`learn-networking|Application Gateway provides application-level rule-based routing comparable to that of the AWS Application Load Balancer.`])}]},_={building:`tide-gauges`,overview:[n(`You cannot keep a workload available if you cannot see it. This building covers visibility and limits: service quotas (the ceilings AWS puts on your account), tracing (where in a request the time or the errors are), and metrics (the numbers that tell you whether the availability the business asked for is being delivered).`,[`servicequotas`,`xray`,`waf-availability`],[`servicequotas|Quotas, also referred to as limits in AWS services, are the maximum values for the resources, actions, and items in your AWS account.`,`xray|AWS X-Ray is a service that collects data about requests that your application serves, and provides tools that you can use to view, filter, and gain insights into that data to identify issues and opportunities for optimization.`,`waf-availability|Availability is the percentage of time that a workload is available for use.`])],bullets:[{id:`2.2-K10`,concepts:[n(`Service quotas, also called limits, are the maximum values for the resources, actions and items in your AWS account. Each AWS service defines its quotas and default values. Service Quotas lets you look up quotas in one place and request increases; Support might approve, deny or partially approve a request.`,`servicequotas`,[`Quotas, also referred to as limits in AWS services, are the maximum values for the resources, actions, and items in your AWS account.`,`Each AWS service defines its quotas and establishes default values for those quotas.`,`Service Quotas enables you to look up your service quotas and to request increases.`,`Support might approve, deny, or partially approve your requests.`]),n(`You can create CloudWatch alarms to notify you when you are close to a quota threshold, and an alarm can help alert you if you need to request a quota increase.`,`servicequotas-cw`,[`You can create Amazon CloudWatch alarms to notify you when you're close to a quota value threshold.`,`Setting an alarm can help alert you if you need to request a quota increase.`]),n(`Standby and multi-Region environments need their own quotas. If you use multiple accounts or Regions, request the appropriate quotas in all environments where production workloads run, so failover to another Region or account is not blocked by quota exhaustion. Quota drift, where a limit is changed in one Region and not in all, is important to track.`,`waf-quotas-regions`,[`If you are using multiple accounts or Regions, request the appropriate quotas in all environments in which your production workloads run.`,`Desired outcome: Services and applications should not be affected by service quota exhaustion for configurations that span accounts or Regions or that have resilience designs using zone, Region, or account failover.`,`Service quota drift, the condition where service quota limits for a specific named quota is changed in one Region and not all Regions, is very important to track and assess.`]),n(`Failover tests are often not scaled to peak capacity, so exercises can fail to find quota differences between Regions. Plan for the passive Region to be able to take the full load.`,`waf-quotas-regions`,[`The testing plans of passive Region failovers are rarely scaled to peak active capacity, meaning that game day or table top exercises can fail to find differences in service quotas between Regions and also then maintain the correct limits.`]),n(`Throttling is a service protecting itself by rejecting requests over a rate. API Gateway throttles with a token bucket and answers 429 Too Many Requests when the rate and burst limits are exceeded; Lambda has an account concurrency limit across functions in a Region with reserved and provisioned concurrency controls. Both are covered in Serverless Mill.`,[`apigw-throttling`,`lambda-concurrency`],[`apigw-throttling|Clients may receive 429 Too Many Requests error responses at this point.`,`lambda-concurrency|By default, Lambda provides your account with a total concurrency limit of 1,000 concurrent executions across all Event Functions in an AWS Region.`]),n(`AWS Trusted Advisor inspects your AWS environment and recommends where to save money, improve availability and performance, or close security gaps. With the Basic Support plan you can use the console to access all checks in the Service Limits category and selected checks in the Security and Fault tolerance categories.`,`trusted-advisor`,[`Trusted Advisor inspects your AWS environment, and then makes recommendations when opportunities exist to save money, improve system availability and performance, or help close security gaps.`,`If you have a Basic Support plan, you can use the Trusted Advisor console to access all checks in the Service Limits category and selected checks in the Security and Fault tolerance categories.`])],services:[`AWS Trusted Advisor`,`Amazon API Gateway`,`Amazon CloudWatch`],design:[n(`A launch, load test or failover that fails with a limit error although nothing is mis-configured points to a service quota: check and raise it in that Region (and in the standby Region). A "Too Many Requests" throttling error from an API points to the request rate: slow retries down with exponential backoff (see Single Point Watch).`,[`servicequotas`,`waf-quotas-regions`,`apigw-throttling`,`waf-retries`],[`servicequotas|If your business needs aren't met by the default limit of service resources or operations that apply to an AWS account, resource, or an AWS Region, you might need to increase your service quota values.`,`waf-quotas-regions|Services and applications should not be affected by service quota exhaustion for configurations that span accounts or Regions or that have resilience designs using zone, Region, or account failover.`,`apigw-throttling|Clients may receive 429 Too Many Requests error responses at this point.`,`waf-retries|Use exponential backoff to retry requests at progressively longer intervals between each retry.`])]},{id:`2.2-K12`,concepts:[n(`AWS X-Ray collects data about the requests your application serves and gives you tools to view, filter and gain insights into that data. For any traced request you can see detail about the request and response, and about calls your application makes to downstream AWS resources, microservices, databases and web APIs.`,`xray`,[`AWS X-Ray is a service that collects data about requests that your application serves, and provides tools that you can use to view, filter, and gain insights into that data to identify issues and opportunities for optimization.`,`For any traced request to your application, you can see detailed information not only about the request and response, but also about calls that your application makes to downstream AWS resources, microservices, databases, and web APIs.`]),n(`X-Ray builds a trace map from the data, which you use to identify bottlenecks, latency spikes and other issues. Instrumenting your application means sending trace data for incoming and outbound requests; integrated AWS services can add tracing headers and send trace data themselves.`,`xray`,[`Use the trace map to identify bottlenecks, latency spikes, and other issues to solve to improve the performance of your applications.`,`Instrumenting your application involves sending trace data for incoming and outbound requests and other events within your application, along with metadata about each request.`,`AWS services that are integrated with X-Ray can add tracing headers to incoming requests, send trace data to X-Ray, or run the X-Ray daemon.`]),n(`Monitor all components of the workload, including the front-end, business logic and storage tiers, with Amazon CloudWatch or third-party tools: define key metrics, and set thresholds for invoking alarms.`,`waf-monitor-components`,[`All components of your workload should be monitored, including the front-end, business logic, and storage tiers.`,`Define key metrics, describe how to extract them from logs (if necessary), and set thresholds for invoking corresponding alarm events.`]),n(`CloudWatch, CloudTrail and AWS Config answer different questions. CloudWatch collects metrics and sets alarms on them against thresholds (how is it performing). CloudTrail records actions taken by a user, role or AWS service as events, including actions in the console, CLI and SDKs (who did what). AWS Config provides a detailed view of how AWS resources are configured and how that changed over time (what changed, and does the configuration comply).`,[`cw-what-is`,`cloudtrail-what-is`,`config-what-is`],[`cw-what-is|Operational visibility with metrics, alarms, and dashboards Metrics collect and track key performance data at user-defined intervals.`,`cw-what-is|You can set up alarms that continuously monitor CloudWatch metrics against user-defined thresholds.`,`cloudtrail-what-is|Actions taken by a user, role, or an AWS service are recorded as events in CloudTrail.`,`cloudtrail-what-is|Events include actions taken in the AWS Management Console, AWS Command Line Interface, and AWS SDKs and APIs.`,`config-what-is|AWS Config provides a detailed view of the configuration of AWS resources in your AWS account.`,`config-what-is|This includes how the resources are related to one another and how they were configured in the past so that you can see how the configurations and relationships change over time.`,`config-what-is|You can use AWS Config rules to evaluate the configuration settings of your AWS resources.`])],services:[`AWS X-Ray`,`Amazon CloudWatch`,`AWS CloudTrail`,`AWS Config`],design:[n(`Use CloudWatch to know that something is wrong (metrics, alarms, logs), and X-Ray to find where in a distributed request the latency or errors arise.`,[`cw-what-is`,`xray`],[`cw-what-is|Operational visibility with metrics, alarms, and dashboards`,`xray|Use the trace map to identify bottlenecks, latency spikes, and other issues to solve to improve the performance of your applications.`])]},{id:`2.2-S3`,concepts:[n(`Availability is the percentage of time that a workload is available for use, calculated over a period such as a month or year; "five nines" is shorthand for 99.999% available.`,`waf-availability`,[`Availability is the percentage of time that a workload is available for use.`,`This percentage is calculated over a period of time, such as a month, year, or trailing three years.`,`Common short-hand refers only to the “number of nines”; for example, “five nines” translates to being 99.999% available`],{allow:[`five`,`three`]}),n(`For load balanced applications, the Application Load Balancer publishes HealthyHostCount and UnHealthyHostCount, the numbers of targets considered healthy and unhealthy. AWS recommends monitoring for a non-zero UnHealthyHostCount and alarming on a non-zero value for more than one data point.`,`alb-metrics`,[`Dimensions TargetGroup, LoadBalancer TargetGroup, AvailabilityZone, LoadBalancer HealthyHostCount The number of targets that are considered healthy.`,`We recommend you monitor for non-zero UnHealthyHostCount in the Minimum statistic, and alarm on non-zero value for more than one data point.`]),n(`Alarms turn metrics into actions: define key metrics and set thresholds for invoking corresponding alarm events.`,[`waf-monitor-components`,`cw-alarms`],[`waf-monitor-components|Define key metrics, describe how to extract them from logs (if necessary), and set thresholds for invoking corresponding alarm events.`,`cw-alarms|The alarm performs one or more actions based on the value of the metric or expression relative to a threshold over a number of time periods.`])],services:[`Amazon CloudWatch`,`Elastic Load Balancing (ELB)`],design:[n(`A common monitoring setup: alarm on the load balancer's target health (UnHealthyHostCount) and on quota usage, and use X-Ray when the need is to locate a slow dependency in a request.`,[`alb-metrics`,`servicequotas-cw`,`xray`],[`alb-metrics|We recommend you monitor for non-zero UnHealthyHostCount in the Minimum statistic, and alarm on non-zero value for more than one data point.`,`servicequotas-cw|You can create Amazon CloudWatch alarms to notify you when you're close to a quota value threshold.`,`xray|Use the trace map to identify bottlenecks, latency spikes, and other issues to solve to improve the performance of your applications.`])]}],cues:[e(`cannot launch more instances / limit exceeded error`,`Service Quotas: check the quota and request an increase`,n(`Service Quotas lets you look up quotas and request increases.`,`servicequotas`,[`Service Quotas enables you to look up your service quotas and to request increases.`])),e(`standby Region must be able to take the full load`,`Request matching quotas in the standby Region`,n(`Request the appropriate quotas in all environments in which production workloads run.`,`waf-quotas-regions`,[`If you are using multiple accounts or Regions, request the appropriate quotas in all environments in which your production workloads run.`])),e(`find which downstream call is slow in a distributed request`,`AWS X-Ray`,n(`X-Ray traces calls to downstream AWS resources, microservices, databases and web APIs.`,`xray`,[`For any traced request to your application, you can see detailed information not only about the request and response, but also about calls that your application makes to downstream AWS resources, microservices, databases, and web APIs.`])),e(`who made this API call / who changed this setting`,`AWS CloudTrail`,n(`CloudTrail records actions taken by a user, role or AWS service as events.`,`cloudtrail-what-is`,[`Actions taken by a user, role, or an AWS service are recorded as events in CloudTrail.`])),e(`track how a resource configuration changed over time / evaluate against rules`,`AWS Config`,n(`AWS Config shows how resources were configured in the past and evaluates them with rules.`,`config-what-is`,[`This includes how the resources are related to one another and how they were configured in the past so that you can see how the configurations and relationships change over time.`,`You can use AWS Config rules to evaluate the configuration settings of your AWS resources.`])),e(`alert when a metric crosses a threshold`,`Amazon CloudWatch alarm`,n(`Define key metrics and set thresholds for invoking alarm events.`,`waf-monitor-components`,[`Define key metrics, describe how to extract them from logs (if necessary), and set thresholds for invoking corresponding alarm events.`])),e(`recommendations across cost, performance, fault tolerance, security and service limits`,`AWS Trusted Advisor`,n(`Trusted Advisor makes recommendations to save money, improve availability and performance, or close security gaps.`,`trusted-advisor`,[`Trusted Advisor inspects your AWS environment, and then makes recommendations when opportunities exist to save money, improve system availability and performance, or help close security gaps.`])),e(`number of unhealthy targets behind a load balancer`,`ALB UnHealthyHostCount metric`,n(`UnHealthyHostCount is the number of targets considered unhealthy.`,`alb-metrics`,[`Dimensions LoadBalancer AvailabilityZone, LoadBalancer TargetGroup, LoadBalancer TargetGroup, AvailabilityZone, LoadBalancer UnHealthyHostCount The number of targets that are considered unhealthy.`]))],examples:[],confuse:[],azure:[{concept:`Azure Monitor (Application Insights)`,aws:`Amazon CloudWatch and AWS X-Ray`,mapping:n(`Learn pairs CloudWatch and X-Ray with Azure Monitor, and says to use Application Insights, a feature of Azure Monitor, to instrument your code for in-depth application performance monitoring. That is the role X-Ray plays on AWS.`,[`learn-hub`,`xray`],[`learn-hub|Use Application Insights, a feature of Azure Monitor, to instrument your code for more in-depth application performance monitoring.`,`learn-hub|In AWS, you typically use both X-Ray and CloudWatch.`,`xray|Instrumenting your application involves sending trace data for incoming and outbound requests and other events within your application, along with metadata about each request.`]),breaks:n(`Learn pairs both AWS services with the one Azure service, Azure Monitor. On AWS the jobs sit in two services: CloudWatch collects metrics and sets alarms on them, and X-Ray traces requests.`,[`xray`,`cw-what-is`,`learn-hub`],[`xray|Use the trace map to identify bottlenecks, latency spikes, and other issues to solve to improve the performance of your applications.`,`cw-what-is|You can set up alarms that continuously monitor CloudWatch metrics against user-defined thresholds.`,`learn-hub|DevOps and application monitoring AWS service Azure service Description Amazon CloudWatch and AWS X-Ray Azure Monitor`],{allow:[`two`]})},{concept:`Azure Advisor`,aws:`AWS Trusted Advisor`,mapping:n(`Learn pairs AWS Trusted Advisor with Azure Advisor: analysis of resource configuration to help you use best practices. Learn lists the Trusted Advisor check categories as cost optimization, security, performance, fault tolerance and service limits.`,[`learn-compute`,`trusted-advisor`],[`learn-compute|AWS Trusted Advisor Azure Advisor AWS Trusted Advisor provides checks across cost optimization, security, performance, fault tolerance, and service limits.`,`trusted-advisor|Trusted Advisor inspects your AWS environment, and then makes recommendations when opportunities exist to save money, improve system availability and performance, or help close security gaps.`]),breaks:n(`Learn describes Azure Advisor as covering the same pillars in a single integrated experience. On the AWS side, the Trusted Advisor checks you can see depend on your AWS Support plan: with Basic Support you get the Service Limits category and selected Security and Fault tolerance checks.`,[`trusted-advisor`,`learn-compute`],[`trusted-advisor|If you have a Basic Support plan, you can use the Trusted Advisor console to access all checks in the Service Limits category and selected checks in the Security and Fault tolerance categories.`,`learn-compute|Azure Advisor covers the same pillars in a single integrated experience.`])}]},v=Object.values(Object.assign({"../../content/notes/harbor/blueprint-hall.ts":r,"../../content/notes/harbor/cache-edge-breakwater.ts":i,"../../content/notes/harbor/cargo-hall.ts":a,"../../content/notes/harbor/container-dock.ts":o,"../../content/notes/harbor/disaster-bunker.ts":s,"../../content/notes/harbor/durable-dock.ts":c,"../../content/notes/harbor/island-charts.ts":l,"../../content/notes/harbor/legacy-wharf.ts":u,"../../content/notes/harbor/message-quay.ts":d,"../../content/notes/harbor/purpose-built-workshop.ts":f,"../../content/notes/harbor/rebuild-yard.ts":p,"../../content/notes/harbor/scaling-floodgate.ts":m,"../../content/notes/harbor/serverless-mill.ts":h,"../../content/notes/harbor/single-point-watch.ts":g,"../../content/notes/harbor/tide-gauges.ts":_}));export{v as notes};